{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-06-17T03:20:57.771Z","sessionId":"af861ed9-9e1d-41a7-8400-b53c2995aed8","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>wxriffakw</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01BgNDP6PwM4xSMxcXzngu18</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-Documents-Coding-entire-brain/4e09703c-666f-441f-9b82-52efaa7da599/tasks/wxriffakw.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Dynamic workflow \"Adversarial audit of the new `entire brain dash` TUI + cli wiring; returns verified bug list\" completed</summary>\n<result>{\"confirmed\":[{\"title\":\"switchTab panics (index out of range) when leaving a wider tab for a narrower one: SetColumns runs against the previous tab's stale, wider rows\",\"file\":\"internal/tui/tui.go\",\"line\":\"163\",\"severity\":\"crash\",\"detail\":\"In switchTab the table's columns are swapped to the NEW tab BEFORE the rows are replaced:\\n\\n    m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner)) // tui.go:163 — new (narrower) columns\\n    m.table.SetCursor(0)                                // tui.go:164\\n    m.rebuildVisible()                                  // tui.go:165 — only here are new rows set\\n\\nbubbles/table.SetColumns immediately calls UpdateViewport(), which renders the rows still held in the table (the OLD tab's rows) against the NEW columns. renderRow does `for i, value := range m.rows[r] { ... m.cols[i] ... }` (vendored table.go:430-431), so when an old row has more cells than the new tab has columns, m.cols[i] indexes out of range and the whole program panics.\\n\\nColumn counts per tab: Home=3, Facts=3, Sessions=4, History=2, Semantic=3. Any switch from a tab whose visible rows are wider than the destination's column count crashes, e.g. Sessions(4-cell rows) -&gt; History(2 cols), Semantic(3) -&gt; History(2), Facts(3) -&gt; History(2), Sessions(4) -&gt; Semantic(3). This is the primary navigation gesture (Tab / Shift+Tab to cycle sections), so any user with a populated brain hits it almost immediately. switchTab is reached from both Section (Tab, delta +1, tui.go:123) and PrevTab (Shift+Tab, delta -1, tui.go:126).\\n\\nReproduced deterministically: with the sample snapshot, NewModel + WindowSizeMsg, then Tab,Tab,Tab (Home-&gt;Facts-&gt;Sessions-&gt;History) panics: `runtime error: index out of range [2] with length 2` at table.go:431 (renderRow) &lt;- table.go:321 (SetColumns) &lt;- tui.go:163 (switchTab). Also reproduced via Shift+Tab Home-&gt;Semantic-&gt;History. The existing tests miss it because TestViewSmoke/TestClickSelectsCorrectRow only ever switch Home-&gt;Facts, where both tabs have 3 columns (equal count, no overflow).\\n\\nNote resize() (tui.go:244) calls the same SetColumns but is safe there because the tab is unchanged, so the new columns have the same count as the in-table rows; only switchTab changes the column count out from under the existing rows.\",\"suggested_fix\":\"Clear the rows before narrowing the columns so SetColumns never renders stale wide rows. Simplest: insert `m.table.SetRows(nil)` immediately before the SetColumns call in switchTab (verified to fix the panic and keep all existing tests green):\\n\\n    m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\")\\n    m.table.SetRows(nil)\\n    m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner))\\n    m.table.SetCursor(0)\\n    m.rebuildVisible()\\n\\nrebuildVisible then repopulates the rows that match the new columns. Add a regression test that cycles through every tab forward and backward (Tab x6, Shift+Tab x6) and calls View(), so a future reorder is caught.\",\"dimension\":\"panics\",\"verdict\":{\"real\":true,\"reasoning\":\"CONFIRMED as a real crash. I reproduced the panic deterministically and verified the suggested fix.\\n\\nRoot cause (verified against source):\\n- internal/tui/tui.go:157-166 switchTab sets the NEW tab's columns BEFORE replacing the rows: line 162 m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\"), line 163 m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner)), line 164 m.table.SetCursor(0), and only at line 165 m.rebuildVisible() (which calls m.table.SetRows). So between line 163 and 165 the table holds the OLD tab's rows but the NEW tab's columns.\\n- Vendored bubbles v1.0.0 confirms the mechanism. table.go:319-322 SetColumns sets m.cols=c then immediately calls UpdateViewport(). UpdateViewport (table.go:278-280) renders the existing m.rows. renderRow (table.go:428-437) does `for i, value := range m.rows[r] { ... m.cols[i].Width ... }` — it indexes m.cols by the OLD row's cell count. When an old row has more cells than the new column count, m.cols[i] panics with index out of range.\\n- Per-tab cell/column counts (internal/tui/table.go columnsFor + rowsFor): Home=3, Facts=3, Sessions=4, History=2, Semantic=3. Any switch into a narrower tab (e.g. Sessions(4)-&gt;History(2), Facts(3)-&gt;History(2), Semantic(3)-&gt;History(2), Sessions(4)-&gt;Semantic(3)) overflows. switchTab is the primary nav gesture, reached from Section/Tab (tui.go:123, delta +1) and PrevTab/Shift+Tab (tui.go:126, delta -1), so a user with a populated brain hits it almost immediately.\\n\\nReproduction: I added a temporary test that does NewModel(sampleSnapshot) + WindowSizeMsg{120,40} then three Tab presses (Home-&gt;Facts-&gt;Sessions-&gt;History) and called View(). It panicked exactly as the finding predicts:\\n  panic: runtime error: index out of range [2] with length 2\\n  table.go:431 renderRow &lt;- table.go:279 UpdateViewport &lt;- table.go:321 SetColumns &lt;- tui.go:163 switchTab &lt;- tui.go:123 Update\\nThe [2]/length 2 matches Sessions' 4-cell rows being rendered against History's 2 columns.\\n\\nWhy existing tests miss it: TestViewSmoke (tui_test.go:132) and TestClickSelectsCorrectRow (tui_test.go:144) only switch Home-&gt;Facts (both 3 columns, equal count, no overflow). No existing test cycles into History (2 cols) or Semantic from a wider tab. The sampleSnapshot IS populated (Sessions:2, Facts:3, History:1, Semantic:1), so rows exist at switch time.\\n\\nWhy resize() (tui.go:244) is safe: it calls the same SetColumns but the tab is unchanged, so the new column count equals the in-table rows' cell count; only switchTab changes the column count out from under the existing rows. Confirmed.\\n\\nFix verification: I applied the suggested fix (insert `m.table.SetRows(nil)` immediately before SetColumns at tui.go:163). The repro test then passed and the full existing suite (go test ./internal/tui/) stayed green. The empty-rows path is safe because UpdateViewport's loop renders no rows when m.rows is nil; rebuildVisible() then repopulates rows that match the new columns.\\n\\nI reverted both the temporary test and the fix edit; the (untracked) working tree is restored to its original state.\\n\\nSuggested fix (as proposed, verified): in switchTab add `m.table.SetRows(nil)` before the SetColumns call so SetColumns never renders stale wide rows:\\n    m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\")\\n    m.table.SetRows(nil)\\n    m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner))\\n    m.table.SetCursor(0)\\n    m.rebuildVisible()\\nAlso add a regression test that cycles every tab forward (Tab x6) and backward (Shift+Tab x6) and calls View() so a future tab reorder is caught.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}},{\"title\":\"Home detail \\\"Sources\\\" block is garbled on any real (colored) terminal: truncate() is ANSI-unaware and eats the visible text\",\"file\":\"internal/tui/detail.go\",\"line\":\"67\",\"severity\":\"bug\",\"detail\":\"renderHome() builds each source line by concatenating three pre-styled (ANSI-containing) lipgloss renders — `lipgloss.NewStyle().Foreground(color).Render(\\\"  \\\"+dot+\\\" \\\") + th.textStyle().Render(fmt.Sprintf(\\\"%-9s\\\", src.Name)) + \\\"  \\\" + th.dimStyle().Render(src.Detail)` (lines 62-66) — and then passes that already-styled string into `truncate(line, width)` (line 67). `truncate` calls `runewidth.Truncate` (tui.go:392), which is NOT ANSI-aware: it counts the bytes of every `\\\\x1b[..m` escape sequence toward the width budget. Each source line carries 3 styled segments = ~6 escape sequences (~30+ bytes), so the escapes alone exhaust most of `width` and the visible text is cut far too early — the source Detail text (\\\"3 entrypoints\\\", \\\"55 symbols · 8 files\\\", \\\"12 facts across 1 branch(es)\\\") is dropped, and because the visible content collapses while trailing newlines/styling remain, multiple source rows render merged onto single lines. I reproduced this with `termenv.ANSI256` (the profile every real terminal uses): renderHome at width 44 produced a visible \\\"Sources\\\" block reading `● Seed  ● Sessions  ● Semantic  ○ History` all on ONE line and `● Facts  ▌ Freshness` merged, with every Detail string \n... (truncated 12947 chars, full result in /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-Documents-Coding-entire-brain/4e09703c-666f-441f-9b82-52efaa7da599/tasks/wxriffakw.output)</result>\n<usage><agent_count>22</agent_count><subagent_tokens>1174843</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>333</tool_uses><duration_ms>313698</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
