{"parentUuid":"7940d02e-26de-44c9-8d4f-4bbbf3ab63ad","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01P15jcX8nqyCrBn7jdk4rLf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The audit completed (22 agents, 6 dimensions). The result was truncated — let me read the full findings file."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3884,"cache_read_input_tokens":295531,"output_tokens":197,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3884,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":197,"cache_read_input_tokens":295531,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3884,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3884},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cc87Tx5a5KMhb6eKk8vNq","type":"assistant","uuid":"cd051c82-b290-44e1-a1ff-b244e0d65814","timestamp":"2026-06-17T03:21:01.676Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain","sessionId":"af861ed9-9e1d-41a7-8400-b53c2995aed8","version":"2.1.177","gitBranch":"claude/brain-dash-tui","slug":"zippy-floating-lovelace"}
{"parentUuid":"cd051c82-b290-44e1-a1ff-b244e0d65814","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01P15jcX8nqyCrBn7jdk4rLf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017GMPQQ6MrhNwkNjMW2butK","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-Documents-Coding-entire-brain/4e09703c-666f-441f-9b82-52efaa7da599/tasks/wxriffakw.output"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3884,"cache_read_input_tokens":295531,"output_tokens":197,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3884,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":197,"cache_read_input_tokens":295531,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3884,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3884},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cc87Tx5a5KMhb6eKk8vNq","type":"assistant","uuid":"eff3f4c3-cc02-4513-891e-3aa1244f6252","timestamp":"2026-06-17T03:21:02.318Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain","sessionId":"af861ed9-9e1d-41a7-8400-b53c2995aed8","version":"2.1.177","gitBranch":"claude/brain-dash-tui","slug":"zippy-floating-lovelace"}
{"parentUuid":"eff3f4c3-cc02-4513-891e-3aa1244f6252","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"5563e8a9-6ed3-4311-8d52-e70ea1c8bc86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017GMPQQ6MrhNwkNjMW2butK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"summary\": \"Adversarial audit of the new `entire brain dash` TUI + cli wiring; returns verified bug list\",\n3\t  \"agentCount\": 22,\n4\t  \"logs\": [\n5\t    \"auditing 6 dimensions of the brain dash feature\",\n6\t    \"raised 16 findings, 4 confirmed real\"\n7\t  ],\n8\t  \"result\": {\n9\t    \"confirmed\": [\n10\t      {\n11\t        \"title\": \"switchTab panics (index out of range) when leaving a wider tab for a narrower one: SetColumns runs against the previous tab's stale, wider rows\",\n12\t        \"file\": \"internal/tui/tui.go\",\n13\t        \"line\": \"163\",\n14\t        \"severity\": \"crash\",\n15\t        \"detail\": \"In switchTab the table's columns are swapped to the NEW tab BEFORE the rows are replaced:\\n\\n    m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner)) // tui.go:163 — new (narrower) columns\\n    m.table.SetCursor(0)                                // tui.go:164\\n    m.rebuildVisible()                                  // tui.go:165 — only here are new rows set\\n\\nbubbles/table.SetColumns immediately calls UpdateViewport(), which renders the rows still held in the table (the OLD tab's rows) against the NEW columns. renderRow does `for i, value := range m.rows[r] { ... m.cols[i] ... }` (vendored table.go:430-431), so when an old row has more cells than the new tab has columns, m.cols[i] indexes out of range and the whole program panics.\\n\\nColumn counts per tab: Home=3, Facts=3, Sessions=4, History=2, Semantic=3. Any switch from a tab whose visible rows are wider than the destination's column count crashes, e.g. Sessions(4-cell rows) -> History(2 cols), Semantic(3) -> History(2), Facts(3) -> History(2), Sessions(4) -> Semantic(3). This is the primary navigation gesture (Tab / Shift+Tab to cycle sections), so any user with a populated brain hits it almost immediately. switchTab is reached from both Section (Tab, delta +1, tui.go:123) and PrevTab (Shift+Tab, delta -1, tui.go:126).\\n\\nReproduced deterministically: with the sample snapshot, NewModel + WindowSizeMsg, then Tab,Tab,Tab (Home->Facts->Sessions->History) panics: `runtime error: index out of range [2] with length 2` at table.go:431 (renderRow) <- table.go:321 (SetColumns) <- tui.go:163 (switchTab). Also reproduced via Shift+Tab Home->Semantic->History. The existing tests miss it because TestViewSmoke/TestClickSelectsCorrectRow only ever switch Home->Facts, where both tabs have 3 columns (equal count, no overflow).\\n\\nNote resize() (tui.go:244) calls the same SetColumns but is safe there because the tab is unchanged, so the new columns have the same count as the in-table rows; only switchTab changes the column count out from under the existing rows.\",\n16\t        \"suggested_fix\": \"Clear the rows before narrowing the columns so SetColumns never renders stale wide rows. Simplest: insert `m.table.SetRows(nil)` immediately before the SetColumns call in switchTab (verified to fix the panic and keep all existing tests green):\\n\\n    m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\")\\n    m.table.SetRows(nil)\\n    m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner))\\n    m.table.SetCursor(0)\\n    m.rebuildVisible()\\n\\nrebuildVisible then repopulates the rows that match the new columns. Add a regression test that cycles through every tab forward and backward (Tab x6, Shift+Tab x6) and calls View(), so a future reorder is caught.\",\n17\t        \"dimension\": \"panics\",\n18\t        \"verdict\": {\n19\t          \"real\": true,\n20\t          \"reasoning\": \"CONFIRMED as a real crash. I reproduced the panic deterministically and verified the suggested fix.\\n\\nRoot cause (verified against source):\\n- internal/tui/tui.go:157-166 switchTab sets the NEW tab's columns BEFORE replacing the rows: line 162 m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\"), line 163 m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner)), line 164 m.table.SetCursor(0), and only at line 165 m.rebuildVisible() (which calls m.table.SetRows). So between line 163 and 165 the table holds the OLD tab's rows but the NEW tab's columns.\\n- Vendored bubbles v1.0.0 confirms the mechanism. table.go:319-322 SetColumns sets m.cols=c then immediately calls UpdateViewport(). UpdateViewport (table.go:278-280) renders the existing m.rows. renderRow (table.go:428-437) does `for i, value := range m.rows[r] { ... m.cols[i].Width ... }` — it indexes m.cols by the OLD row's cell count. When an old row has more cells than the new column count, m.cols[i] panics with index out of range.\\n- Per-tab cell/column counts (internal/tui/table.go columnsFor + rowsFor): Home=3, Facts=3, Sessions=4, History=2, Semantic=3. Any switch into a narrower tab (e.g. Sessions(4)->History(2), Facts(3)->History(2), Semantic(3)->History(2), Sessions(4)->Semantic(3)) overflows. switchTab is the primary nav gesture, reached from Section/Tab (tui.go:123, delta +1) and PrevTab/Shift+Tab (tui.go:126, delta -1), so a user with a populated brain hits it almost immediately.\\n\\nReproduction: I added a temporary test that does NewModel(sampleSnapshot) + WindowSizeMsg{120,40} then three Tab presses (Home->Facts->Sessions->History) and called View(). It panicked exactly as the finding predicts:\\n  panic: runtime error: index out of range [2] with length 2\\n  table.go:431 renderRow <- table.go:279 UpdateViewport <- table.go:321 SetColumns <- tui.go:163 switchTab <- tui.go:123 Update\\nThe [2]/length 2 matches Sessions' 4-cell rows being rendered against History's 2 columns.\\n\\nWhy existing tests miss it: TestViewSmoke (tui_test.go:132) and TestClickSelectsCorrectRow (tui_test.go:144) only switch Home->Facts (both 3 columns, equal count, no overflow). No existing test cycles into History (2 cols) or Semantic from a wider tab. The sampleSnapshot IS populated (Sessions:2, Facts:3, History:1, Semantic:1), so rows exist at switch time.\\n\\nWhy resize() (tui.go:244) is safe: it calls the same SetColumns but the tab is unchanged, so the new column count equals the in-table rows' cell count; only switchTab changes the column count out from under the existing rows. Confirmed.\\n\\nFix verification: I applied the suggested fix (insert `m.table.SetRows(nil)` immediately before SetColumns at tui.go:163). The repro test then passed and the full existing suite (go test ./internal/tui/) stayed green. The empty-rows path is safe because UpdateViewport's loop renders no rows when m.rows is nil; rebuildVisible() then repopulates rows that match the new columns.\\n\\nI reverted both the temporary test and the fix edit; the (untracked) working tree is restored to its original state.\\n\\nSuggested fix (as proposed, verified): in switchTab add `m.table.SetRows(nil)` before the SetColumns call so SetColumns never renders stale wide rows:\\n    m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\")\\n    m.table.SetRows(nil)\\n    m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner))\\n    m.table.SetCursor(0)\\n    m.rebuildVisible()\\nAlso add a regression test that cycles every tab forward (Tab x6) and backward (Shift+Tab x6) and calls View() so a future tab reorder is caught.\",\n21\t          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n22\t        }\n23\t      },\n24\t      {\n25\t        \"title\": \"Home detail \\\"Sources\\\" block is garbled on any real (colored) terminal: truncate() is ANSI-unaware and eats the visible text\",\n26\t        \"file\": \"internal/tui/detail.go\",\n27\t        \"line\": \"67\",\n28\t        \"severity\": \"bug\",\n29\t        \"detail\": \"renderHome() builds each source line by concatenating three pre-styled (ANSI-containing) lipgloss renders — `lipgloss.NewStyle().Foreground(color).Render(\\\"  \\\"+dot+\\\" \\\") + th.textStyle().Render(fmt.Sprintf(\\\"%-9s\\\", src.Name)) + \\\"  \\\" + th.dimStyle().Render(src.Detail)` (lines 62-66) — and then passes that already-styled string into `truncate(line, width)` (line 67). `truncate` calls `runewidth.Truncate` (tui.go:392), which is NOT ANSI-aware: it counts the bytes of every `\\\\x1b[..m` escape sequence toward the width budget. Each source line carries 3 styled segments = ~6 escape sequences (~30+ bytes), so the escapes alone exhaust most of `width` and the visible text is cut far too early — the source Detail text (\\\"3 entrypoints\\\", \\\"55 symbols · 8 files\\\", \\\"12 facts across 1 branch(es)\\\") is dropped, and because the visible content collapses while trailing newlines/styling remain, multiple source rows render merged onto single lines. I reproduced this with `termenv.ANSI256` (the profile every real terminal uses): renderHome at width 44 produced a visible \\\"Sources\\\" block reading `● Seed  ● Sessions  ● Semantic  ○ History` all on ONE line and `● Facts  ▌ Freshness` merged, with every Detail string gone; measured visible width after `truncate(line,44)` was 15 vs the intended ~40. This is invisible in the test suite ONLY because tui_test.go's TestMain sets `lipgloss.SetColorProfile(termenv.Ascii)` (zero-byte escapes), so the rendered-output tests (TestViewSmoke, TestRenderDetailPerTab, TestDumpDashboard) never exercise the colored path. Every OTHER truncate() call site truncates PLAIN text first and styles afterward (e.g. detail.go:83 `th.dimStyle().Render(truncate(line, width))`, the provenance block 158-175, table.go rows) — line 67 is the only site that inverts the order and truncates an already-styled string, so it is the only affected location.\",\n30\t        \"suggested_fix\": \"Make the width-clamp ANSI-aware at the styled-line site. Simplest: replace `b.WriteString(truncate(line, width))` with `b.WriteString(lipgloss.NewStyle().MaxWidth(width).Render(line))` — lipgloss's MaxWidth measures display width and ignores escape sequences (verified: MaxWidth(44) on the same line preserves visible width 43 vs 15 from runewidth.Truncate). Alternatively, build the line from PLAIN pieces, truncate the plain string, then apply styling last (the pattern used everywhere else in this file). Either way, add a regression test that renders renderHome under a non-Ascii color profile (e.g. wrap a case in `lipgloss.SetColorProfile(termenv.ANSI256)`) and asserts a long source Detail survives, since the current Ascii-only TestMain masks the defect.\",\n31\t        \"dimension\": \"interaction\",\n32\t        \"verdict\": {\n33\t          \"real\": true,\n34\t          \"reasoning\": \"CONFIRMED as a real bug. I verified every load-bearing claim against the actual code and reproduced the defect empirically.\\n\\nEvidence:\\n1. `truncate` is ANSI-unaware. internal/tui/tui.go:392-396 defines `truncate(value, max)` as a thin wrapper over `runewidth.Truncate(value, max, \\\"...\\\")` (import \\\"github.com/mattn/go-runewidth\\\" at tui.go:25). runewidth measures raw runes/bytes — it has no knowledge of `\\\\x1b[..m` escape sequences and counts them toward the width budget.\\n\\n2. Line 67 is the only inverted call site. internal/tui/detail.go:62-67 builds `line` by concatenating THREE pre-styled lipgloss renders — `lipgloss.NewStyle().Foreground(color).Render(\\\"  \\\"+dot+\\\" \\\")` + `th.textStyle().Render(fmt.Sprintf(\\\"%-9s\\\", src.Name))` + `\\\"  \\\" + th.dimStyle().Render(src.Detail)` — then passes that already-styled (ANSI-laden) string into `truncate(line, width)` at line 67. Every OTHER truncate() site in detail.go (lines 51, 83, 129, 175, 201, 203, 233, 257, 277, 284, 348) and all table.go row sites (100-127) truncate a PLAIN string first and style afterward — e.g. line 83 `th.dimStyle().Render(truncate(line, width))` and the provenance block 158-175 which builds a plain `line` then truncates then `th.dimStyle().Render(...)`. So line 67 is genuinely the sole affected location.\\n\\n3. The test suite masks it. internal/tui/tui_test.go:14-16 TestMain sets `lipgloss.SetColorProfile(termenv.Ascii)`, which emits zero-byte escape sequences, so the rendered-output tests never exercise the colored path.\\n\\n4. Empirical reproduction. I wrote a standalone program using the project's exact dep versions (lipgloss v1.1.0, go-runewidth v0.0.19, termenv v0.16.0) under `termenv.ANSI256`. For the line `●  Semantic   55 symbols · 8 files` (visible width 35, raw byte length 65 due to ~30 bytes of ANSI escapes): `runewidth.Truncate(line, 44)` produced output with visible width only 26 and DROPPED the \\\"55 symbols\\\" Detail text entirely, even though the 35-column visible content fits within budget 44. The suggested fix `lipgloss.NewStyle().MaxWidth(44).Render(line)` preserved full visible width 35 with the Detail intact.\\n\\nThis is a user-visible rendering corruption on any real color terminal (the Sources block Detail strings vanish and rows can collapse), invisible only because tests run under Ascii. The suggested fix (use lipgloss MaxWidth at the styled-line site, plus an ANSI256 regression test) is correct and addresses the root cause. A maintainer would fix this.\",\n35\t          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n36\t        }\n37\t      },\n38\t      {\n39\t        \"title\": \"`o` key can open files OUTSIDE the brain via `../` traversal in brain-relative anchor paths\",\n40\t        \"file\": \"internal/cli/dash.go\",\n41\t        \"line\": \"473-478\",\n42\t        \"severity\": \"security\",\n43\t        \"detail\": \"`brainRelPath` rejects empty and absolute paths but does NOT reject `../` traversal. It is fed the brain-relative `Transcript`/`Path` strings that come straight out of on-disk brain files (`factAnchor.Transcript` from facts.ndjson at dash.go:272, `historyRecord.Path` from history/index.json at dash.go:329, and `exportSession.TranscriptPath` from the manifest at dash.go:307). Those per-record path strings are read raw — `loadFacts`/`parseFactsFile` (facts.go:216) and `loadBrainHistoryIndex` (history.go:1576) validate only the *container* file path, never the path fields inside the JSON/NDJSON. The resulting absolute `Source` is stored in the view model and, when the user presses `o`, handed verbatim to the OS opener via `Snapshot.openTarget` (viewmodels.go:217) -> `Model.openSelected` (tui.go:273) -> `openCmd` (tui.go:287, runs `open`/`xdg-open`/`rundll32`). I confirmed `filepath.Join(\\\"/home/user/.entire/brain\\\", \\\"../../../../etc/passwd\\\")` resolves to `/etc/passwd`, which is outside the brain. Since brain/semantic content can be imported from an external archive (`entire brain semantic import <archive>`, semantic.go:4285), a crafted brain is a real threat model — and the codebase already defends this exact class with `validateSemanticSnapshotPath`/`rejectSymlinkPathComponents`. A malicious fact anchor or history record could make `o` launch an arbitrary file (e.g. an attacker-staged script or a sensitive file like `~/.ssh/id_rsa`) outside the read-only brain, breaking the command's containment guarantee.\",\n44\t        \"suggested_fix\": \"Make `brainRelPath` reject traversal and enforce containment: after `filepath.Join`, verify the result stays inside `brainDir` and is not a `../`-prefixed clean path. e.g.\\n\\n```go\\nfunc brainRelPath(brainDir, rel string) string {\\n    if rel == \\\"\\\" || filepath.IsAbs(rel) {\\n        return \\\"\\\"\\n    }\\n    clean := filepath.Clean(filepath.FromSlash(rel))\\n    if clean == \\\"..\\\" || strings.HasPrefix(clean, \\\"..\\\"+string(filepath.Separator)) {\\n        return \\\"\\\"\\n    }\\n    full := filepath.Join(brainDir, clean)\\n    if r, err := filepath.Rel(brainDir, full); err != nil || r == \\\"..\\\" || strings.HasPrefix(r, \\\"..\\\"+string(filepath.Separator)) {\\n        return \\\"\\\"\\n    }\\n    return full\\n}\\n```\\n\\nAdd a test alongside `TestBrainRelPathRejectsAbsolute` (dash_test.go:170) asserting that `brainRelPath(\\\"/brain\\\", \\\"../../etc/passwd\\\") == \\\"\\\"`.\",\n45\t        \"dimension\": \"security\",\n46\t        \"verdict\": {\n47\t          \"real\": true,\n48\t          \"reasoning\": \"Verified the full chain in source; the finding is genuine. (1) brainRelPath (internal/cli/dash.go:473-478) rejects only empty and filepath.IsAbs paths — it does NOT reject `../` traversal. filepath.Join(\\\"/brain\\\",\\\"../../etc/passwd\\\") cleans to \\\"/etc/passwd\\\", outside the brain. The existing test TestBrainRelPathRejectsAbsolute (dash_test.go:170-177) only covers absolute + clean-relative, not traversal. (2) Raw, unvalidated brain-file path fields are fed straight in: dashFactViews feeds f.Provenance[0].Transcript (factAnchor.Transcript, facts.go:111) at dash.go:272 — parseFactsFile (facts.go:216-245) JSON-unmarshals lines raw and never validates the inner Transcript field; dashSessionViews feeds s.TranscriptPath at dash.go:307; dashHistoryViews feeds r.Path (historyRecord.Path, history.go:74) at dash.go:329 — loadBrainHistoryIndex (history.go:1576-1596) validates only the container IndexPath, never per-record .Path. (3) The resulting absolute view.Source flows with no containment check to Snapshot.openTarget (viewmodels.go:217-237) -> Model.openSelected (tui.go:273-283) -> openCmd (tui.go:287-302), which runs open/xdg-open/rundll32 on the path verbatim. The `o` key is bound (keys.go:29) and dispatched (tui.go:120-121). A grep confirmed there is no filepath.Rel/pathInside/Clean/EvalSymlinks containment guard anywhere in internal/tui/*.go or the dash open-target path. (4) The threat model is real and the codebase already defends this exact class elsewhere: validateSemanticSnapshotPath and rejectSymlinkPathComponents (semantic.go:4698) plus the pathInside containment primitive (semantic.go:4870) are applied to semantic snapshot paths, but the dash open-target path bypasses that bar. Since brain content can be imported from an external archive (semantic import), a crafted `../`-laden fact anchor or history record could make `o` launch an arbitrary file outside the read-only brain, breaking the command's containment guarantee. The suggested fix (post-Clean `..`-prefix check + filepath.Rel-based containment, mirroring pathInside, plus a traversal test) is correct and idiomatic. I was asked to refute, but the code does not support refutation; every link is verifiable at the cited file:line.\",\n49\t          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n50\t        }\n51\t      },\n52\t      {\n53\t        \"title\": \"TestHyperlinkIsZeroWidth asserts a property of dead code — osc8 is never emitted by the dashboard\",\n54\t        \"file\": \"internal/tui/tui_test.go:183\",\n55\t        \"line\": \"183-191\",\n56\t        \"severity\": \"minor\",\n57\t        \"detail\": \"TestHyperlinkIsZeroWidth verifies osc8() escapes are zero-width 'or the viewport and pane padding miscount and the right border misaligns.' But grep over non-test tui/*.go shows osc8 (and linkStyle) are never called in the production render path — writeOpenHint (detail.go:353-359) ignores the link entirely and prints a static 'press o to open source' string, discarding the `source` argument's value. So the dashboard never emits an OSC 8 hyperlink, and this test gives false confidence that a width/border concern is covered when the code it protects doesn't ship. Either the feature regressed out of the render path (osc8 should be used in writeOpenHint) or the test (and osc8/linkStyle) is testing abandoned code.\",\n58\t        \"suggested_fix\": \"Decide intent: if hyperlinks are meant to ship, wire osc8 into writeOpenHint (e.g. render osc8(fileURL(source), 'open ↗') and pass it through a non-width-constrained style) and add a View()-level test asserting the rendered detail contains the OSC 8 escape for a row with a Source. If not, delete osc8/linkStyle/TestHyperlinkIsZeroWidth as dead code so the suite doesn't certify an unused helper.\",\n59\t        \"dimension\": \"tests\",\n60\t        \"verdict\": {\n61\t          \"real\": true,\n62\t          \"reasoning\": \"I verified every factual claim in the finding against the code; all hold.\\n\\nEvidence:\\n- `osc8` is defined at internal/tui/styles.go:150. A repo-wide grep (`grep -rcn \\\"osc8\\\" internal/`) shows its only caller is the test at internal/tui/tui_test.go:187. Zero production callers.\\n- `linkStyle` is defined at internal/tui/styles.go:141 and has ZERO callers anywhere (not even in tests). Its sole input, the `Theme.Link` color field (styles.go:18, set in 4 theme presets at lines 33/46/59/72), is consumed only by `linkStyle` — so `Link`, `linkStyle`, and `osc8` form a dead-code triad relative to the shipped feature.\\n- The production \\\"open source\\\" path does NOT use OSC 8. `writeOpenHint` (internal/tui/detail.go:353-359) takes a `source` argument, checks only whether it is empty, then prints a static literal \\\"press o to open source\\\" — the `source` value is discarded, no hyperlink is rendered. It is the only consumer of a row's Source in the detail render path (called at detail.go:181/239/265/308).\\n- Source-opening actually works via a different mechanism: the `o` keybinding (internal/tui/keys.go:29) dispatches to `m.openSelected()` (tui.go:120-121) → `openCmd` (tui.go:287-302), which shells out to `open`/`xdg-open`/`rundll32`. So no rendered string ever contains an OSC 8 escape sequence.\\n\\nConclusion: TestHyperlinkIsZeroWidth (tui_test.go:183-191) asserts that `osc8`'s escapes are zero-width to protect viewport/pane-padding/right-border alignment — but no production render path ever emits an OSC 8 sequence, so the width/border concern the test claims to cover does not exist in shipped code. This is genuine: the test certifies a property of an unused helper (false coverage signal), and `osc8` + `linkStyle` + the `Link` theme color are dead code. A maintainer would act on this — it is exactly the kind of dead-code-plus-vacuous-test issue review catches.\\n\\nIt is correctly scored minor: the user-visible \\\"open source\\\" feature still functions (via shell-out), so this is not a functional regression — only the OSC 8 hyperlink rendering and its guarding test are dead. The suggested fix (decide intent: either wire `osc8(fileURL(source), ...)` into writeOpenHint through a non-width-constrained style and add a View()-level assertion that a row with a Source renders the OSC 8 escape, OR delete osc8/linkStyle/Theme.Link/TestHyperlinkIsZeroWidth as dead code) is concrete and correct. Note one detail: `fileURL` does not yet exist in the package (grep found no definition), so the \\\"wire it in\\\" branch would also need that helper added.\",\n63\t          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n64\t        }\n65\t      }\n66\t    ],\n67\t    \"raisedCount\": 16,\n68\t    \"confirmedCount\": 4\n69\t  }\n70\t}"}]},"uuid":"9fc624f8-aade-4ce7-8330-49584eb3dfab","timestamp":"2026-06-17T03:21:02.356Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-Documents-Coding-entire-brain/4e09703c-666f-441f-9b82-52efaa7da599/tasks/wxriffakw.output","content":"{\n  \"summary\": \"Adversarial audit of the new `entire brain dash` TUI + cli wiring; returns verified bug list\",\n  \"agentCount\": 22,\n  \"logs\": [\n    \"auditing 6 dimensions of the brain dash feature\",\n    \"raised 16 findings, 4 confirmed real\"\n  ],\n  \"result\": {\n    \"confirmed\": [\n      {\n        \"title\": \"switchTab panics (index out of range) when leaving a wider tab for a narrower one: SetColumns runs against the previous tab's stale, wider rows\",\n        \"file\": \"internal/tui/tui.go\",\n        \"line\": \"163\",\n        \"severity\": \"crash\",\n        \"detail\": \"In switchTab the table's columns are swapped to the NEW tab BEFORE the rows are replaced:\\n\\n    m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner)) // tui.go:163 — new (narrower) columns\\n    m.table.SetCursor(0)                                // tui.go:164\\n    m.rebuildVisible()                                  // tui.go:165 — only here are new rows set\\n\\nbubbles/table.SetColumns immediately calls UpdateViewport(), which renders the rows still held in the table (the OLD tab's rows) against the NEW columns. renderRow does `for i, value := range m.rows[r] { ... m.cols[i] ... }` (vendored table.go:430-431), so when an old row has more cells than the new tab has columns, m.cols[i] indexes out of range and the whole program panics.\\n\\nColumn counts per tab: Home=3, Facts=3, Sessions=4, History=2, Semantic=3. Any switch from a tab whose visible rows are wider than the destination's column count crashes, e.g. Sessions(4-cell rows) -> History(2 cols), Semantic(3) -> History(2), Facts(3) -> History(2), Sessions(4) -> Semantic(3). This is the primary navigation gesture (Tab / Shift+Tab to cycle sections), so any user with a populated brain hits it almost immediately. switchTab is reached from both Section (Tab, delta +1, tui.go:123) and PrevTab (Shift+Tab, delta -1, tui.go:126).\\n\\nReproduced deterministically: with the sample snapshot, NewModel + WindowSizeMsg, then Tab,Tab,Tab (Home->Facts->Sessions->History) panics: `runtime error: index out of range [2] with length 2` at table.go:431 (renderRow) <- table.go:321 (SetColumns) <- tui.go:163 (switchTab). Also reproduced via Shift+Tab Home->Semantic->History. The existing tests miss it because TestViewSmoke/TestClickSelectsCorrectRow only ever switch Home->Facts, where both tabs have 3 columns (equal count, no overflow).\\n\\nNote resize() (tui.go:244) calls the same SetColumns but is safe there because the tab is unchanged, so the new columns have the same count as the in-table rows; only switchTab changes the column count out from under the existing rows.\",\n        \"suggested_fix\": \"Clear the rows before narrowing the columns so SetColumns never renders stale wide rows. Simplest: insert `m.table.SetRows(nil)` immediately before the SetColumns call in switchTab (verified to fix the panic and keep all existing tests green):\\n\\n    m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\")\\n    m.table.SetRows(nil)\\n    m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner))\\n    m.table.SetCursor(0)\\n    m.rebuildVisible()\\n\\nrebuildVisible then repopulates the rows that match the new columns. Add a regression test that cycles through every tab forward and backward (Tab x6, Shift+Tab x6) and calls View(), so a future reorder is caught.\",\n        \"dimension\": \"panics\",\n        \"verdict\": {\n          \"real\": true,\n          \"reasoning\": \"CONFIRMED as a real crash. I reproduced the panic deterministically and verified the suggested fix.\\n\\nRoot cause (verified against source):\\n- internal/tui/tui.go:157-166 switchTab sets the NEW tab's columns BEFORE replacing the rows: line 162 m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\"), line 163 m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner)), line 164 m.table.SetCursor(0), and only at line 165 m.rebuildVisible() (which calls m.table.SetRows). So between line 163 and 165 the table holds the OLD tab's rows but the NEW tab's columns.\\n- Vendored bubbles v1.0.0 confirms the mechanism. table.go:319-322 SetColumns sets m.cols=c then immediately calls UpdateViewport(). UpdateViewport (table.go:278-280) renders the existing m.rows. renderRow (table.go:428-437) does `for i, value := range m.rows[r] { ... m.cols[i].Width ... }` — it indexes m.cols by the OLD row's cell count. When an old row has more cells than the new column count, m.cols[i] panics with index out of range.\\n- Per-tab cell/column counts (internal/tui/table.go columnsFor + rowsFor): Home=3, Facts=3, Sessions=4, History=2, Semantic=3. Any switch into a narrower tab (e.g. Sessions(4)->History(2), Facts(3)->History(2), Semantic(3)->History(2), Sessions(4)->Semantic(3)) overflows. switchTab is the primary nav gesture, reached from Section/Tab (tui.go:123, delta +1) and PrevTab/Shift+Tab (tui.go:126, delta -1), so a user with a populated brain hits it almost immediately.\\n\\nReproduction: I added a temporary test that does NewModel(sampleSnapshot) + WindowSizeMsg{120,40} then three Tab presses (Home->Facts->Sessions->History) and called View(). It panicked exactly as the finding predicts:\\n  panic: runtime error: index out of range [2] with length 2\\n  table.go:431 renderRow <- table.go:279 UpdateViewport <- table.go:321 SetColumns <- tui.go:163 switchTab <- tui.go:123 Update\\nThe [2]/length 2 matches Sessions' 4-cell rows being rendered against History's 2 columns.\\n\\nWhy existing tests miss it: TestViewSmoke (tui_test.go:132) and TestClickSelectsCorrectRow (tui_test.go:144) only switch Home->Facts (both 3 columns, equal count, no overflow). No existing test cycles into History (2 cols) or Semantic from a wider tab. The sampleSnapshot IS populated (Sessions:2, Facts:3, History:1, Semantic:1), so rows exist at switch time.\\n\\nWhy resize() (tui.go:244) is safe: it calls the same SetColumns but the tab is unchanged, so the new column count equals the in-table rows' cell count; only switchTab changes the column count out from under the existing rows. Confirmed.\\n\\nFix verification: I applied the suggested fix (insert `m.table.SetRows(nil)` immediately before SetColumns at tui.go:163). The repro test then passed and the full existing suite (go test ./internal/tui/) stayed green. The empty-rows path is safe because UpdateViewport's loop renders no rows when m.rows is nil; rebuildVisible() then repopulates rows that match the new columns.\\n\\nI reverted both the temporary test and the fix edit; the (untracked) working tree is restored to its original state.\\n\\nSuggested fix (as proposed, verified): in switchTab add `m.table.SetRows(nil)` before the SetColumns call so SetColumns never renders stale wide rows:\\n    m.filter.SetValue(\\\"\\\")\\n    m.table.SetRows(nil)\\n    m.table.SetColumns(columnsFor(m.tab, m.leftInner))\\n    m.table.SetCursor(0)\\n    m.rebuildVisible()\\nAlso add a regression test that cycles every tab forward (Tab x6) and backward (Shift+Tab x6) and calls View() so a future tab reorder is caught.\",\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"Home detail \\\"Sources\\\" block is garbled on any real (colored) terminal: truncate() is ANSI-unaware and eats the visible text\",\n        \"file\": \"internal/tui/detail.go\",\n        \"line\": \"67\",\n        \"severity\": \"bug\",\n        \"detail\": \"renderHome() builds each source line by concatenating three pre-styled (ANSI-containing) lipgloss renders — `lipgloss.NewStyle().Foreground(color).Render(\\\"  \\\"+dot+\\\" \\\") + th.textStyle().Render(fmt.Sprintf(\\\"%-9s\\\", src.Name)) + \\\"  \\\" + th.dimStyle().Render(src.Detail)` (lines 62-66) — and then passes that already-styled string into `truncate(line, width)` (line 67). `truncate` calls `runewidth.Truncate` (tui.go:392), which is NOT ANSI-aware: it counts the bytes of every `\\\\x1b[..m` escape sequence toward the width budget. Each source line carries 3 styled segments = ~6 escape sequences (~30+ bytes), so the escapes alone exhaust most of `width` and the visible text is cut far too early — the source Detail text (\\\"3 entrypoints\\\", \\\"55 symbols · 8 files\\\", \\\"12 facts across 1 branch(es)\\\") is dropped, and because the visible content collapses while trailing newlines/styling remain, multiple source rows render merged onto single lines. I reproduced this with `termenv.ANSI256` (the profile every real terminal uses): renderHome at width 44 produced a visible \\\"Sources\\\" block reading `● Seed  ● Sessions  ● Semantic  ○ History` all on ONE line and `● Facts  ▌ Freshness` merged, with every Detail string gone; measured visible width after `truncate(line,44)` was 15 vs the intended ~40. This is invisible in the test suite ONLY because tui_test.go's TestMain sets `lipgloss.SetColorProfile(termenv.Ascii)` (zero-byte escapes), so the rendered-output tests (TestViewSmoke, TestRenderDetailPerTab, TestDumpDashboard) never exercise the colored path. Every OTHER truncate() call site truncates PLAIN text first and styles afterward (e.g. detail.go:83 `th.dimStyle().Render(truncate(line, width))`, the provenance block 158-175, table.go rows) — line 67 is the only site that inverts the order and truncates an already-styled string, so it is the only affected location.\",\n        \"suggested_fix\": \"Make the width-clamp ANSI-aware at the styled-line site. Simplest: replace `b.WriteString(truncate(line, width))` with `b.WriteString(lipgloss.NewStyle().MaxWidth(width).Render(line))` — lipgloss's MaxWidth measures display width and ignores escape sequences (verified: MaxWidth(44) on the same line preserves visible width 43 vs 15 from runewidth.Truncate). Alternatively, build the line from PLAIN pieces, truncate the plain string, then apply styling last (the pattern used everywhere else in this file). Either way, add a regression test that renders renderHome under a non-Ascii color profile (e.g. wrap a case in `lipgloss.SetColorProfile(termenv.ANSI256)`) and asserts a long source Detail survives, since the current Ascii-only TestMain masks the defect.\",\n        \"dimension\": \"interaction\",\n        \"verdict\": {\n          \"real\": true,\n          \"reasoning\": \"CONFIRMED as a real bug. I verified every load-bearing claim against the actual code and reproduced the defect empirically.\\n\\nEvidence:\\n1. `truncate` is ANSI-unaware. internal/tui/tui.go:392-396 defines `truncate(value, max)` as a thin wrapper over `runewidth.Truncate(value, max, \\\"...\\\")` (import \\\"github.com/mattn/go-runewidth\\\" at tui.go:25). runewidth measures raw runes/bytes — it has no knowledge of `\\\\x1b[..m` escape sequences and counts them toward the width budget.\\n\\n2. Line 67 is the only inverted call site. internal/tui/detail.go:62-67 builds `line` by concatenating THREE pre-styled lipgloss renders — `lipgloss.NewStyle().Foreground(color).Render(\\\"  \\\"+dot+\\\" \\\")` + `th.textStyle().Render(fmt.Sprintf(\\\"%-9s\\\", src.Name))` + `\\\"  \\\" + th.dimStyle().Render(src.Detail)` — then passes that already-styled (ANSI-laden) string into `truncate(line, width)` at line 67. Every OTHER truncate() site in detail.go (lines 51, 83, 129, 175, 201, 203, 233, 257, 277, 284, 348) and all table.go row sites (100-127) truncate a PLAIN string first and style afterward — e.g. line 83 `th.dimStyle().Render(truncate(line, width))` and the provenance block 158-175 which builds a plain `line` then truncates then `th.dimStyle().Render(...)`. So line 67 is genuinely the sole affected location.\\n\\n3. The test suite masks it. internal/tui/tui_test.go:14-16 TestMain sets `lipgloss.SetColorProfile(termenv.Ascii)`, which emits zero-byte escape sequences, so the rendered-output tests never exercise the colored path.\\n\\n4. Empirical reproduction. I wrote a standalone program using the project's exact dep versions (lipgloss v1.1.0, go-runewidth v0.0.19, termenv v0.16.0) under `termenv.ANSI256`. For the line `●  Semantic   55 symbols · 8 files` (visible width 35, raw byte length 65 due to ~30 bytes of ANSI escapes): `runewidth.Truncate(line, 44)` produced output with visible width only 26 and DROPPED the \\\"55 symbols\\\" Detail text entirely, even though the 35-column visible content fits within budget 44. The suggested fix `lipgloss.NewStyle().MaxWidth(44).Render(line)` preserved full visible width 35 with the Detail intact.\\n\\nThis is a user-visible rendering corruption on any real color terminal (the Sources block Detail strings vanish and rows can collapse), invisible only because tests run under Ascii. The suggested fix (use lipgloss MaxWidth at the styled-line site, plus an ANSI256 regression test) is correct and addresses the root cause. A maintainer would fix this.\",\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"`o` key can open files OUTSIDE the brain via `../` traversal in brain-relative anchor paths\",\n        \"file\": \"internal/cli/dash.go\",\n        \"line\": \"473-478\",\n        \"severity\": \"security\",\n        \"detail\": \"`brainRelPath` rejects empty and absolute paths but does NOT reject `../` traversal. It is fed the brain-relative `Transcript`/`Path` strings that come straight out of on-disk brain files (`factAnchor.Transcript` from facts.ndjson at dash.go:272, `historyRecord.Path` from history/index.json at dash.go:329, and `exportSession.TranscriptPath` from the manifest at dash.go:307). Those per-record path strings are read raw — `loadFacts`/`parseFactsFile` (facts.go:216) and `loadBrainHistoryIndex` (history.go:1576) validate only the *container* file path, never the path fields inside the JSON/NDJSON. The resulting absolute `Source` is stored in the view model and, when the user presses `o`, handed verbatim to the OS opener via `Snapshot.openTarget` (viewmodels.go:217) -> `Model.openSelected` (tui.go:273) -> `openCmd` (tui.go:287, runs `open`/`xdg-open`/`rundll32`). I confirmed `filepath.Join(\\\"/home/user/.entire/brain\\\", \\\"../../../../etc/passwd\\\")` resolves to `/etc/passwd`, which is outside the brain. Since brain/semantic content can be imported from an external archive (`entire brain semantic import <archive>`, semantic.go:4285), a crafted brain is a real threat model — and the codebase already defends this exact class with `validateSemanticSnapshotPath`/`rejectSymlinkPathComponents`. A malicious fact anchor or history record could make `o` launch an arbitrary file (e.g. an attacker-staged script or a sensitive file like `~/.ssh/id_rsa`) outside the read-only brain, breaking the command's containment guarantee.\",\n        \"suggested_fix\": \"Make `brainRelPath` reject traversal and enforce containment: after `filepath.Join`, verify the result stays inside `brainDir` and is not a `../`-prefixed clean path. e.g.\\n\\n```go\\nfunc brainRelPath(brainDir, rel string) string {\\n    if rel == \\\"\\\" || filepath.IsAbs(rel) {\\n        return \\\"\\\"\\n    }\\n    clean := filepath.Clean(filepath.FromSlash(rel))\\n    if clean == \\\"..\\\" || strings.HasPrefix(clean, \\\"..\\\"+string(filepath.Separator)) {\\n        return \\\"\\\"\\n    }\\n    full := filepath.Join(brainDir, clean)\\n    if r, err := filepath.Rel(brainDir, full); err != nil || r == \\\"..\\\" || strings.HasPrefix(r, \\\"..\\\"+string(filepath.Separator)) {\\n        return \\\"\\\"\\n    }\\n    return full\\n}\\n```\\n\\nAdd a test alongside `TestBrainRelPathRejectsAbsolute` (dash_test.go:170) asserting that `brainRelPath(\\\"/brain\\\", \\\"../../etc/passwd\\\") == \\\"\\\"`.\",\n        \"dimension\": \"security\",\n        \"verdict\": {\n          \"real\": true,\n          \"reasoning\": \"Verified the full chain in source; the finding is genuine. (1) brainRelPath (internal/cli/dash.go:473-478) rejects only empty and filepath.IsAbs paths — it does NOT reject `../` traversal. filepath.Join(\\\"/brain\\\",\\\"../../etc/passwd\\\") cleans to \\\"/etc/passwd\\\", outside the brain. The existing test TestBrainRelPathRejectsAbsolute (dash_test.go:170-177) only covers absolute + clean-relative, not traversal. (2) Raw, unvalidated brain-file path fields are fed straight in: dashFactViews feeds f.Provenance[0].Transcript (factAnchor.Transcript, facts.go:111) at dash.go:272 — parseFactsFile (facts.go:216-245) JSON-unmarshals lines raw and never validates the inner Transcript field; dashSessionViews feeds s.TranscriptPath at dash.go:307; dashHistoryViews feeds r.Path (historyRecord.Path, history.go:74) at dash.go:329 — loadBrainHistoryIndex (history.go:1576-1596) validates only the container IndexPath, never per-record .Path. (3) The resulting absolute view.Source flows with no containment check to Snapshot.openTarget (viewmodels.go:217-237) -> Model.openSelected (tui.go:273-283) -> openCmd (tui.go:287-302), which runs open/xdg-open/rundll32 on the path verbatim. The `o` key is bound (keys.go:29) and dispatched (tui.go:120-121). A grep confirmed there is no filepath.Rel/pathInside/Clean/EvalSymlinks containment guard anywhere in internal/tui/*.go or the dash open-target path. (4) The threat model is real and the codebase already defends this exact class elsewhere: validateSemanticSnapshotPath and rejectSymlinkPathComponents (semantic.go:4698) plus the pathInside containment primitive (semantic.go:4870) are applied to semantic snapshot paths, but the dash open-target path bypasses that bar. Since brain content can be imported from an external archive (semantic import), a crafted `../`-laden fact anchor or history record could make `o` launch an arbitrary file outside the read-only brain, breaking the command's containment guarantee. The suggested fix (post-Clean `..`-prefix check + filepath.Rel-based containment, mirroring pathInside, plus a traversal test) is correct and idiomatic. I was asked to refute, but the code does not support refutation; every link is verifiable at the cited file:line.\",\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        }\n      },\n      {\n        \"title\": \"TestHyperlinkIsZeroWidth asserts a property of dead code — osc8 is never emitted by the dashboard\",\n        \"file\": \"internal/tui/tui_test.go:183\",\n        \"line\": \"183-191\",\n        \"severity\": \"minor\",\n        \"detail\": \"TestHyperlinkIsZeroWidth verifies osc8() escapes are zero-width 'or the viewport and pane padding miscount and the right border misaligns.' But grep over non-test tui/*.go shows osc8 (and linkStyle) are never called in the production render path — writeOpenHint (detail.go:353-359) ignores the link entirely and prints a static 'press o to open source' string, discarding the `source` argument's value. So the dashboard never emits an OSC 8 hyperlink, and this test gives false confidence that a width/border concern is covered when the code it protects doesn't ship. Either the feature regressed out of the render path (osc8 should be used in writeOpenHint) or the test (and osc8/linkStyle) is testing abandoned code.\",\n        \"suggested_fix\": \"Decide intent: if hyperlinks are meant to ship, wire osc8 into writeOpenHint (e.g. render osc8(fileURL(source), 'open ↗') and pass it through a non-width-constrained style) and add a View()-level test asserting the rendered detail contains the OSC 8 escape for a row with a Source. If not, delete osc8/linkStyle/TestHyperlinkIsZeroWidth as dead code so the suite doesn't certify an unused helper.\",\n        \"dimension\": \"tests\",\n        \"verdict\": {\n          \"real\": true,\n          \"reasoning\": \"I verified every factual claim in the finding against the code; all hold.\\n\\nEvidence:\\n- `osc8` is defined at internal/tui/styles.go:150. A repo-wide grep (`grep -rcn \\\"osc8\\\" internal/`) shows its only caller is the test at internal/tui/tui_test.go:187. Zero production callers.\\n- `linkStyle` is defined at internal/tui/styles.go:141 and has ZERO callers anywhere (not even in tests). Its sole input, the `Theme.Link` color field (styles.go:18, set in 4 theme presets at lines 33/46/59/72), is consumed only by `linkStyle` — so `Link`, `linkStyle`, and `osc8` form a dead-code triad relative to the shipped feature.\\n- The production \\\"open source\\\" path does NOT use OSC 8. `writeOpenHint` (internal/tui/detail.go:353-359) takes a `source` argument, checks only whether it is empty, then prints a static literal \\\"press o to open source\\\" — the `source` value is discarded, no hyperlink is rendered. It is the only consumer of a row's Source in the detail render path (called at detail.go:181/239/265/308).\\n- Source-opening actually works via a different mechanism: the `o` keybinding (internal/tui/keys.go:29) dispatches to `m.openSelected()` (tui.go:120-121) → `openCmd` (tui.go:287-302), which shells out to `open`/`xdg-open`/`rundll32`. So no rendered string ever contains an OSC 8 escape sequence.\\n\\nConclusion: TestHyperlinkIsZeroWidth (tui_test.go:183-191) asserts that `osc8`'s escapes are zero-width to protect viewport/pane-padding/right-border alignment — but no production render path ever emits an OSC 8 sequence, so the width/border concern the test claims to cover does not exist in shipped code. This is genuine: the test certifies a property of an unused helper (false coverage signal), and `osc8` + `linkStyle` + the `Link` theme color are dead code. A maintainer would act on this — it is exactly the kind of dead-code-plus-vacuous-test issue review catches.\\n\\nIt is correctly scored minor: the user-visible \\\"open source\\\" feature still functions (via shell-out), so this is not a functional regression — only the OSC 8 hyperlink rendering and its guarding test are dead. The suggested fix (decide intent: either wire `osc8(fileURL(source), ...)` into writeOpenHint through a non-width-constrained style and add a View()-level assertion that a row with a Source renders the OSC 8 escape, OR delete osc8/linkStyle/Theme.Link/TestHyperlinkIsZeroWidth as dead code) is concrete and correct. Note one detail: `fileURL` does not yet exist in the package (grep found no definition), so the \\\"wire it in\\\" branch would also need that helper added.\",\n          \"confidence\": \"high\"\n        }\n      }\n    ],\n    \"raisedCount\": 16,\n    \"confirmedCount\": 4\n  }\n}","numLines":70,"startLine":1,"totalLines":70}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"eff3f4c3-cc02-4513-891e-3aa1244f6252","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain","sessionId":"af861ed9-9e1d-41a7-8400-b53c2995aed8","version":"2.1.177","gitBranch":"claude/brain-dash-tui","slug":"zippy-floating-lovelace"}
