_brain_positive\\\"\\n```\\nSo candidate `proof_ready` requires **n>=4 per side** AND a `brain_positive` verdict. `brain_positive` itself requires `correctness_win` OR `efficiency_win` (`run.py:3473-3478`):\\n```python\\nbrain_validation_clean = success_rate_condition == 1.0\\ncorrectness_win = brain_validation_clean and (success_delta > 0 or score_delta >= 3)\\nequal_or_better = success_delta >= 0 and score_delta >= 0\\nefficiency_win = brain_validation_clean and equal_or_better and (\\n    (isinstance(time_improvement, float) and time_improvement >= 0.20)\\n    or (isinstance(token_improvement, float) and token_improvement >= 0.20)\\n)\\n```\\nNote: the brain arm must be **100% pass** (`brain_validation_clean`), and either it strictly out-passes/out-scores the baseline, or (passing equal-or-better) shows a **>=20% relative** time-or-token improvement.\\n\\n**Pass B — stability-gated final, `run.py:3873-3874`** (overwrites the candidate after Holm correction is attached):\\n```python\\ncomp[\\\"candidate_proof_ready\\\"] = bool(comp.get(\\\"proof_ready\\\"))\\ncomp[\\\"proof_ready\\\"] = bool(comp.get(\\\"candidate_proof_ready\\\")\\n                           and comp[\\\"stability\\\"].get(\\\"tag\\\") == \\\"brain_positive_stable\\\")\\n```\\nSo **final `proof_ready` = (candidate proof_ready) AND (stability tag == `brain_positive_stable`).** This is the authoritative per-comparison definition written into each suite's `summary.json`.\\n\\n**Stage 2 — the auditor (`audit_codex.py`) only counts a comparison toward `proof_ready_comparisons` if it ALSO survives evidence checks.** A comparison is dropped (its `pass` set False, or never counted) if any of these flags fire:\\n- `audit_codex.py:986-989` — `G:proof_ready_without_n4` (n<4 per side) or `G:proof_ready_without_stable_gate` (stability tag != `brain_positive_stable`).\\n- `audit_codex.py:1254-1268` — record backing missing: `G:proof_ready_without_matching_records` (and the MCP-specific `G:proof_ready_without_mcp_verified_condition_records`, `..._named_mcp_tool...`, `..._completed_named_mcp_tool...`).\\n- `audit_codex.py:1270` — only counted if `c.get(\\\"proof_ready\\\") and c.get(\\\"pass\\\")`:\\n```python\\nsuite_proofs = sum(1 for c in comp_audits if c.get(\\\"proof_ready\\\") and c.get(\\\"pass\\\"))\\n```\\n\\n**Record backing** (`proof_ready_record_backing`, `audit_codex.py:1008-1092`): for the stored `n_condition`/`n_baseline`, there must exist that many **distinct, passing, provenance-OK records** with matching `run_id` AND `repetition` for both the condition and the `no_brain` baseline (`audit_codex.py:1061-1070`), and for MCP conditions, that many `mcp_verified` (and for named-tool scopes, `mcp_named_tool_verified` + `mcp_named_tool_completed`) records (`1071-1092`).\\n\\n## 2. Operational definitions of the gate vocabulary\\n\\n- **`saturated`** — both arms already pass 100% (no correctness headroom). Two slightly different forms:\\n  - Verdict layer (`run.py:3480-3481`): `both_solved = success_rate_condition == 1.0 and success_rate_baseline == 1.0`; `saturated = both_solved and abs(score_delta) <= 1 and not efficiency_win`.\\n  - Stability layer (`run.py:3628`): `saturated = pass_cond == 1.0 and pass_base == 1.0` — sets tag `\\\"saturated\\\"` when no stable win exists.\\n- **`noisy`** — the stability fallback when there is neither a stable win nor saturation (`run.py:3633-3634`): `else: tag = \\\"noisy\\\"`. There is **no fixed CV threshold** that flips this; CV is *reported* (`run.py:3638-3641`) but the noisy/stable decision is driven by significance + drop-one survival, not a CV cutoff. (Stated uncertainty: I found no numeric CV threshold gating the tag anywhere in `run.py`; CV is descriptive only.)\\n- **`negative`** — `verdict == \\\"brain_negative\\\"` (`run.py:3511-3512`) via `overhead_bad` (`run.py:3482-3487`): `success_delta < 0` OR `score_delta < -1` OR time-overhead `> 0.15` (15% slower) OR token-overhead `> 0.15`. A `brain_negative` (or `saturated`, `inconclusive`, `pilot_brain_positive`) can never be `proof_ready`.\\n- **`no_release_claim`** — a *manifest claim_policy* (`audit_codex.py:1349-1358`, constants at `audit_workspace_radar_evidence.py:21-22`). Under this policy the gate **inverts**: it *requires* `proof_ready_comparisons == 0` and fails if any survive (`audit_codex.py:1354-1355`: `if proof_ready > 0: failures.append(... > 0)`). It is the explicit \\\"we are NOT claiming agent lift yet\\\" lane. `audit_release_matrix.py:131-143` maps it to status `\\\"no-claim\\\"` / `claimable=False`.\\n\\n## 3. The agent-lift statistic and its significance test\\n\\nThe **headline metric is validation pass-rate plus measured total-tokens**, not the composite score (`run.py:3891`): `\\\"headline_metric\\\": \\\"validation pass-rate + measured tokens; composite score and p-values are secondary\\\"`.\\n\\nThe stability gate (`comparison_stability`, `run.py:3582-3649`) accepts a stable brain win via **either**:\\n- **Token win** (`run.py:3613-3617`): tokens significant **AND** lower **AND** survives drop-one. Significance uses the **more conservative of raw Welch p and Holm-adjusted p**, threshold **p < 0.05** (`run.py:3603-3607`):\\n```python\\nraw_p = comparison.get(\\\"p_value_total_tokens\\\")\\nholm_p = comparison.get(\\\"p_value_total_tokens_holm\\\")\\ncandidates = [p for p in (raw_p, holm_p) if isinstance(p, (int, float))]\\neff_p = max(candidates) if candidates else None\\ntokens_significant = eff_p is not None and eff_p < 0.05\\n```\\n- **Pass-rate lift** (`run.py:3621-3626`): `pass_cond > pass_base` AND survives drop-one (no p-test on pass-rate).\\n\\nThe p-value is a **two-sided Welch's t-test** (`run.py:3888`) with **Holm–Bonferroni family-wise correction across all suite p-values** (`run.py:3845-3866`); the `_holm` field is what the gate consumes. The gate \\\"only downgrades; it never manufactures significance\\\" (`run.py:3895`).\\n\\n`delta_survives_drop_one` (`run.py:3563-3579`) is a **one-sided drop-one robustness check** (not full jackknife): it removes the single most brain-favourable rep from each arm and requires the mean delta to still point the right way; returns False below n=2 per arm.\\n\\n## 4. The \\\"retained\\\" / \\\"clean\\\" concept\\n\\nTwo distinct mechanisms:\\n\\n**(a) Retained proof harvesting (`run.py:3913-4004`).** `load_existing_phase2_proofs` walks each suite's `summary.json` but only after `retained_suite_audit_clean` (`run.py:3944-3960`) confirms the cross-referenced `codex-audit-report.json` shows the suite has records, **zero flagged records**, full provenance, and **no flagged comparisons**:\\n```python\\nif int(suite_report.get(\\\"n_flagged_records\\\") or 0) != 0:\\n    return False\\nif int(suite_report.get(\\\"n_provenance_ok\\\") or 0) != records:\\n    return False\\nfor comparison in suite_report.get(\\\"comparisons\\\", []):\\n    if isinstance(comparison, dict) and comparison.get(\\\"flags\\\"):\\n        return False\\n```\\nThen `retained_brain_positive_comparison` (`run.py:3963-4004`) keeps a comparison only if `proof_ready` is true AND `stability.tag == \\\"brain_positive_stable\\\"`, then re-confirms a Holm-significant (`p<0.05`) correctness or metric hit before retaining it as `\\\"proof_level\\\": \\\"existing_repeated_run\\\"`.\\n\\n**(b) \\\"clean\\\" reruns in the release matrix.** `audit_release_matrix.py:140-143` distinguishes the no-claim case: `\\\"B1 clean reruns are retained and audit-clean, but no comparison survived the proof-ready gate\\\"` vs. a still-confounded state. `make_report.py:9` and `full_report.py:4` define \\\"integrity-clean\\\" as suites that cross-reference `codex-audit-report.json` and **drop audit hard-flagged runs and infra failures** (`make_report.py:121`), but neither file reads `proof_ready` — they are presentation tables, not part of the gate.\\n\\n## Scope partitioning (used by `required_*_proof_scopes`)\\n`comparison_proof_scope` (`audit_codex.py:1129-1138`) buckets each proof into `mcp` / `mcp_radar_location_only` / `mcp_radar_answer_assisted` / `mcp_workspace_radar_location_only` / `semantic` / `history` / `other`. The release gate can require >=1 proof in named scopes (`audit_codex.py:1389-1396`), and `require_proof_ready_per_suite` requires every suite to carry at least one proof (`audit_codex.py:1385-1388`).\\n\\n## Key constants\\n- `DEFAULT_PROOF_MIN_REPETITIONS = 4` (`audit_codex.py:39`) — the n>=4 floor.\\n- `MCP_CONDITIONS = {\\\"mcp_semantic\\\",\\\"mcp_history\\\",\\\"mcp_workspace_radar\\\"}` (`audit_codex.py:41`).\\n- `MCP_NAMED_TOOL_REQUIRED_SCOPES` (`audit_codex.py:45-49`).\\n- Effect-size thresholds: efficiency/search win **>=0.20** relative improvement (`run.py:3476-3479`); overhead-bad **>0.15** (`run.py:3485-3486`); significance **p<0.05** (`run.py:3607`, `3988`); saturation score-tolerance **abs(score_delta)<=1** (`run.py:3481`); correctness score gate **score_delta>=3** (`run.py:3473`).\\n\\n## Files NOT part of the gate (verified)\\n- `make_report.py` and `full_report.py`: **zero** `proof_ready` references (grep clean). They build \\\"integrity-clean\\\" master tables by dropping audit-flagged/infra-failure rows (`make_report.py:121`, `full_report.py:4`) but do not compute or read the proof gate.\\n\\n## Uncertainties\\n- `audit_radar_evidence.py:363-370` defines a **second, stricter** `proof_ready` for the Radar track (adds `repeated and brain_clean and baseline_headroom` on top of `reported_proof_ready`). This is Radar-specific and separate from the main run.py/audit_codex gate; I did not find it referenced by the four files you named, so I treat it as adjacent rather than the core gate. The `baseline_headroom` requirement there is the notable extra condition.\\n- No numeric CV threshold gates the `noisy` tag — CV is reported but not a cutoff. If a CV threshold is expected, it is not implemented in `run.py`.\"\n9\t    },\n10\t    {\n11\t      \"subsystem\": \"task-schema\",\n12\t      \"map\": \"I now have a complete, citeable map. Here is the deliverable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Task Schema + What \\\"Clean\\\" Means — Subsystem Map\\n\\nAll paths relative to `/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain`. The harness is `benchmarks/agent-brain/run.py`. Tasks are `benchmarks/agent-brain/tasks/*.json` (54 files). `run_test.py` is the test suite, not the loader — the loader/validator/scorer all live in `run.py`.\\n\\n## 1. Task JSON schema — every field\\n\\nThere is **no struct/dataclass**; tasks are loaded as raw dicts (`run.py:493-509`) and fields are read defensively via `task.get(...)` throughout. The schema is therefore *implicit* in the readers. Below is the full field set, derived from a sweep of all 54 files plus the reader call-sites.\\n\\n```python\\ndef load_tasks(patterns: list[str]) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:\\n    ...\\n    with path.open() as f:\\n        task = json.load(f)\\n    task[\\\"_path\\\"] = str(path)\\n    tasks.append(task)\\n```\\n(`run.py:493-509`)\\n\\n**Field-by-field** (count = how many of 54 tasks use it):\\n\\n| Field | In N tasks | Type | Meaning / read site |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| `id` | 54 | str | Run/dir identity; brief query prefix. `run.py:1715`, `run_one` at `:3241` |\\n| `repo` | 54 | str | Display name in records (`:3248`) |\\n| `repo_path` | 54 | str | Source repo, resolved portably (`~`, `$VARS`, relative→`$AGENT_BENCH_REPO_ROOT`). `resolve_repo_path` `run.py:512-529` |\\n| `prompt` | 54 | str | The issue/task text the agent sees. Base of both `prompt_for` (`:2334`) and `brain_brief_query` (`:1713`) |\\n| `conditions` | 54 | list[str] | Arms to run. Always includes `no_brain`; e.g. `[\\\"no_brain\\\",\\\"semantic_brain\\\"]`. Combos enumerated below |\\n| `validation` | 54 | list[str] | Shell commands; all must exit 0 to pass. `validate()` `run.py:3065-3092` |\\n| `expected_files` | 54 | list[str] | Files the fix *should* touch; drives `patch_focus` (`:3103`,`:3115-3140`). Never empty in current corpus |\\n| `forbidden_files` | 54 | list[str] | Files that must NOT be touched (always `go.mod`,`go.sum`); penalize `patch_focus` (`:3104`,`:3127`) |\\n| `brain_queries` | 54 | list[str] | Retrieval hints injected into brain arms **only** (the B1 confound vector). See §2 |\\n| `setup_replacements` | 51 | list[{path,old,new}] | The regression patch: exact `old`→`new` string swap applied+committed before the agent. `apply_replacements` `run.py:839` |\\n| `hide_expected_from_agent` | 51 | bool | Withhold \\\"Expected edit area\\\" from the prompt (`:2429-2430`) |\\n| `hide_validation_from_agent` | 51 | bool | Withhold validation commands from the prompt; replaced with generic \\\"run the tests you identify\\\" (`:2431-2434`) |\\n| `leak_markers` | 24 | list[str] | High-entropy canaries that must never appear in agent output. `hidden_validation_markers` `run.py:1119-1137` |\\n| `base_commit` | 19 | str | Pinned source commit for reproducibility; recorded separately in provenance (README:88-92) |\\n| `prepare_semantic` | 17 | bool | If false, semantic indexing disabled (large-repo path); changes policy text (`:2363-2373`) |\\n| `copy_checkpoint_ref_from_source` | 16 | bool | Provides local Entire history for history/full-brain conditions (required by preflight `:4396-4401`) |\\n| `setup_commands` | 14 | list[str] | Extra shell mutations during setup (e.g. scrub README hints). Get `$BENCH_SOURCE_REPO` (README:49) |\\n| `agent_hidden_paths` | 14 | list[str] | Dirs removed from worktree before agent runs (e.g. `[\\\"benchmarks/agent-brain\\\"]`) — required on release panels (`:4377-4381`) |\\n| `suite_tags` | 13 | list[str] | SWE-style tagging metadata |\\n| `source` | 13 | str | Provenance label: `swe-bench-style-local` (7) / `swe-style` (6) |\\n| `history_excerpt_lines` | 10 | int | Cap on generated history excerpt (`write_history_excerpt` `:2012`) |\\n| `archetype` | 10 | str | Difficulty/kind metadata (see §3) |\\n| `validation_files` | 5 | list[{path,fixture}] | Hidden test files materialized from `validation-fixtures/` only at validation time. `materialize_validation_files` (`:3073`) |\\n| `radar_include_deletions` | 4 | bool | Radar arm must also flag deleted assignments; toggles `include_deletions:true` requirement (`:1389`,`:2342`) |\\n| `path_prefix` | 4 | str | `\\\"auto\\\"`→dir of host `node` (README:47-48) |\\n| `copy_entire_history_from_source` | 4 | bool | Alternative local-history source (preflight `:4397`) |\\n| `post_brain_replacements` | 2 | list[{path,old,new}] | Stale-context mutation: applied+committed AFTER brain prep, so brain context is stale (README:60-63) |\\n| `workspace_name` | 1 | str | Workspace-radar scenario name (`benchmark_workspace_name`, used `:2375`) |\\n\\nOther fields read by code but absent from current corpus: `post_brain_commands`, `history_exclude_path_terms`, `history_include_raw_snippets`, `history_excerpt` (`:2008-2037`,`:2422`).\\n\\n**Three representative tasks read in full:** `tasks/entire-brain-history-bundle-sha256.json` (full-brain, `setup_commands` scrubbing README hints, `hide_*`), `tasks/github-cli-auth-env-token.json` (semantic, `archetype:rationale-recovery`, `base_commit`-pinned), `tasks/swe-style-github-cli-auth-env-token.json` (SWE-style twin with `source`+`suite_tags`). Also `tasks/entireio-cli-radar-manual-attribution-deletions.json` (radar + `validation_files` + `leak_markers`) and `tasks/entire-brain-stale-query-default-limit.json` (`post_brain_replacements` stale-context).\\n\\n## 2. \\\"Clean\\\" vs \\\"leaky/confounded\\\" — the B1 query-hint confound\\n\\n**The leak vector is `brain_queries`.** `prompt_for` injects them as `Useful query terms: {queries}` into **every brain/MCP arm but never into `no_brain`** (`run.py:2337`, used in the policy strings at `:2367,:2371,:2376,:2380,:2385,:2393,:2415-2421`). So if a query string also contains the hidden answer, the comparison measures *(answer-hint + brain)* vs *(no hint, no brain)* — not brain value. This is exactly the B1 finding:\\n\\n> \\\"`benchmarks/agent-brain/run.py` injects `brain_queries` into brain/MCP-arm prompts only; the `no_brain` arm receives none. … several `brain_queries` contained answer-bearing identifiers, hidden-test names, or near-verbatim hidden-validation strings.\\\" (`docs/release-blockers.md:18-21`)\\n\\n**The detector: `brain_query_leak_audit` (`run.py:1240-1299`).** It compares each query against the **hidden answer texts** assembled by `brain_query_answer_texts` (`run.py:1156-1193`): the `setup_replacements`/`post_brain_replacements` `old`/`new`/`path` (always hidden), `setup_commands`, and — *only when the task hides them* — `expected_files` (if `hide_expected_from_agent`) and validation commands / `-run` test names / `rg '...'` expected strings / `validation_files` fixture contents (if `hide_validation_from_agent`). Visible validation reaches both arms, so it is deliberately not counted as asymmetry (`:1157-1161`).\\n\\nThree flag kinds (`:1261-1297`):\\n\\n```python\\nfor token in words:\\n    if brain_query_token_is_identifier(token) and token.lower() in folded_text:\\n        record(query_text, token, kind, \\\"answer_bearing_identifier\\\")\\n    elif len(token) >= 6 and token.lower() in identifier_folds:\\n        record(query_text, token, kind, \\\"answer_bearing_identifier\\\")\\n```\\n- (1) a **code-shaped identifier** token appearing case-insensitively in hidden text;\\n- (2) a 3-4 word run whose casefolded concatenation **equals** a hidden identifier (catches split/lowercased identifiers, `:1276-1282`);\\n- (3) any contiguous **3+ word phrase** found in hidden text, reported as the maximal phrase (`:1283-1297`).\\n\\n\\\"Identifier-shaped\\\" is defined narrowly so plain English symptom words pass:\\n```python\\ndef brain_query_token_is_identifier(token: str) -> bool:\\n    if len(token) < 4: return False\\n    if token.startswith(\\\"--\\\") or \\\"_\\\" in token or \\\".\\\" in token or \\\"/\\\" in token: return True\\n    return any(ch.isupper() for ch in token[1:])\\n```\\n(`run.py:1196-1204`)\\n\\n**Enforcement (hard gate):** `validate_panel` runs the audit on every task and refuses to run the panel on any finding:\\n```python\\nquery_audit = brain_query_leak_audit(task)\\nif not query_audit[\\\"ok\\\"]:\\n    for finding in query_audit[\\\"findings\\\"]:\\n        ...\\n        f\\\"task {...} brain_queries hand the brain arm answer-bearing content: {leaked!r} \\\"\\n        f\\\"appears in {finding['where']} (release blocker B1 — strip it or give both arms identical hints)\\\"\\n```\\n(`run.py:4387-4395`). On **release** panels it additionally requires `agent_hidden_paths` to hide `benchmarks/agent-brain` and requires `leak_markers` whenever validation is hidden (`run.py:4376-4386`).\\n\\nA **clean** task therefore has: `brain_queries` written at symptom level (e.g. `tasks/entireio-cli-radar-manual-attribution-deletions.json:48` — \\\"session state keeps old base commit\\\"), no identifier/phrase overlap with hidden fix/validation, hidden validation backed by an explicit `leak_markers` canary, and `benchmarks/agent-brain` hidden. A **leaky** task is one whose queries name the fixed symbol, the hidden test (`-run`), or quote the fix verbatim.\\n\\n**Disclosed residual / known limits (`docs/release-blockers.md:55-59`, `run.py:1272-1275`):** the literal \\\"any token\\\" rule from the review is intentionally *not* implemented (would false-flag symptom text); enforced rule = code-shaped identifiers + verbatim 3+-word phrases. **Two-word** runs are deliberately exempt (natural compounds like \\\"base commit\\\"), and 1-2 word plain-English overlaps are not flagged. Note `tasks/entire-brain-stale-query-default-limit.json:26` lists `brain_queries: [..., \\\"newQueryCommand\\\", \\\"newRetrieveCommand\\\", \\\"TestQMDAliasesAcrossRetrievalVerbs\\\"]` — those are identifier-shaped and the test name is in `validation` — but that task does **not** set `hide_validation_from_agent`, so the validation/test-name texts are *not* added to the hidden set (`:1172`) and the audit does not flag them. That is the auditor working as designed (visible validation is symmetric), but it means identifier-shaped queries are only safe because that task shows its validation to both arms. (I flag this as a subtle, easy-to-misread case.)\\n\\nA second, separate leak surface is **agent-output leakage**: `agent_output_leak_audit` (`run.py:1140-1153`) hashes any `leak_markers`/hidden validation marker found in agent stdout/stderr. README:159-164 explains: if `hide_validation_from_agent` and no `leak_markers`, the harness falls back to treating the validation commands themselves as canaries (strict, can false-flag an agent that independently discovers the same focused test).\\n\\n## 3. Easy/\\\"saturated\\\" vs hard/\\\"signal\\\" task\\n\\nThere is **no per-task difficulty number**; difficulty is *measured post-hoc* from the no_brain arm, with two metadata hints:\\n\\n- **`archetype`** (10 tasks): `architecture-localization` (5), `rationale-recovery` (2), `protocol-contract-recovery` (2), `validation-selection` (1). These name the *kind* of signal (e.g. localization tasks reward the brain pointing at the file; rationale-recovery rewards history recalling *why*). It is descriptive, not a scored difficulty knob.\\n- **No-brain pilot screen.** README:140-158: run one `no_brain` rep first; `--stop-after-no-brain-score 90` drops the task. \\\"Retain a scenario for proof only when no-brain is not already above 90, or when the brain condition is demonstrably cheaper/faster at equivalent correctness.\\\" So an **easy/saturated** task = no_brain already solves it (≥90 / both arms 100%); a **hard/signal** task = no_brain fails or is markedly slower.\\n\\nTwo code definitions of \\\"saturated\\\":\\n\\n```python\\nboth_solved = success_rate_condition == 1.0 and success_rate_baseline == 1.0\\nsaturated = both_solved and abs(score_delta) <= 1 and not efficiency_win\\n```\\n(`run.py:3480-3481`; verdict `saturated/no_signal` or `saturated/overhead_negative` at `:3507-3510`). A task only yields **signal** if the brain arm produces a `correctness_win` (brain passes 100% AND success/score lift, `:3473`) or `efficiency_win` (≥20% time/token improvement at equal-or-better correctness, `:3475-3478`).\\n\\nThe **stability gate** (`comparison_stability` `run.py:3582-3649`, README:21-34) is the harder bar. It can only **downgrade**:\\n```python\\nsaturated = pass_cond == 1.0 and pass_base == 1.0\\nif token_win_stable or pass_lift_stable:\\n    tag = \\\"brain_positive_stable\\\"\\nelif saturated:\\n    tag = \\\"saturated\\\"\\nelse:\\n    tag = \\\"noisy\\\"\\n```\\n`brain_positive_stable` requires a token reduction significant at p<0.05 (using the **more conservative** of raw Welch and Holm-adjusted p) *and* drop-one survival, **or** a pass-rate lift surviving drop-one (`:3592-3626`). `proof_ready` is finally gated to `candidate_proof_ready AND tag == \\\"brain_positive_stable\\\"` (`run.py:3873-3874`), needing ≥4 reps/side (`:3471`). Per `release-blockers.md:43-47`, the clean B1 reruns came out exactly here: audit-clean but **noisy** (50%→75% pass, `proof_ready=false`) or **saturated/negative** (both 100%) — i.e. clean but no proof-ready lift.\\n\\n## 4. With-brain vs without-brain expectation, success criteria, grader\\n\\n**Declaring arms:** `conditions` lists the arms; `no_brain` is the baseline every comparison is computed against. Current combos (count): `no_brain,semantic_brain` (15); `no_brain,full_brain` (14); `no_brain,semantic_brain,full_brain` (12); `no_brain,full_cli_compact,mcp_history` (7); `no_brain,mcp_history` (3); plus single multi-arm and workspace-radar tasks. The *expectation difference between arms is entirely in the policy text* `prompt_for` generates per condition (`run.py:2333-2436`): `no_brain` is told \\\"Do not use Entire Brain … inspect the repository normally\\\" (`:2364-2365`); brain arms get the `entire brain brief …` command + `Useful query terms: {queries}` + tool discipline. The task's *correct answer is identical across arms* — only the context-acquisition path differs.\\n\\n**Success criteria (primary):** every `validation` command must exit 0. `validate()` runs each, `ok = all returncode==0` (`run.py:3065-3092`). Hidden test files come from `validation_files` (`{path, fixture}` materialized from `validation-fixtures/` only during validation, then cleaned up — `:3073`,`:3091`).\\n\\n**Grader: `score()` (`run.py:3095-3227`), `score.version = 2`, 0-100, five components** (README:217-225 stresses pass/fail is primary; the score is a composite, not a pure-quality metric, and v1≠v2):\\n- `outcome` (0-45): `round(35 * validation_ratio)` + 10 if agent exit 0 & all validations pass, else +5 (`:3111-3113`).\\n- `patch_focus` (0-30): `expected_files` coverage + minimality − unexpected/`forbidden_files` touches − missing-expected penalty + diff-size bonus (`:3115-3140`).\\n- `validation_discipline` (0-10): +6 ran tests, +2 checked diff, +2 validation ok (`:3143-3149`).\\n- `runtime_efficiency` (0-10): time buckets + token buckets (`:3151-3179`).\\n- `brain_use` (0-5): for `no_brain`, 5 iff it did *not* touch the brain; for brain arms, points for actually using brain / checking freshness / brief / not-locked (`:3181-3194`).\\n\\nFor MCP conditions a **separate** `mcp_condition_audit` (`run.py:1400-1439`) enforces that the required tools were actually called (`brain_brief`/`brain_search`/`brain_status`/`brain_regressions`/`brain_workspace_regressions` per condition, `:1302-1326`) and that no direct `entire brain` CLI was used — a correct-but-bypassed run is flagged. The headline metric reported by `report`/`summarize` stays **validation pass-rate + measured tokens**, with the stability tag from §3 deciding whether a win is citable.\\n\\n**Uncertainty notes:** (a) the schema is implicit (dict + `.get`), so a typo'd field is silently ignored rather than rejected — there is no JSON-schema validator. (b) The `entire-brain-stale-query-default-limit.json` identifier-bearing queries are safe only because that task does not hide its validation; I'd treat it as a borderline case worth a human glance if its `conditions` ever add hidden validation. (c) `run_test.py` (271KB) is the test harness; I did not exhaustively read it — the loader/validator/scorer authoritative source is `run.py` as cited.\"\n13\t    },\n14\t    {\n15\t      \"subsystem\": \"harness-run\",\n16\t      \"map\": \"I have all the evidence needed. Here is the deliverable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Agent-Lift Harness Map: How Runs Execute + What's Runnable Locally\\n\\n**Bottom line:** A real with-brain vs without-brain agent-lift run **cannot** be done without hosted-model access. There is **no mock/offline/dry/deterministic agent mode** anywhere in `run.py`. Every scored run shells out to the real `claude` or `codex` CLI, which authenticate against host account state. The only thing runnable *without* model credentials is the `check` subcommand (validates that tasks fail-as-expected — no agent invoked) and brain-prep (`prep`), which are deterministic/local.\\n\\nAll paths below are under `/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain/benchmarks/agent-brain/`.\\n\\n> Note on git status: the session snapshot showed these files as \\\"deleted\\\", but they are present in the working tree (`ls` confirms). I read live content.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Entry points & invocation\\n\\n**Shell drivers** (`run_cli_matrix.sh`, `run_cli_parallel.sh`, `run_opus_matrix.sh`) are thin wrappers that all converge on one command: `python3 run.py run`. They iterate a hardcoded runner matrix × 2 CLI tasks, skip suites already at ≥9 clean records (idempotent), and call e.g. (`run_cli_matrix.sh:55-64`):\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 run.py run \\\\\\n  --tasks \\\"${task}*\\\" --runners \\\"$runner\\\" \\\\\\n  --conditions \\\"$CONDS\\\" --repetitions \\\"$REPS\\\" \\\\\\n  --checkpoint-limit \\\"$CKPT\\\" --claude-budget \\\"$CBUDGET\\\" \\\\\\n  --pricing-file pricing.json --suite-name \\\"$sn\\\"\\n```\\nTheir shared config: `CONDS=\\\"no_brain,full_cli_compact,mcp_history\\\"`, `REPS=3`, `CKPT=200`, `CBUDGET=25`, `EXPECTED=9` (3 conditions × 3 reps). `run_cli_parallel.sh` adds a `maxjobs` semaphore (`run_cli_parallel.sh:69-76`); `run_opus_matrix.sh` pins `claude:opus:<effort>` only.\\n\\n**argparse** lives in `main()` at `run.py:4602`, with subcommands `run / panel / prep / report / check / discover` (`run.py:4604-4674`). The `run` subcommand args (`run.py:4606-4631`): `--tasks`, `--agents` (default `codex`), `--runners` (\\\"agent[:model[:effort]]\\\", overrides `--agents`), `--conditions` (default `no_brain,semantic_brain,full_brain`), `--repetitions`, `--timeout` (default 1800s), `--agent-retries` (default 2), `--claude-budget`, `--pricing-file/--pricing-json`, `--checkpoint-limit` (200), `--suite-name`, brain-cache flags. The committed reproducible config is the **panel** subcommand: `python3 run.py panel full` runs `panels/full.json` (`README.md:11-18`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. How an agent is actually driven (the invocation)\\n\\nReal CLI shell-outs, built in `run_agent()` (`run.py:2518`). There is **no** HTTP API call, no SDK, and no fake agent. `run_cmd` (`run.py:445-469`) is a plain `subprocess.run`.\\n\\n**Codex** (`run.py:2535-2554`):\\n```python\\ncmd = [\\\"codex\\\", \\\"exec\\\", \\\"--ephemeral\\\", \\\"--ignore-user-config\\\",\\n       \\\"--ignore-rules\\\", \\\"--sandbox\\\", \\\"workspace-write\\\", \\\"--json\\\",\\n       \\\"--cd\\\", str(worktree)]\\nif mcp_enabled: cmd.extend(codex_mcp_config_args(tools, env))\\nif runner.model: cmd.extend([\\\"--model\\\", runner.model])\\nif runner.effort: cmd.extend([\\\"--config\\\", f'model_reasoning_effort=\\\"{runner.effort}\\\"'])\\ncmd.append(prompt)\\n```\\n\\n**Claude** (`run.py:2555-2577`):\\n```python\\ncmd = [\\\"claude\\\", \\\"--print\\\", \\\"--no-session-persistence\\\", \\\"--strict-mcp-config\\\",\\n       \\\"--mcp-config\\\", claude_mcp_config(tools, env) if mcp_enabled else '{\\\"mcpServers\\\":{}}',\\n       \\\"--disable-slash-commands\\\", \\\"--permission-mode\\\", \\\"bypassPermissions\\\",\\n       \\\"--output-format\\\", \\\"stream-json\\\" if mcp_enabled else \\\"json\\\"]\\nif runner.model: cmd.extend([\\\"--model\\\", runner.model])\\nif runner.effort: cmd.extend([\\\"--effort\\\", runner.effort])\\n...\\nif claude_budget > 0: cmd[1:1] = [\\\"--max-budget-usd\\\", str(claude_budget)]\\ncmd.append(prompt)\\n```\\nThe unknown-agent branch raises (`run.py:2578-2579`): `raise ValueError(f\\\"unknown agent: {runner.agent}\\\")` — i.e. only `codex`/`claude` exist; no `local`/`mock`/`offline` fallback.\\n\\n**Searches for an offline mode came up empty.** A targeted grep for `local.?command.?agent|local_agent|mock_agent|fake_agent|--dry|dry_run|offline_mode|DETERMINISTIC|stub` over `run.py` returned **zero hits**. The string \\\"local-command-agent.py\\\" exists only as a *deleted evidence artifact* (`evidence/distill-perf/local-command-agent.py` in the git-status deletion list) and is **not referenced** by `run.py` or `run_test.py` — so it is not a runnable agent mode in this harness. (I did not inspect that file's contents; it appears to be a one-off proof fixture, not part of the run path.)\\n\\n**The one offline path:** `cmd_check` (`run.py:4563-4599`) builds tools, creates a worktree, runs `prepare_brain(..., \\\"no_brain\\\", ...)`, applies setup, and calls `validate()` — then prints `fails-as-expected` / `unexpected-pass`. **It never calls `run_agent`.** This verifies task fixtures locally with no model access.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. EXACTLY what a real run needs (external access)\\n\\n**Binaries on PATH:**\\n- `claude` and/or `codex` CLI (hard-required for any scored run; the only two agents).\\n- `go` — `build_tools` (`run.py:539-544`) runs `go build -o .../entire-brain ./cmd/entire-brain` (in repo `ROOT`) and `go build -o .../entire-sem ./cmd/entire-sem` (in the **sibling** `../entire-sem` dir, `ROOT.parent / \\\"entire-sem\\\"`). Both `check=True` — a missing `entire-sem` sibling repo breaks the build.\\n- `git` (worktree creation, `git rev-parse`, etc.).\\n- `node` — `apply_task_env` resolves `path_prefix: \\\"auto\\\"` to `dirname(which(\\\"node\\\"))` for tsx-based validations (`run.py:1521-1526`).\\n- `jq` — used by the shell drivers' idempotency check (not by `run.py`).\\n\\n**Auth / credentials (the blocker):** No `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`/`OPENAI_API_KEY` env var is read by `run.py` (grep found none). Instead auth comes from **host account state**, made explicit in the `ISOLATION` table (`run.py:39-54`):\\n```python\\n\\\"codex\\\":  {... \\\"auth\\\": \\\"host CODEX_HOME auth may still be used\\\"},\\n\\\"claude\\\": {... \\\"auth\\\": \\\"host Claude Max/OAuth auth may still be used\\\",\\n                \\\"settings\\\": \\\"default non-bare settings required for Max auth\\\"},\\n```\\nREADME corroborates (`README.md:189-192`): \\\"Authentication still comes from the host account state (`CODEX_HOME` for Codex and the configured Claude Max/OAuth state for Claude)\\\". **So you need a logged-in `claude` (Max/OAuth) and/or a `CODEX_HOME` with valid Codex auth.** Without one of these, no with-brain vs without-brain run produces a record.\\n\\n**Network:** The agent CLIs reach their hosted models (network required). The brain itself is local stdio — `mcp__entire_brain` runs as `entire-brain mcp` over stdio (`run.py:2454-2467`, isolation note \\\"entire-brain local stdio only\\\" at `run.py:2623`), and `mcp_server_env` (`run.py:2439-2452`) passes only `ENTIRE_*` plus `PATH` — so the brain side is no-egress, but the model side is not.\\n\\n**Source repos:** Tasks reference sibling repos via portable `repo_path` resolved against `$AGENT_BENCH_REPO_ROOT` (default = parent of this repo) (`README.md:36-49`). The CLI tasks need `entireio/cli` checked out as a sibling; the Ultron **session-history** scenarios additionally need machine-local `.entire` checkpoint data that is **not committed** (`README.md:51-54`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. with-brain vs without-brain setup\\n\\nConditions are sets defined at `run.py:263-275`:\\n- `no_brain` — baseline.\\n- CLI-delivery brain: `semantic_brain/semantic_cli`, `full_brain`, `full_cli_original`, `full_cli_compact`.\\n- MCP-delivery brain: `mcp_semantic`, `mcp_history`, `mcp_workspace_radar` (`MCP_CONDITIONS`, gated by `is_mcp_condition`, `run.py:306-307`).\\n\\n**no_brain** short-circuits in `prepare_brain` (`run.py:1636-1637`): `if condition == \\\"no_brain\\\": return env, prep` — no brain artifacts, no MCP, no context injection.\\n\\n**Brain conditions** build the brain via `brain_prep_commands` (`run.py:1609-1621`), which run the real `entire-brain` binary:\\n```python\\ncommands = [[tools[\\\"brain\\\"], \\\"refresh\\\", \\\"seed\\\", worktree, \\\"--agent\\\", \\\"none\\\", \\\"--force\\\"]]\\nif task.get(\\\"prepare_semantic\\\", True):\\n    commands.append([tools[\\\"brain\\\"], \\\"refresh\\\", \\\"index\\\", worktree, \\\"--sem-binary\\\", tools[\\\"entire\\\"], \\\"--force\\\"])\\nif condition_prepares_history(condition):\\n    commands.insert(0, [tools[\\\"brain\\\"], \\\"refresh\\\", \\\"sessions\\\", \\\"--checkpoint-limit\\\", str(checkpoint_limit), \\\"--history-index\\\"])\\n```\\nArtifacts are cached under `cache/` by repo/base/condition/tool hash and copied into the worktree's ignored `.benchmark/plugin/` (`run.py:1639-1700`; `README.md:65-72`). The plugin dir is injected via env in `plugin_env` (`run.py:1502-1514`): `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT`, `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_{CONFIG,DATA,STATE,CACHE}_DIR`, and `PATH` prepended with the built `bin/`.\\n\\n**Delivery split:** CLI conditions inject context into the prompt (and for `full_*`, write `.benchmark/brain-history-excerpt.md`); MCP conditions instead register the `entire_brain` stdio MCP server — Claude via `--mcp-config` JSON (`run.py:2454-2467`), Codex via `--config mcp_servers.entire_brain.*` TOML (`run.py:2474-2495`), with `enabled_tools` listing the `brain_*` toolset. The condition-specific prompt is produced by `prompt_for` (`run.py:2333`), with MCP policy text steering exact tool usage (`run.py:2376-2407`). For `no_brain`, the prompt withholds exact file/test names that are supposed to come from history (`README.md:155-158`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Suite size / model-call volume\\n\\nA run record = **1 model call** (the single `claude`/`codex` invocation; `--agent-retries 2` only re-fires on *transient* capacity/rate-limit failures, `run.py:2584-2600` + `TRANSIENT_AGENT_FAILURE_PATTERNS` `run.py:2498-2505`).\\n\\nRun count = runners × tasks × conditions × repetitions. Computed from the committed manifests:\\n- **`panels/full.json`**: 2 runners × 9 tasks × 3 conditions × 4 reps = **216 model calls**.\\n- The shell drivers: e.g. `run_cli_matrix.sh codex` = 8 runners × 2 tasks × 3 conditions × 3 reps = **144 calls**; `claude` = 12 runners × 2 × 3 × 3 = **216 calls**.\\n- The 13 committed `release-*` panels are mostly 1×1×2×4 = **8 calls** each (one is 1×2×2×4=16); summing them ≈ **120 calls**.\\n- 54 task fixtures exist under `tasks/`.\\n\\n**Wall-clock:** Per-call `--timeout` default is 1800s (30 min cap); brain-prep subprocess timeout is 900s (`run.py:1665`). Real agent calls typically run minutes each. So a full panel (216 calls) is on the order of **a few hours** wall-clock even with `run_cli_parallel.sh` concurrency, plus per-suite Go builds and one-time brain-prep (cached after first build). I cannot cite a measured total runtime from the code — that is an estimate from the timeout/call-count, not a recorded benchmark number.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Uncertainties / caveats\\n- I did not read the deleted `evidence/distill-perf/local-command-agent.py`; based on its location and zero references from `run.py`/`run_test.py`, it is a proof fixture, not a runnable agent mode in this harness — but I can't 100% rule out that some other harness used it.\\n- `run_test.py` (271KB) is the test suite, not a runner; I did not exhaustively read it, but its name and the argparse structure confirm `run.py` is the sole runner. The grep for offline-agent terms covered `run.py` only.\\n- Exact full-suite wall-clock is not stored as an evidence number anywhere I read; treat the \\\"few hours\\\" as derived, not measured.\"\n17\t    },\n18\t    {\n19\t      \"subsystem\": \"why-0of3\",\n20\t      \"map\": \"I have all the evidence needed. Here is the deliverable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Why the current \\\"clean\\\" suites score 0/3 proof-ready\\n\\n## TL;DR\\nThere are exactly **three \\\"clean\\\" (post-B1) agent-lift suites in the release lane**, each at **n=4/side**, all `proof_ready=false`. One fails for **noise** (real positive direction, n=4 too small, Holm-blocked, drop-one fails); the other two fail for **saturation + negative** (both arms already 100% pass, so the only movable axis — mean score — went *down*). The release manifest is in `no_release_claim` mode and the readiness audit records the lane as having **\\\"0 proof-ready comparisons\\\"** (`docs/release_readiness_audit.md:283-284`). The \\\"0/3\\\" is structural: every retained release comparison hits a different wall of the proof-ready gate.\\n\\nThe contrast that proves the fix is in a *separate* lane (`semantic-clean-proof/`), where the same task that was `noisy` at n=4 flipped to `proof_ready=true` once n hit 16 — that is the load-bearing evidence for the synthesis below.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Enumeration of the \\\"clean\\\" agent-lift suites\\n\\nThe three retained release-lane suites (manifest minimum = 3 suites / 24 records, `benchmarks/agent-brain/evidence/release/manifest.json:7-15`):\\n\\n| # | Suite (evidence path under `benchmarks/agent-brain/evidence/release/`) | Delivery scope | Condition vs baseline | n/side |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | `release-candidate-entire-brain-schema-contract-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json` | `full_brain` (scope: `history`) | `full_brain` vs `no_brain` | 4 |\\n| 2 | `release-candidate-entire-cli-mcp-manual-attribution-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json` | `mcp` | `mcp_history` vs `no_brain` | 4 |\\n| 3 | `release-candidate-cli-radar-mcp-del-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json` | `mcp_radar_location_only` | `mcp_history` vs `no_brain` | 4 |\\n\\n(Codex agent, `codex` field, all three.) These are the only suites the manifest treats as release-citable (`release_citable_suite_globs: [\\\"release-candidate-*\\\"]`, `manifest.json:4-6`).\\n\\nFor context, two **other clean lanes exist but are intentionally NOT in the release lane** (`README.md:5-7` says they are \\\"deliberately separate\\\"):\\n- `evidence/semantic-clean-proof/` — Layer-B GitHub-CLI task + history negatives.\\n- `evidence/history-delivery-fix/` — the history net-harm fix (efficiency-at-parity).\\n\\nThese two are discussed in §3 because they are where the proof-ready gate is actually *cleared* (and where it still fails for a different reason), which is what tells you the property new release tasks need.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Per-suite measured numbers and the EXACT gate reason\\n\\n### Suite 1 — schema-contract (`full_brain`) → **NOISY**\\nFile: `release-candidate-entire-brain-schema-contract-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json`\\n\\n- pass rate: `0.5 → 0.75` (`pass_rate_baseline` L62, `pass_rate_condition` L63)\\n- mean score: `86.5 → 87.75` (`mean_baseline` L24, `mean_condition` L27); `delta: 1.25` (L19)\\n- core score: `75.0 → 79.0` (`delta_score_core: 4.0`)\\n- tokens: `568,177 → 855,568` (i.e. brain used *more* tokens, `token_improvement_ratio: -0.506`)\\n- stability block (L78-82): `\\\"tag\\\": \\\"noisy\\\"`, `\\\"saturated\\\": false`, `\\\"token_win_survives_drop_one\\\": false`, `\\\"tokens_p_raw\\\": 0.2174`, `\\\"tokens_p_holm\\\": 0.8697`, `tokens_significant_p_lt_0_05: false`\\n- `\\\"verdict\\\": \\\"brain_negative\\\"` (L88), `\\\"proof_ready\\\": false` (L64)\\n\\n**Gate reason = NOISY.** Pass-rate moved in the right direction (50→75%) and there is headroom (not saturated), but at n=4 nothing is significant: token p-raw 0.22 (and tokens went the *wrong* way anyway), no metric survives Holm, drop-one fails. Audit verdict text: *\\\"audit-clean but noisy: pass rate moved from 50% to 75%, mean score from 86.5 to 87.75, and `proof_ready=false`\\\"* (`release/README.md:55-58`; same line in `docs/release-blockers.md:45`).\\n\\n### Suite 2 — MCP manual-attribution (`mcp`) → **SATURATED + NEGATIVE**\\nFile: `release-candidate-entire-cli-mcp-manual-attribution-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json`\\n\\n- pass rate: `1.0 → 1.0` (L62-63) — both arms 4/4\\n- mean score: `94.25 → 91.25` (L24/L27); `delta: -3.0` (L19); core `84.0 → 81.0`\\n- stability block (L78-79): `\\\"saturated\\\": true`, `\\\"tag\\\": \\\"saturated\\\"`, drop-one false, tokens p-raw 0.203 / Holm 0.811\\n- `\\\"verdict\\\": \\\"brain_negative\\\"` (L88), `\\\"proof_ready\\\": false` (L64)\\n\\n**Gate reason = SATURATED (and NEGATIVE on the score axis).** Both arms already pass 100%, so pass-rate has nowhere to climb; the only remaining quality signal (mean score) dropped. Audit verdict: *\\\"it saturated: both arms passed 4/4 and the condition mean score was lower (`94.25 -> 91.25`, `proof_ready=false`). It is not citable as MCP correctness/pass-rate, named-tool MCP, or efficiency proof\\\"* (`docs/release_readiness_audit.md:546-550`).\\n\\n### Suite 3 — Radar deletion-attribution (`mcp_radar_location_only`) → **SATURATED + NEGATIVE**\\nFile: `release-candidate-cli-radar-mcp-del-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json`\\n\\n- pass rate: `1.0 → 1.0` (L64-65) — both arms 4/4\\n- mean score: `94.5 → 90.5` (L26/L29); `delta: -4.0` (L19); core `82.5 → 79.0`\\n- stability block (L80-81): `\\\"saturated\\\": true`, `\\\"tag\\\": \\\"saturated\\\"`, drop-one false, tokens p-raw 0.238 / Holm 0.953\\n- `\\\"verdict\\\": \\\"brain_negative\\\"` (L90), `\\\"proof_ready\\\": false` (L66)\\n\\n**Gate reason = SATURATED (and NEGATIVE on the score axis).** Identical failure mode to Suite 2. Audit verdict: *\\\"both arms passed 4/4 and the comparison was saturated/brain-negative (`proof_ready=false`). It is not citable as focused location-only deletion Radar… until a future clean task creates baseline headroom and passes the proof-ready gate\\\"* (`docs/release_readiness_audit.md:531-535`).\\n\\n### The \\\"0/3\\\" verdict in the readiness audit\\n> *\\\"the audit has 24/24 provenance-backed records, 0 hard integrity flags, 8 MCP datapoints, 4 named-tool MCP datapoints, and **0 proof-ready comparisons**. The lane therefore remains in `claim_policy: \\\"no_release_claim\\\"` mode because the clean reruns did not produce a stable positive replay-lab lift, not because of a remaining integrity confound.\\\"* — `docs/release_readiness_audit.md:282-287`\\n\\nSame count in `docs/release-blockers.md:39-41` (\\\"3 suites, 24 records… and 0 proof-ready comparisons\\\"). The manifest encodes the no-claim posture directly: `proof_ready_comparisons: 0`, `require_proof_ready_per_suite: false`, `claim_policy: \\\"no_release_claim\\\"` (`manifest.json:2,9,26`).\\n\\nNote: none of these three fail for **\\\"confounded\\\" (query-hint)** — that was the *old* B1 failure, now resolved by the `brain_query_leak_audit` and the rewrite to symptom-level queries (`release-blockers.md:22-34`). The clean reruns replaced the confounded suites; the remaining failures are statistical/headroom failures, not integrity failures.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Synthesis — what property a new task must have to flip a suite to `proof_ready`\\n\\nThe three failures map to exactly two missing properties, and the *adjacent clean lanes* show concretely what fixing each looks like.\\n\\n**Property A — A LOW-ENOUGH BASELINE TO ESCAPE SATURATION (the binding constraint for 2 of 3).**\\nSuites 2 and 3 are dead on arrival because `no_brain` already scores 4/4 / ~94 mean. When the baseline is at the ceiling, the only metric left to move is mean score, and it moved *down* — there is no possible positive quality signal. A new MCP/Radar task must be **hard enough that the no-brain arm genuinely fails a meaningful fraction** (i.e. a sub-100% baseline pass-rate), creating headroom the brain can fill. This is exactly the audit's own prescription: *\\\"until a future clean task creates baseline headroom and passes the proof-ready gate\\\"* (`release_readiness_audit.md:535`) and *\\\"It is not citable… unless a future runner/task revision creates real baseline headroom\\\"* (`:513`). The semantic-XDG and review-file-count pilots saturated for the same reason (`:289-295`, `:514-524`).\\n\\n**Property B — ENOUGH REPETITIONS (and low CV) TO SURVIVE Holm + drop-one (the binding constraint for Suite 1).**\\nSuite 1 had headroom and the right direction but lost on power: at n=4, p-raw never crossed 0.05, Holm crushed it, and drop-one failed. The `semantic-clean-proof/` lane is the existence proof that this is purely a power problem: the **same task** climbed the n-ladder and only flipped at n=16:\\n\\n- `layer-b-pilot` (n=1): `saturated` (`report.json`: pass 1.0/1.0, mean 87→86)\\n- `layer-b-proof` (n=4): `candidate_proof_ready=true` but `proof_ready=false`, tag `noisy`, tokens p-raw 0.128 / Holm 1.0\\n- `layer-b-proof-fw8` (n=8): still `noisy`, tokens p-raw 0.072 / Holm 0.30 — Holm-blocked\\n- `layer-b-confirm-fw16` (n=16): **`\\\"tag\\\": \\\"brain_positive_stable\\\"`, `\\\"proof_ready\\\": true`**, tokens p-raw 0.0124 / **Holm 0.0495**, `token_win_survives_drop_one: true` (`semantic-clean-proof/layer-b-confirm-fw16-20260611/report.json`; mirrored at `release_readiness_audit.md:247-254`)\\n\\nSo the proof-ready gate (per `release/README.md:13-17`) requires, together: provenance-complete + audit-clean + non-empty validation + **≥4 reps/side** + a **stable** proof-ready comparison. \\\"Stable\\\" means Holm-corrected significance AND drop-one survival — which at the observed CVs needs roughly **n≈16**, not 4.\\n\\n**Property C — measure on an axis that can actually move; pick the verdict axis deliberately.**\\nA subtle trap visible in both `semantic-clean-proof/` and `history-delivery-fix/`: the top-line `verdict` field is **score/success-delta driven**, so a real, stable *efficiency* win still reads `brain_negative` if score is flat (`hist-fix-redaction-n16`: `brain_positive_stable` tag on tokens −44%/Holm 4.6e-05, yet `verdict=brain_negative`, `proof_ready=False` because one rep's record was missing of 16 + negative verdict — `history-delivery-fix/README.md:121-143`). Lesson for a new task: to flip *release* `proof_ready` you want the lift on the **same axis the verdict reads** (correctness/pass-rate), which again means a failing baseline (Property A) so brain can lift pass-rate, not just tokens at parity.\\n\\n### Concrete recipe for a proof-ready release task\\n1. **Lower the baseline below the ceiling.** Design the MCP/Radar/history task so `no_brain` pass-rate sits well under 100% (real headroom) — directly fixes Suites 2 & 3's saturation.\\n2. **Size to n≈16/side, not 4.** The format-web-conflict ladder shows 4 and 8 are Holm-blocked `noisy`; 16 is where drop-one + Holm both pass — fixes Suite 1's noise.\\n3. **Keep prompts clean (symptom-level only).** Preserve the B1 fix — `brain_query_leak_audit` must pass so the win is not a re-introduced query-hint confound (`release-blockers.md:24-27`).\\n4. **Aim the lift at correctness/pass-rate**, since the `verdict`/proof gate keys off score-delta; an efficiency-only win at parity will still read `brain_negative` and stay non-proof-ready (`history-delivery-fix/README.md:104-107,134-143`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Uncertainty / caveats\\n- The three release `summary.json` files report `mean_turns_*: null` and `mean_cost_usd_*: null` for the Codex arms, and the `search_calls`/`used_brain` counters are unreliable for Claude/opus runs (parses Codex protocol-json, not Claude stream-json) — see `history-delivery-fix/README.md:94-99`. So \\\"efficiency\\\" claims rest on `total_tokens` + pass-rate only; I did not treat turn/cost/search counts as load-bearing.\\n- I read the `summary.json`/`report.json` structured fields and the README/audit prose; I did not re-derive the p-values from `records.ndjson`. The numbers quoted are the committed values in those files (line refs given). The narrative magnitudes in the READMEs (e.g. \\\"988,429 → 540,512\\\") are token totals across the 16 reps, whereas the `summary.json` figures I cite are per-run means — both are internally consistent but stated on different bases, so I labeled each.\""}]},"uuid":"bc656ecb-761a-4469-8b27-db7c07f284a2","timestamp":"2026-06-18T17:15:03.008Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-Documents-Coding-entire-brain/4e09703c-666f-441f-9b82-52efaa7da599/tasks/w5fk2l6k1.output","content":"{\n  \"summary\": \"Map the entire-brain proof infrastructure: proof gate, task schema/cleanliness, agent-lift harness runnability, why 0/3, distill perf, eval-ledger gates\",\n  \"agentCount\": 6,\n  \"logs\": [],\n  \"result\": [\n    {\n      \"subsystem\": \"gate\",\n      \"map\": \"I now have the complete picture across all five gate layers. Here is the deliverable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# THE PROOF GATE — `proof_ready` computation map\\n\\nThe benchmark uses a **two-stage proof gate**: (1) per-comparison `proof_ready` is *computed in* `run.py::summarize`, then (2) `audit_codex.py` *re-validates and counts* those flags against evidence (records/provenance/MCP), and (3) `audit_release_matrix.py` enforces the cross-track release policy. `make_report.py` / `full_report.py` are downstream tables and do **not** touch `proof_ready` at all.\\n\\n## 1. The exact predicate that sets `proof_ready` true/false\\n\\n`proof_ready` is set in **two passes** in `run.py`, both required to be true.\\n\\n**Pass A — candidate (verdict-based), `run.py:3520`:**\\n```python\\n\\\"proof_ready\\\": proof_repeated and verdict == \\\"brain_positive\\\",\\n```\\nwhere (`run.py:3471`, `3505-3516`):\\n```python\\nproof_repeated = n_condition >= 4 and n_baseline >= 4\\n...\\nif correctness_win or efficiency_win:\\n    verdict = \\\"brain_positive\\\"\\n...\\nif not proof_repeated and verdict == \\\"brain_positive\\\":\\n    verdict = \\\"pilot_brain_positive\\\"\\n```\\nSo candidate `proof_ready` requires **n>=4 per side** AND a `brain_positive` verdict. `brain_positive` itself requires `correctness_win` OR `efficiency_win` (`run.py:3473-3478`):\\n```python\\nbrain_validation_clean = success_rate_condition == 1.0\\ncorrectness_win = brain_validation_clean and (success_delta > 0 or score_delta >= 3)\\nequal_or_better = success_delta >= 0 and score_delta >= 0\\nefficiency_win = brain_validation_clean and equal_or_better and (\\n    (isinstance(time_improvement, float) and time_improvement >= 0.20)\\n    or (isinstance(token_improvement, float) and token_improvement >= 0.20)\\n)\\n```\\nNote: the brain arm must be **100% pass** (`brain_validation_clean`), and either it strictly out-passes/out-scores the baseline, or (passing equal-or-better) shows a **>=20% relative** time-or-token improvement.\\n\\n**Pass B — stability-gated final, `run.py:3873-3874`** (overwrites the candidate after Holm correction is attached):\\n```python\\ncomp[\\\"candidate_proof_ready\\\"] = bool(comp.get(\\\"proof_ready\\\"))\\ncomp[\\\"proof_ready\\\"] = bool(comp.get(\\\"candidate_proof_ready\\\")\\n                           and comp[\\\"stability\\\"].get(\\\"tag\\\") == \\\"brain_positive_stable\\\")\\n```\\nSo **final `proof_ready` = (candidate proof_ready) AND (stability tag == `brain_positive_stable`).** This is the authoritative per-comparison definition written into each suite's `summary.json`.\\n\\n**Stage 2 — the auditor (`audit_codex.py`) only counts a comparison toward `proof_ready_comparisons` if it ALSO survives evidence checks.** A comparison is dropped (its `pass` set False, or never counted) if any of these flags fire:\\n- `audit_codex.py:986-989` — `G:proof_ready_without_n4` (n<4 per side) or `G:proof_ready_without_stable_gate` (stability tag != `brain_positive_stable`).\\n- `audit_codex.py:1254-1268` — record backing missing: `G:proof_ready_without_matching_records` (and the MCP-specific `G:proof_ready_without_mcp_verified_condition_records`, `..._named_mcp_tool...`, `..._completed_named_mcp_tool...`).\\n- `audit_codex.py:1270` — only counted if `c.get(\\\"proof_ready\\\") and c.get(\\\"pass\\\")`:\\n```python\\nsuite_proofs = sum(1 for c in comp_audits if c.get(\\\"proof_ready\\\") and c.get(\\\"pass\\\"))\\n```\\n\\n**Record backing** (`proof_ready_record_backing`, `audit_codex.py:1008-1092`): for the stored `n_condition`/`n_baseline`, there must exist that many **distinct, passing, provenance-OK records** with matching `run_id` AND `repetition` for both the condition and the `no_brain` baseline (`audit_codex.py:1061-1070`), and for MCP conditions, that many `mcp_verified` (and for named-tool scopes, `mcp_named_tool_verified` + `mcp_named_tool_completed`) records (`1071-1092`).\\n\\n## 2. Operational definitions of the gate vocabulary\\n\\n- **`saturated`** — both arms already pass 100% (no correctness headroom). Two slightly different forms:\\n  - Verdict layer (`run.py:3480-3481`): `both_solved = success_rate_condition == 1.0 and success_rate_baseline == 1.0`; `saturated = both_solved and abs(score_delta) <= 1 and not efficiency_win`.\\n  - Stability layer (`run.py:3628`): `saturated = pass_cond == 1.0 and pass_base == 1.0` — sets tag `\\\"saturated\\\"` when no stable win exists.\\n- **`noisy`** — the stability fallback when there is neither a stable win nor saturation (`run.py:3633-3634`): `else: tag = \\\"noisy\\\"`. There is **no fixed CV threshold** that flips this; CV is *reported* (`run.py:3638-3641`) but the noisy/stable decision is driven by significance + drop-one survival, not a CV cutoff. (Stated uncertainty: I found no numeric CV threshold gating the tag anywhere in `run.py`; CV is descriptive only.)\\n- **`negative`** — `verdict == \\\"brain_negative\\\"` (`run.py:3511-3512`) via `overhead_bad` (`run.py:3482-3487`): `success_delta < 0` OR `score_delta < -1` OR time-overhead `> 0.15` (15% slower) OR token-overhead `> 0.15`. A `brain_negative` (or `saturated`, `inconclusive`, `pilot_brain_positive`) can never be `proof_ready`.\\n- **`no_release_claim`** — a *manifest claim_policy* (`audit_codex.py:1349-1358`, constants at `audit_workspace_radar_evidence.py:21-22`). Under this policy the gate **inverts**: it *requires* `proof_ready_comparisons == 0` and fails if any survive (`audit_codex.py:1354-1355`: `if proof_ready > 0: failures.append(... > 0)`). It is the explicit \\\"we are NOT claiming agent lift yet\\\" lane. `audit_release_matrix.py:131-143` maps it to status `\\\"no-claim\\\"` / `claimable=False`.\\n\\n## 3. The agent-lift statistic and its significance test\\n\\nThe **headline metric is validation pass-rate plus measured total-tokens**, not the composite score (`run.py:3891`): `\\\"headline_metric\\\": \\\"validation pass-rate + measured tokens; composite score and p-values are secondary\\\"`.\\n\\nThe stability gate (`comparison_stability`, `run.py:3582-3649`) accepts a stable brain win via **either**:\\n- **Token win** (`run.py:3613-3617`): tokens significant **AND** lower **AND** survives drop-one. Significance uses the **more conservative of raw Welch p and Holm-adjusted p**, threshold **p < 0.05** (`run.py:3603-3607`):\\n```python\\nraw_p = comparison.get(\\\"p_value_total_tokens\\\")\\nholm_p = comparison.get(\\\"p_value_total_tokens_holm\\\")\\ncandidates = [p for p in (raw_p, holm_p) if isinstance(p, (int, float))]\\neff_p = max(candidates) if candidates else None\\ntokens_significant = eff_p is not None and eff_p < 0.05\\n```\\n- **Pass-rate lift** (`run.py:3621-3626`): `pass_cond > pass_base` AND survives drop-one (no p-test on pass-rate).\\n\\nThe p-value is a **two-sided Welch's t-test** (`run.py:3888`) with **Holm–Bonferroni family-wise correction across all suite p-values** (`run.py:3845-3866`); the `_holm` field is what the gate consumes. The gate \\\"only downgrades; it never manufactures significance\\\" (`run.py:3895`).\\n\\n`delta_survives_drop_one` (`run.py:3563-3579`) is a **one-sided drop-one robustness check** (not full jackknife): it removes the single most brain-favourable rep from each arm and requires the mean delta to still point the right way; returns False below n=2 per arm.\\n\\n## 4. The \\\"retained\\\" / \\\"clean\\\" concept\\n\\nTwo distinct mechanisms:\\n\\n**(a) Retained proof harvesting (`run.py:3913-4004`).** `load_existing_phase2_proofs` walks each suite's `summary.json` but only after `retained_suite_audit_clean` (`run.py:3944-3960`) confirms the cross-referenced `codex-audit-report.json` shows the suite has records, **zero flagged records**, full provenance, and **no flagged comparisons**:\\n```python\\nif int(suite_report.get(\\\"n_flagged_records\\\") or 0) != 0:\\n    return False\\nif int(suite_report.get(\\\"n_provenance_ok\\\") or 0) != records:\\n    return False\\nfor comparison in suite_report.get(\\\"comparisons\\\", []):\\n    if isinstance(comparison, dict) and comparison.get(\\\"flags\\\"):\\n        return False\\n```\\nThen `retained_brain_positive_comparison` (`run.py:3963-4004`) keeps a comparison only if `proof_ready` is true AND `stability.tag == \\\"brain_positive_stable\\\"`, then re-confirms a Holm-significant (`p<0.05`) correctness or metric hit before retaining it as `\\\"proof_level\\\": \\\"existing_repeated_run\\\"`.\\n\\n**(b) \\\"clean\\\" reruns in the release matrix.** `audit_release_matrix.py:140-143` distinguishes the no-claim case: `\\\"B1 clean reruns are retained and audit-clean, but no comparison survived the proof-ready gate\\\"` vs. a still-confounded state. `make_report.py:9` and `full_report.py:4` define \\\"integrity-clean\\\" as suites that cross-reference `codex-audit-report.json` and **drop audit hard-flagged runs and infra failures** (`make_report.py:121`), but neither file reads `proof_ready` — they are presentation tables, not part of the gate.\\n\\n## Scope partitioning (used by `required_*_proof_scopes`)\\n`comparison_proof_scope` (`audit_codex.py:1129-1138`) buckets each proof into `mcp` / `mcp_radar_location_only` / `mcp_radar_answer_assisted` / `mcp_workspace_radar_location_only` / `semantic` / `history` / `other`. The release gate can require >=1 proof in named scopes (`audit_codex.py:1389-1396`), and `require_proof_ready_per_suite` requires every suite to carry at least one proof (`audit_codex.py:1385-1388`).\\n\\n## Key constants\\n- `DEFAULT_PROOF_MIN_REPETITIONS = 4` (`audit_codex.py:39`) — the n>=4 floor.\\n- `MCP_CONDITIONS = {\\\"mcp_semantic\\\",\\\"mcp_history\\\",\\\"mcp_workspace_radar\\\"}` (`audit_codex.py:41`).\\n- `MCP_NAMED_TOOL_REQUIRED_SCOPES` (`audit_codex.py:45-49`).\\n- Effect-size thresholds: efficiency/search win **>=0.20** relative improvement (`run.py:3476-3479`); overhead-bad **>0.15** (`run.py:3485-3486`); significance **p<0.05** (`run.py:3607`, `3988`); saturation score-tolerance **abs(score_delta)<=1** (`run.py:3481`); correctness score gate **score_delta>=3** (`run.py:3473`).\\n\\n## Files NOT part of the gate (verified)\\n- `make_report.py` and `full_report.py`: **zero** `proof_ready` references (grep clean). They build \\\"integrity-clean\\\" master tables by dropping audit-flagged/infra-failure rows (`make_report.py:121`, `full_report.py:4`) but do not compute or read the proof gate.\\n\\n## Uncertainties\\n- `audit_radar_evidence.py:363-370` defines a **second, stricter** `proof_ready` for the Radar track (adds `repeated and brain_clean and baseline_headroom` on top of `reported_proof_ready`). This is Radar-specific and separate from the main run.py/audit_codex gate; I did not find it referenced by the four files you named, so I treat it as adjacent rather than the core gate. The `baseline_headroom` requirement there is the notable extra condition.\\n- No numeric CV threshold gates the `noisy` tag — CV is reported but not a cutoff. If a CV threshold is expected, it is not implemented in `run.py`.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"subsystem\": \"task-schema\",\n      \"map\": \"I now have a complete, citeable map. Here is the deliverable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Task Schema + What \\\"Clean\\\" Means — Subsystem Map\\n\\nAll paths relative to `/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain`. The harness is `benchmarks/agent-brain/run.py`. Tasks are `benchmarks/agent-brain/tasks/*.json` (54 files). `run_test.py` is the test suite, not the loader — the loader/validator/scorer all live in `run.py`.\\n\\n## 1. Task JSON schema — every field\\n\\nThere is **no struct/dataclass**; tasks are loaded as raw dicts (`run.py:493-509`) and fields are read defensively via `task.get(...)` throughout. The schema is therefore *implicit* in the readers. Below is the full field set, derived from a sweep of all 54 files plus the reader call-sites.\\n\\n```python\\ndef load_tasks(patterns: list[str]) -> list[dict[str, Any]]:\\n    ...\\n    with path.open() as f:\\n        task = json.load(f)\\n    task[\\\"_path\\\"] = str(path)\\n    tasks.append(task)\\n```\\n(`run.py:493-509`)\\n\\n**Field-by-field** (count = how many of 54 tasks use it):\\n\\n| Field | In N tasks | Type | Meaning / read site |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| `id` | 54 | str | Run/dir identity; brief query prefix. `run.py:1715`, `run_one` at `:3241` |\\n| `repo` | 54 | str | Display name in records (`:3248`) |\\n| `repo_path` | 54 | str | Source repo, resolved portably (`~`, `$VARS`, relative→`$AGENT_BENCH_REPO_ROOT`). `resolve_repo_path` `run.py:512-529` |\\n| `prompt` | 54 | str | The issue/task text the agent sees. Base of both `prompt_for` (`:2334`) and `brain_brief_query` (`:1713`) |\\n| `conditions` | 54 | list[str] | Arms to run. Always includes `no_brain`; e.g. `[\\\"no_brain\\\",\\\"semantic_brain\\\"]`. Combos enumerated below |\\n| `validation` | 54 | list[str] | Shell commands; all must exit 0 to pass. `validate()` `run.py:3065-3092` |\\n| `expected_files` | 54 | list[str] | Files the fix *should* touch; drives `patch_focus` (`:3103`,`:3115-3140`). Never empty in current corpus |\\n| `forbidden_files` | 54 | list[str] | Files that must NOT be touched (always `go.mod`,`go.sum`); penalize `patch_focus` (`:3104`,`:3127`) |\\n| `brain_queries` | 54 | list[str] | Retrieval hints injected into brain arms **only** (the B1 confound vector). See §2 |\\n| `setup_replacements` | 51 | list[{path,old,new}] | The regression patch: exact `old`→`new` string swap applied+committed before the agent. `apply_replacements` `run.py:839` |\\n| `hide_expected_from_agent` | 51 | bool | Withhold \\\"Expected edit area\\\" from the prompt (`:2429-2430`) |\\n| `hide_validation_from_agent` | 51 | bool | Withhold validation commands from the prompt; replaced with generic \\\"run the tests you identify\\\" (`:2431-2434`) |\\n| `leak_markers` | 24 | list[str] | High-entropy canaries that must never appear in agent output. `hidden_validation_markers` `run.py:1119-1137` |\\n| `base_commit` | 19 | str | Pinned source commit for reproducibility; recorded separately in provenance (README:88-92) |\\n| `prepare_semantic` | 17 | bool | If false, semantic indexing disabled (large-repo path); changes policy text (`:2363-2373`) |\\n| `copy_checkpoint_ref_from_source` | 16 | bool | Provides local Entire history for history/full-brain conditions (required by preflight `:4396-4401`) |\\n| `setup_commands` | 14 | list[str] | Extra shell mutations during setup (e.g. scrub README hints). Get `$BENCH_SOURCE_REPO` (README:49) |\\n| `agent_hidden_paths` | 14 | list[str] | Dirs removed from worktree before agent runs (e.g. `[\\\"benchmarks/agent-brain\\\"]`) — required on release panels (`:4377-4381`) |\\n| `suite_tags` | 13 | list[str] | SWE-style tagging metadata |\\n| `source` | 13 | str | Provenance label: `swe-bench-style-local` (7) / `swe-style` (6) |\\n| `history_excerpt_lines` | 10 | int | Cap on generated history excerpt (`write_history_excerpt` `:2012`) |\\n| `archetype` | 10 | str | Difficulty/kind metadata (see §3) |\\n| `validation_files` | 5 | list[{path,fixture}] | Hidden test files materialized from `validation-fixtures/` only at validation time. `materialize_validation_files` (`:3073`) |\\n| `radar_include_deletions` | 4 | bool | Radar arm must also flag deleted assignments; toggles `include_deletions:true` requirement (`:1389`,`:2342`) |\\n| `path_prefix` | 4 | str | `\\\"auto\\\"`→dir of host `node` (README:47-48) |\\n| `copy_entire_history_from_source` | 4 | bool | Alternative local-history source (preflight `:4397`) |\\n| `post_brain_replacements` | 2 | list[{path,old,new}] | Stale-context mutation: applied+committed AFTER brain prep, so brain context is stale (README:60-63) |\\n| `workspace_name` | 1 | str | Workspace-radar scenario name (`benchmark_workspace_name`, used `:2375`) |\\n\\nOther fields read by code but absent from current corpus: `post_brain_commands`, `history_exclude_path_terms`, `history_include_raw_snippets`, `history_excerpt` (`:2008-2037`,`:2422`).\\n\\n**Three representative tasks read in full:** `tasks/entire-brain-history-bundle-sha256.json` (full-brain, `setup_commands` scrubbing README hints, `hide_*`), `tasks/github-cli-auth-env-token.json` (semantic, `archetype:rationale-recovery`, `base_commit`-pinned), `tasks/swe-style-github-cli-auth-env-token.json` (SWE-style twin with `source`+`suite_tags`). Also `tasks/entireio-cli-radar-manual-attribution-deletions.json` (radar + `validation_files` + `leak_markers`) and `tasks/entire-brain-stale-query-default-limit.json` (`post_brain_replacements` stale-context).\\n\\n## 2. \\\"Clean\\\" vs \\\"leaky/confounded\\\" — the B1 query-hint confound\\n\\n**The leak vector is `brain_queries`.** `prompt_for` injects them as `Useful query terms: {queries}` into **every brain/MCP arm but never into `no_brain`** (`run.py:2337`, used in the policy strings at `:2367,:2371,:2376,:2380,:2385,:2393,:2415-2421`). So if a query string also contains the hidden answer, the comparison measures *(answer-hint + brain)* vs *(no hint, no brain)* — not brain value. This is exactly the B1 finding:\\n\\n> \\\"`benchmarks/agent-brain/run.py` injects `brain_queries` into brain/MCP-arm prompts only; the `no_brain` arm receives none. … several `brain_queries` contained answer-bearing identifiers, hidden-test names, or near-verbatim hidden-validation strings.\\\" (`docs/release-blockers.md:18-21`)\\n\\n**The detector: `brain_query_leak_audit` (`run.py:1240-1299`).** It compares each query against the **hidden answer texts** assembled by `brain_query_answer_texts` (`run.py:1156-1193`): the `setup_replacements`/`post_brain_replacements` `old`/`new`/`path` (always hidden), `setup_commands`, and — *only when the task hides them* — `expected_files` (if `hide_expected_from_agent`) and validation commands / `-run` test names / `rg '...'` expected strings / `validation_files` fixture contents (if `hide_validation_from_agent`). Visible validation reaches both arms, so it is deliberately not counted as asymmetry (`:1157-1161`).\\n\\nThree flag kinds (`:1261-1297`):\\n\\n```python\\nfor token in words:\\n    if brain_query_token_is_identifier(token) and token.lower() in folded_text:\\n        record(query_text, token, kind, \\\"answer_bearing_identifier\\\")\\n    elif len(token) >= 6 and token.lower() in identifier_folds:\\n        record(query_text, token, kind, \\\"answer_bearing_identifier\\\")\\n```\\n- (1) a **code-shaped identifier** token appearing case-insensitively in hidden text;\\n- (2) a 3-4 word run whose casefolded concatenation **equals** a hidden identifier (catches split/lowercased identifiers, `:1276-1282`);\\n- (3) any contiguous **3+ word phrase** found in hidden text, reported as the maximal phrase (`:1283-1297`).\\n\\n\\\"Identifier-shaped\\\" is defined narrowly so plain English symptom words pass:\\n```python\\ndef brain_query_token_is_identifier(token: str) -> bool:\\n    if len(token) < 4: return False\\n    if token.startswith(\\\"--\\\") or \\\"_\\\" in token or \\\".\\\" in token or \\\"/\\\" in token: return True\\n    return any(ch.isupper() for ch in token[1:])\\n```\\n(`run.py:1196-1204`)\\n\\n**Enforcement (hard gate):** `validate_panel` runs the audit on every task and refuses to run the panel on any finding:\\n```python\\nquery_audit = brain_query_leak_audit(task)\\nif not query_audit[\\\"ok\\\"]:\\n    for finding in query_audit[\\\"findings\\\"]:\\n        ...\\n        f\\\"task {...} brain_queries hand the brain arm answer-bearing content: {leaked!r} \\\"\\n        f\\\"appears in {finding['where']} (release blocker B1 — strip it or give both arms identical hints)\\\"\\n```\\n(`run.py:4387-4395`). On **release** panels it additionally requires `agent_hidden_paths` to hide `benchmarks/agent-brain` and requires `leak_markers` whenever validation is hidden (`run.py:4376-4386`).\\n\\nA **clean** task therefore has: `brain_queries` written at symptom level (e.g. `tasks/entireio-cli-radar-manual-attribution-deletions.json:48` — \\\"session state keeps old base commit\\\"), no identifier/phrase overlap with hidden fix/validation, hidden validation backed by an explicit `leak_markers` canary, and `benchmarks/agent-brain` hidden. A **leaky** task is one whose queries name the fixed symbol, the hidden test (`-run`), or quote the fix verbatim.\\n\\n**Disclosed residual / known limits (`docs/release-blockers.md:55-59`, `run.py:1272-1275`):** the literal \\\"any token\\\" rule from the review is intentionally *not* implemented (would false-flag symptom text); enforced rule = code-shaped identifiers + verbatim 3+-word phrases. **Two-word** runs are deliberately exempt (natural compounds like \\\"base commit\\\"), and 1-2 word plain-English overlaps are not flagged. Note `tasks/entire-brain-stale-query-default-limit.json:26` lists `brain_queries: [..., \\\"newQueryCommand\\\", \\\"newRetrieveCommand\\\", \\\"TestQMDAliasesAcrossRetrievalVerbs\\\"]` — those are identifier-shaped and the test name is in `validation` — but that task does **not** set `hide_validation_from_agent`, so the validation/test-name texts are *not* added to the hidden set (`:1172`) and the audit does not flag them. That is the auditor working as designed (visible validation is symmetric), but it means identifier-shaped queries are only safe because that task shows its validation to both arms. (I flag this as a subtle, easy-to-misread case.)\\n\\nA second, separate leak surface is **agent-output leakage**: `agent_output_leak_audit` (`run.py:1140-1153`) hashes any `leak_markers`/hidden validation marker found in agent stdout/stderr. README:159-164 explains: if `hide_validation_from_agent` and no `leak_markers`, the harness falls back to treating the validation commands themselves as canaries (strict, can false-flag an agent that independently discovers the same focused test).\\n\\n## 3. Easy/\\\"saturated\\\" vs hard/\\\"signal\\\" task\\n\\nThere is **no per-task difficulty number**; difficulty is *measured post-hoc* from the no_brain arm, with two metadata hints:\\n\\n- **`archetype`** (10 tasks): `architecture-localization` (5), `rationale-recovery` (2), `protocol-contract-recovery` (2), `validation-selection` (1). These name the *kind* of signal (e.g. localization tasks reward the brain pointing at the file; rationale-recovery rewards history recalling *why*). It is descriptive, not a scored difficulty knob.\\n- **No-brain pilot screen.** README:140-158: run one `no_brain` rep first; `--stop-after-no-brain-score 90` drops the task. \\\"Retain a scenario for proof only when no-brain is not already above 90, or when the brain condition is demonstrably cheaper/faster at equivalent correctness.\\\" So an **easy/saturated** task = no_brain already solves it (≥90 / both arms 100%); a **hard/signal** task = no_brain fails or is markedly slower.\\n\\nTwo code definitions of \\\"saturated\\\":\\n\\n```python\\nboth_solved = success_rate_condition == 1.0 and success_rate_baseline == 1.0\\nsaturated = both_solved and abs(score_delta) <= 1 and not efficiency_win\\n```\\n(`run.py:3480-3481`; verdict `saturated/no_signal` or `saturated/overhead_negative` at `:3507-3510`). A task only yields **signal** if the brain arm produces a `correctness_win` (brain passes 100% AND success/score lift, `:3473`) or `efficiency_win` (≥20% time/token improvement at equal-or-better correctness, `:3475-3478`).\\n\\nThe **stability gate** (`comparison_stability` `run.py:3582-3649`, README:21-34) is the harder bar. It can only **downgrade**:\\n```python\\nsaturated = pass_cond == 1.0 and pass_base == 1.0\\nif token_win_stable or pass_lift_stable:\\n    tag = \\\"brain_positive_stable\\\"\\nelif saturated:\\n    tag = \\\"saturated\\\"\\nelse:\\n    tag = \\\"noisy\\\"\\n```\\n`brain_positive_stable` requires a token reduction significant at p<0.05 (using the **more conservative** of raw Welch and Holm-adjusted p) *and* drop-one survival, **or** a pass-rate lift surviving drop-one (`:3592-3626`). `proof_ready` is finally gated to `candidate_proof_ready AND tag == \\\"brain_positive_stable\\\"` (`run.py:3873-3874`), needing ≥4 reps/side (`:3471`). Per `release-blockers.md:43-47`, the clean B1 reruns came out exactly here: audit-clean but **noisy** (50%→75% pass, `proof_ready=false`) or **saturated/negative** (both 100%) — i.e. clean but no proof-ready lift.\\n\\n## 4. With-brain vs without-brain expectation, success criteria, grader\\n\\n**Declaring arms:** `conditions` lists the arms; `no_brain` is the baseline every comparison is computed against. Current combos (count): `no_brain,semantic_brain` (15); `no_brain,full_brain` (14); `no_brain,semantic_brain,full_brain` (12); `no_brain,full_cli_compact,mcp_history` (7); `no_brain,mcp_history` (3); plus single multi-arm and workspace-radar tasks. The *expectation difference between arms is entirely in the policy text* `prompt_for` generates per condition (`run.py:2333-2436`): `no_brain` is told \\\"Do not use Entire Brain … inspect the repository normally\\\" (`:2364-2365`); brain arms get the `entire brain brief …` command + `Useful query terms: {queries}` + tool discipline. The task's *correct answer is identical across arms* — only the context-acquisition path differs.\\n\\n**Success criteria (primary):** every `validation` command must exit 0. `validate()` runs each, `ok = all returncode==0` (`run.py:3065-3092`). Hidden test files come from `validation_files` (`{path, fixture}` materialized from `validation-fixtures/` only during validation, then cleaned up — `:3073`,`:3091`).\\n\\n**Grader: `score()` (`run.py:3095-3227`), `score.version = 2`, 0-100, five components** (README:217-225 stresses pass/fail is primary; the score is a composite, not a pure-quality metric, and v1≠v2):\\n- `outcome` (0-45): `round(35 * validation_ratio)` + 10 if agent exit 0 & all validations pass, else +5 (`:3111-3113`).\\n- `patch_focus` (0-30): `expected_files` coverage + minimality − unexpected/`forbidden_files` touches − missing-expected penalty + diff-size bonus (`:3115-3140`).\\n- `validation_discipline` (0-10): +6 ran tests, +2 checked diff, +2 validation ok (`:3143-3149`).\\n- `runtime_efficiency` (0-10): time buckets + token buckets (`:3151-3179`).\\n- `brain_use` (0-5): for `no_brain`, 5 iff it did *not* touch the brain; for brain arms, points for actually using brain / checking freshness / brief / not-locked (`:3181-3194`).\\n\\nFor MCP conditions a **separate** `mcp_condition_audit` (`run.py:1400-1439`) enforces that the required tools were actually called (`brain_brief`/`brain_search`/`brain_status`/`brain_regressions`/`brain_workspace_regressions` per condition, `:1302-1326`) and that no direct `entire brain` CLI was used — a correct-but-bypassed run is flagged. The headline metric reported by `report`/`summarize` stays **validation pass-rate + measured tokens**, with the stability tag from §3 deciding whether a win is citable.\\n\\n**Uncertainty notes:** (a) the schema is implicit (dict + `.get`), so a typo'd field is silently ignored rather than rejected — there is no JSON-schema validator. (b) The `entire-brain-stale-query-default-limit.json` identifier-bearing queries are safe only because that task does not hide its validation; I'd treat it as a borderline case worth a human glance if its `conditions` ever add hidden validation. (c) `run_test.py` (271KB) is the test harness; I did not exhaustively read it — the loader/validator/scorer authoritative source is `run.py` as cited.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"subsystem\": \"harness-run\",\n      \"map\": \"I have all the evidence needed. Here is the deliverable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Agent-Lift Harness Map: How Runs Execute + What's Runnable Locally\\n\\n**Bottom line:** A real with-brain vs without-brain agent-lift run **cannot** be done without hosted-model access. There is **no mock/offline/dry/deterministic agent mode** anywhere in `run.py`. Every scored run shells out to the real `claude` or `codex` CLI, which authenticate against host account state. The only thing runnable *without* model credentials is the `check` subcommand (validates that tasks fail-as-expected — no agent invoked) and brain-prep (`prep`), which are deterministic/local.\\n\\nAll paths below are under `/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain/benchmarks/agent-brain/`.\\n\\n> Note on git status: the session snapshot showed these files as \\\"deleted\\\", but they are present in the working tree (`ls` confirms). I read live content.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Entry points & invocation\\n\\n**Shell drivers** (`run_cli_matrix.sh`, `run_cli_parallel.sh`, `run_opus_matrix.sh`) are thin wrappers that all converge on one command: `python3 run.py run`. They iterate a hardcoded runner matrix × 2 CLI tasks, skip suites already at ≥9 clean records (idempotent), and call e.g. (`run_cli_matrix.sh:55-64`):\\n\\n```sh\\npython3 run.py run \\\\\\n  --tasks \\\"${task}*\\\" --runners \\\"$runner\\\" \\\\\\n  --conditions \\\"$CONDS\\\" --repetitions \\\"$REPS\\\" \\\\\\n  --checkpoint-limit \\\"$CKPT\\\" --claude-budget \\\"$CBUDGET\\\" \\\\\\n  --pricing-file pricing.json --suite-name \\\"$sn\\\"\\n```\\nTheir shared config: `CONDS=\\\"no_brain,full_cli_compact,mcp_history\\\"`, `REPS=3`, `CKPT=200`, `CBUDGET=25`, `EXPECTED=9` (3 conditions × 3 reps). `run_cli_parallel.sh` adds a `maxjobs` semaphore (`run_cli_parallel.sh:69-76`); `run_opus_matrix.sh` pins `claude:opus:<effort>` only.\\n\\n**argparse** lives in `main()` at `run.py:4602`, with subcommands `run / panel / prep / report / check / discover` (`run.py:4604-4674`). The `run` subcommand args (`run.py:4606-4631`): `--tasks`, `--agents` (default `codex`), `--runners` (\\\"agent[:model[:effort]]\\\", overrides `--agents`), `--conditions` (default `no_brain,semantic_brain,full_brain`), `--repetitions`, `--timeout` (default 1800s), `--agent-retries` (default 2), `--claude-budget`, `--pricing-file/--pricing-json`, `--checkpoint-limit` (200), `--suite-name`, brain-cache flags. The committed reproducible config is the **panel** subcommand: `python3 run.py panel full` runs `panels/full.json` (`README.md:11-18`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. How an agent is actually driven (the invocation)\\n\\nReal CLI shell-outs, built in `run_agent()` (`run.py:2518`). There is **no** HTTP API call, no SDK, and no fake agent. `run_cmd` (`run.py:445-469`) is a plain `subprocess.run`.\\n\\n**Codex** (`run.py:2535-2554`):\\n```python\\ncmd = [\\\"codex\\\", \\\"exec\\\", \\\"--ephemeral\\\", \\\"--ignore-user-config\\\",\\n       \\\"--ignore-rules\\\", \\\"--sandbox\\\", \\\"workspace-write\\\", \\\"--json\\\",\\n       \\\"--cd\\\", str(worktree)]\\nif mcp_enabled: cmd.extend(codex_mcp_config_args(tools, env))\\nif runner.model: cmd.extend([\\\"--model\\\", runner.model])\\nif runner.effort: cmd.extend([\\\"--config\\\", f'model_reasoning_effort=\\\"{runner.effort}\\\"'])\\ncmd.append(prompt)\\n```\\n\\n**Claude** (`run.py:2555-2577`):\\n```python\\ncmd = [\\\"claude\\\", \\\"--print\\\", \\\"--no-session-persistence\\\", \\\"--strict-mcp-config\\\",\\n       \\\"--mcp-config\\\", claude_mcp_config(tools, env) if mcp_enabled else '{\\\"mcpServers\\\":{}}',\\n       \\\"--disable-slash-commands\\\", \\\"--permission-mode\\\", \\\"bypassPermissions\\\",\\n       \\\"--output-format\\\", \\\"stream-json\\\" if mcp_enabled else \\\"json\\\"]\\nif runner.model: cmd.extend([\\\"--model\\\", runner.model])\\nif runner.effort: cmd.extend([\\\"--effort\\\", runner.effort])\\n...\\nif claude_budget > 0: cmd[1:1] = [\\\"--max-budget-usd\\\", str(claude_budget)]\\ncmd.append(prompt)\\n```\\nThe unknown-agent branch raises (`run.py:2578-2579`): `raise ValueError(f\\\"unknown agent: {runner.agent}\\\")` — i.e. only `codex`/`claude` exist; no `local`/`mock`/`offline` fallback.\\n\\n**Searches for an offline mode came up empty.** A targeted grep for `local.?command.?agent|local_agent|mock_agent|fake_agent|--dry|dry_run|offline_mode|DETERMINISTIC|stub` over `run.py` returned **zero hits**. The string \\\"local-command-agent.py\\\" exists only as a *deleted evidence artifact* (`evidence/distill-perf/local-command-agent.py` in the git-status deletion list) and is **not referenced** by `run.py` or `run_test.py` — so it is not a runnable agent mode in this harness. (I did not inspect that file's contents; it appears to be a one-off proof fixture, not part of the run path.)\\n\\n**The one offline path:** `cmd_check` (`run.py:4563-4599`) builds tools, creates a worktree, runs `prepare_brain(..., \\\"no_brain\\\", ...)`, applies setup, and calls `validate()` — then prints `fails-as-expected` / `unexpected-pass`. **It never calls `run_agent`.** This verifies task fixtures locally with no model access.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. EXACTLY what a real run needs (external access)\\n\\n**Binaries on PATH:**\\n- `claude` and/or `codex` CLI (hard-required for any scored run; the only two agents).\\n- `go` — `build_tools` (`run.py:539-544`) runs `go build -o .../entire-brain ./cmd/entire-brain` (in repo `ROOT`) and `go build -o .../entire-sem ./cmd/entire-sem` (in the **sibling** `../entire-sem` dir, `ROOT.parent / \\\"entire-sem\\\"`). Both `check=True` — a missing `entire-sem` sibling repo breaks the build.\\n- `git` (worktree creation, `git rev-parse`, etc.).\\n- `node` — `apply_task_env` resolves `path_prefix: \\\"auto\\\"` to `dirname(which(\\\"node\\\"))` for tsx-based validations (`run.py:1521-1526`).\\n- `jq` — used by the shell drivers' idempotency check (not by `run.py`).\\n\\n**Auth / credentials (the blocker):** No `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`/`OPENAI_API_KEY` env var is read by `run.py` (grep found none). Instead auth comes from **host account state**, made explicit in the `ISOLATION` table (`run.py:39-54`):\\n```python\\n\\\"codex\\\":  {... \\\"auth\\\": \\\"host CODEX_HOME auth may still be used\\\"},\\n\\\"claude\\\": {... \\\"auth\\\": \\\"host Claude Max/OAuth auth may still be used\\\",\\n                \\\"settings\\\": \\\"default non-bare settings required for Max auth\\\"},\\n```\\nREADME corroborates (`README.md:189-192`): \\\"Authentication still comes from the host account state (`CODEX_HOME` for Codex and the configured Claude Max/OAuth state for Claude)\\\". **So you need a logged-in `claude` (Max/OAuth) and/or a `CODEX_HOME` with valid Codex auth.** Without one of these, no with-brain vs without-brain run produces a record.\\n\\n**Network:** The agent CLIs reach their hosted models (network required). The brain itself is local stdio — `mcp__entire_brain` runs as `entire-brain mcp` over stdio (`run.py:2454-2467`, isolation note \\\"entire-brain local stdio only\\\" at `run.py:2623`), and `mcp_server_env` (`run.py:2439-2452`) passes only `ENTIRE_*` plus `PATH` — so the brain side is no-egress, but the model side is not.\\n\\n**Source repos:** Tasks reference sibling repos via portable `repo_path` resolved against `$AGENT_BENCH_REPO_ROOT` (default = parent of this repo) (`README.md:36-49`). The CLI tasks need `entireio/cli` checked out as a sibling; the Ultron **session-history** scenarios additionally need machine-local `.entire` checkpoint data that is **not committed** (`README.md:51-54`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. with-brain vs without-brain setup\\n\\nConditions are sets defined at `run.py:263-275`:\\n- `no_brain` — baseline.\\n- CLI-delivery brain: `semantic_brain/semantic_cli`, `full_brain`, `full_cli_original`, `full_cli_compact`.\\n- MCP-delivery brain: `mcp_semantic`, `mcp_history`, `mcp_workspace_radar` (`MCP_CONDITIONS`, gated by `is_mcp_condition`, `run.py:306-307`).\\n\\n**no_brain** short-circuits in `prepare_brain` (`run.py:1636-1637`): `if condition == \\\"no_brain\\\": return env, prep` — no brain artifacts, no MCP, no context injection.\\n\\n**Brain conditions** build the brain via `brain_prep_commands` (`run.py:1609-1621`), which run the real `entire-brain` binary:\\n```python\\ncommands = [[tools[\\\"brain\\\"], \\\"refresh\\\", \\\"seed\\\", worktree, \\\"--agent\\\", \\\"none\\\", \\\"--force\\\"]]\\nif task.get(\\\"prepare_semantic\\\", True):\\n    commands.append([tools[\\\"brain\\\"], \\\"refresh\\\", \\\"index\\\", worktree, \\\"--sem-binary\\\", tools[\\\"entire\\\"], \\\"--force\\\"])\\nif condition_prepares_history(condition):\\n    commands.insert(0, [tools[\\\"brain\\\"], \\\"refresh\\\", \\\"sessions\\\", \\\"--checkpoint-limit\\\", str(checkpoint_limit), \\\"--history-index\\\"])\\n```\\nArtifacts are cached under `cache/` by repo/base/condition/tool hash and copied into the worktree's ignored `.benchmark/plugin/` (`run.py:1639-1700`; `README.md:65-72`). The plugin dir is injected via env in `plugin_env` (`run.py:1502-1514`): `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT`, `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_{CONFIG,DATA,STATE,CACHE}_DIR`, and `PATH` prepended with the built `bin/`.\\n\\n**Delivery split:** CLI conditions inject context into the prompt (and for `full_*`, write `.benchmark/brain-history-excerpt.md`); MCP conditions instead register the `entire_brain` stdio MCP server — Claude via `--mcp-config` JSON (`run.py:2454-2467`), Codex via `--config mcp_servers.entire_brain.*` TOML (`run.py:2474-2495`), with `enabled_tools` listing the `brain_*` toolset. The condition-specific prompt is produced by `prompt_for` (`run.py:2333`), with MCP policy text steering exact tool usage (`run.py:2376-2407`). For `no_brain`, the prompt withholds exact file/test names that are supposed to come from history (`README.md:155-158`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Suite size / model-call volume\\n\\nA run record = **1 model call** (the single `claude`/`codex` invocation; `--agent-retries 2` only re-fires on *transient* capacity/rate-limit failures, `run.py:2584-2600` + `TRANSIENT_AGENT_FAILURE_PATTERNS` `run.py:2498-2505`).\\n\\nRun count = runners × tasks × conditions × repetitions. Computed from the committed manifests:\\n- **`panels/full.json`**: 2 runners × 9 tasks × 3 conditions × 4 reps = **216 model calls**.\\n- The shell drivers: e.g. `run_cli_matrix.sh codex` = 8 runners × 2 tasks × 3 conditions × 3 reps = **144 calls**; `claude` = 12 runners × 2 × 3 × 3 = **216 calls**.\\n- The 13 committed `release-*` panels are mostly 1×1×2×4 = **8 calls** each (one is 1×2×2×4=16); summing them ≈ **120 calls**.\\n- 54 task fixtures exist under `tasks/`.\\n\\n**Wall-clock:** Per-call `--timeout` default is 1800s (30 min cap); brain-prep subprocess timeout is 900s (`run.py:1665`). Real agent calls typically run minutes each. So a full panel (216 calls) is on the order of **a few hours** wall-clock even with `run_cli_parallel.sh` concurrency, plus per-suite Go builds and one-time brain-prep (cached after first build). I cannot cite a measured total runtime from the code — that is an estimate from the timeout/call-count, not a recorded benchmark number.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Uncertainties / caveats\\n- I did not read the deleted `evidence/distill-perf/local-command-agent.py`; based on its location and zero references from `run.py`/`run_test.py`, it is a proof fixture, not a runnable agent mode in this harness — but I can't 100% rule out that some other harness used it.\\n- `run_test.py` (271KB) is the test suite, not a runner; I did not exhaustively read it, but its name and the argparse structure confirm `run.py` is the sole runner. The grep for offline-agent terms covered `run.py` only.\\n- Exact full-suite wall-clock is not stored as an evidence number anywhere I read; treat the \\\"few hours\\\" as derived, not measured.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"subsystem\": \"why-0of3\",\n      \"map\": \"I have all the evidence needed. Here is the deliverable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Why the current \\\"clean\\\" suites score 0/3 proof-ready\\n\\n## TL;DR\\nThere are exactly **three \\\"clean\\\" (post-B1) agent-lift suites in the release lane**, each at **n=4/side**, all `proof_ready=false`. One fails for **noise** (real positive direction, n=4 too small, Holm-blocked, drop-one fails); the other two fail for **saturation + negative** (both arms already 100% pass, so the only movable axis — mean score — went *down*). The release manifest is in `no_release_claim` mode and the readiness audit records the lane as having **\\\"0 proof-ready comparisons\\\"** (`docs/release_readiness_audit.md:283-284`). The \\\"0/3\\\" is structural: every retained release comparison hits a different wall of the proof-ready gate.\\n\\nThe contrast that proves the fix is in a *separate* lane (`semantic-clean-proof/`), where the same task that was `noisy` at n=4 flipped to `proof_ready=true` once n hit 16 — that is the load-bearing evidence for the synthesis below.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Enumeration of the \\\"clean\\\" agent-lift suites\\n\\nThe three retained release-lane suites (manifest minimum = 3 suites / 24 records, `benchmarks/agent-brain/evidence/release/manifest.json:7-15`):\\n\\n| # | Suite (evidence path under `benchmarks/agent-brain/evidence/release/`) | Delivery scope | Condition vs baseline | n/side |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | `release-candidate-entire-brain-schema-contract-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json` | `full_brain` (scope: `history`) | `full_brain` vs `no_brain` | 4 |\\n| 2 | `release-candidate-entire-cli-mcp-manual-attribution-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json` | `mcp` | `mcp_history` vs `no_brain` | 4 |\\n| 3 | `release-candidate-cli-radar-mcp-del-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json` | `mcp_radar_location_only` | `mcp_history` vs `no_brain` | 4 |\\n\\n(Codex agent, `codex` field, all three.) These are the only suites the manifest treats as release-citable (`release_citable_suite_globs: [\\\"release-candidate-*\\\"]`, `manifest.json:4-6`).\\n\\nFor context, two **other clean lanes exist but are intentionally NOT in the release lane** (`README.md:5-7` says they are \\\"deliberately separate\\\"):\\n- `evidence/semantic-clean-proof/` — Layer-B GitHub-CLI task + history negatives.\\n- `evidence/history-delivery-fix/` — the history net-harm fix (efficiency-at-parity).\\n\\nThese two are discussed in §3 because they are where the proof-ready gate is actually *cleared* (and where it still fails for a different reason), which is what tells you the property new release tasks need.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Per-suite measured numbers and the EXACT gate reason\\n\\n### Suite 1 — schema-contract (`full_brain`) → **NOISY**\\nFile: `release-candidate-entire-brain-schema-contract-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json`\\n\\n- pass rate: `0.5 → 0.75` (`pass_rate_baseline` L62, `pass_rate_condition` L63)\\n- mean score: `86.5 → 87.75` (`mean_baseline` L24, `mean_condition` L27); `delta: 1.25` (L19)\\n- core score: `75.0 → 79.0` (`delta_score_core: 4.0`)\\n- tokens: `568,177 → 855,568` (i.e. brain used *more* tokens, `token_improvement_ratio: -0.506`)\\n- stability block (L78-82): `\\\"tag\\\": \\\"noisy\\\"`, `\\\"saturated\\\": false`, `\\\"token_win_survives_drop_one\\\": false`, `\\\"tokens_p_raw\\\": 0.2174`, `\\\"tokens_p_holm\\\": 0.8697`, `tokens_significant_p_lt_0_05: false`\\n- `\\\"verdict\\\": \\\"brain_negative\\\"` (L88), `\\\"proof_ready\\\": false` (L64)\\n\\n**Gate reason = NOISY.** Pass-rate moved in the right direction (50→75%) and there is headroom (not saturated), but at n=4 nothing is significant: token p-raw 0.22 (and tokens went the *wrong* way anyway), no metric survives Holm, drop-one fails. Audit verdict text: *\\\"audit-clean but noisy: pass rate moved from 50% to 75%, mean score from 86.5 to 87.75, and `proof_ready=false`\\\"* (`release/README.md:55-58`; same line in `docs/release-blockers.md:45`).\\n\\n### Suite 2 — MCP manual-attribution (`mcp`) → **SATURATED + NEGATIVE**\\nFile: `release-candidate-entire-cli-mcp-manual-attribution-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json`\\n\\n- pass rate: `1.0 → 1.0` (L62-63) — both arms 4/4\\n- mean score: `94.25 → 91.25` (L24/L27); `delta: -3.0` (L19); core `84.0 → 81.0`\\n- stability block (L78-79): `\\\"saturated\\\": true`, `\\\"tag\\\": \\\"saturated\\\"`, drop-one false, tokens p-raw 0.203 / Holm 0.811\\n- `\\\"verdict\\\": \\\"brain_negative\\\"` (L88), `\\\"proof_ready\\\": false` (L64)\\n\\n**Gate reason = SATURATED (and NEGATIVE on the score axis).** Both arms already pass 100%, so pass-rate has nowhere to climb; the only remaining quality signal (mean score) dropped. Audit verdict: *\\\"it saturated: both arms passed 4/4 and the condition mean score was lower (`94.25 -> 91.25`, `proof_ready=false`). It is not citable as MCP correctness/pass-rate, named-tool MCP, or efficiency proof\\\"* (`docs/release_readiness_audit.md:546-550`).\\n\\n### Suite 3 — Radar deletion-attribution (`mcp_radar_location_only`) → **SATURATED + NEGATIVE**\\nFile: `release-candidate-cli-radar-mcp-del-clean-20260611T0412Z/summary.json`\\n\\n- pass rate: `1.0 → 1.0` (L64-65) — both arms 4/4\\n- mean score: `94.5 → 90.5` (L26/L29); `delta: -4.0` (L19); core `82.5 → 79.0`\\n- stability block (L80-81): `\\\"saturated\\\": true`, `\\\"tag\\\": \\\"saturated\\\"`, drop-one false, tokens p-raw 0.238 / Holm 0.953\\n- `\\\"verdict\\\": \\\"brain_negative\\\"` (L90), `\\\"proof_ready\\\": false` (L66)\\n\\n**Gate reason = SATURATED (and NEGATIVE on the score axis).** Identical failure mode to Suite 2. Audit verdict: *\\\"both arms passed 4/4 and the comparison was saturated/brain-negative (`proof_ready=false`). It is not citable as focused location-only deletion Radar… until a future clean task creates baseline headroom and passes the proof-ready gate\\\"* (`docs/release_readiness_audit.md:531-535`).\\n\\n### The \\\"0/3\\\" verdict in the readiness audit\\n> *\\\"the audit has 24/24 provenance-backed records, 0 hard integrity flags, 8 MCP datapoints, 4 named-tool MCP datapoints, and **0 proof-ready comparisons**. The lane therefore remains in `claim_policy: \\\"no_release_claim\\\"` mode because the clean reruns did not produce a stable positive replay-lab lift, not because of a remaining integrity confound.\\\"* — `docs/release_readiness_audit.md:282-287`\\n\\nSame count in `docs/release-blockers.md:39-41` (\\\"3 suites, 24 records… and 0 proof-ready comparisons\\\"). The manifest encodes the no-claim posture directly: `proof_ready_comparisons: 0`, `require_proof_ready_per_suite: false`, `claim_policy: \\\"no_release_claim\\\"` (`manifest.json:2,9,26`).\\n\\nNote: none of these three fail for **\\\"confounded\\\" (query-hint)** — that was the *old* B1 failure, now resolved by the `brain_query_leak_audit` and the rewrite to symptom-level queries (`release-blockers.md:22-34`). The clean reruns replaced the confounded suites; the remaining failures are statistical/headroom failures, not integrity failures.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Synthesis — what property a new task must have to flip a suite to `proof_ready`\\n\\nThe three failures map to exactly two missing properties, and the *adjacent clean lanes* show concretely what fixing each looks like.\\n\\n**Property A — A LOW-ENOUGH BASELINE TO ESCAPE SATURATION (the binding constraint for 2 of 3).**\\nSuites 2 and 3 are dead on arrival because `no_brain` already scores 4/4 / ~94 mean. When the baseline is at the ceiling, the only metric left to move is mean score, and it moved *down* — there is no possible positive quality signal. A new MCP/Radar task must be **hard enough that the no-brain arm genuinely fails a meaningful fraction** (i.e. a sub-100% baseline pass-rate), creating headroom the brain can fill. This is exactly the audit's own prescription: *\\\"until a future clean task creates baseline headroom and passes the proof-ready gate\\\"* (`release_readiness_audit.md:535`) and *\\\"It is not citable… unless a future runner/task revision creates real baseline headroom\\\"* (`:513`). The semantic-XDG and review-file-count pilots saturated for the same reason (`:289-295`, `:514-524`).\\n\\n**Property B — ENOUGH REPETITIONS (and low CV) TO SURVIVE Holm + drop-one (the binding constraint for Suite 1).**\\nSuite 1 had headroom and the right direction but lost on power: at n=4, p-raw never crossed 0.05, Holm crushed it, and drop-one failed. The `semantic-clean-proof/` lane is the existence proof that this is purely a power problem: the **same task** climbed the n-ladder and only flipped at n=16:\\n\\n- `layer-b-pilot` (n=1): `saturated` (`report.json`: pass 1.0/1.0, mean 87→86)\\n- `layer-b-proof` (n=4): `candidate_proof_ready=true` but `proof_ready=false`, tag `noisy`, tokens p-raw 0.128 / Holm 1.0\\n- `layer-b-proof-fw8` (n=8): still `noisy`, tokens p-raw 0.072 / Holm 0.30 — Holm-blocked\\n- `layer-b-confirm-fw16` (n=16): **`\\\"tag\\\": \\\"brain_positive_stable\\\"`, `\\\"proof_ready\\\": true`**, tokens p-raw 0.0124 / **Holm 0.0495**, `token_win_survives_drop_one: true` (`semantic-clean-proof/layer-b-confirm-fw16-20260611/report.json`; mirrored at `release_readiness_audit.md:247-254`)\\n\\nSo the proof-ready gate (per `release/README.md:13-17`) requires, together: provenance-complete + audit-clean + non-empty validation + **≥4 reps/side** + a **stable** proof-ready comparison. \\\"Stable\\\" means Holm-corrected significance AND drop-one survival — which at the observed CVs needs roughly **n≈16**, not 4.\\n\\n**Property C — measure on an axis that can actually move; pick the verdict axis deliberately.**\\nA subtle trap visible in both `semantic-clean-proof/` and `history-delivery-fix/`: the top-line `verdict` field is **score/success-delta driven**, so a real, stable *efficiency* win still reads `brain_negative` if score is flat (`hist-fix-redaction-n16`: `brain_positive_stable` tag on tokens −44%/Holm 4.6e-05, yet `verdict=brain_negative`, `proof_ready=False` because one rep's record was missing of 16 + negative verdict — `history-delivery-fix/README.md:121-143`). Lesson for a new task: to flip *release* `proof_ready` you want the lift on the **same axis the verdict reads** (correctness/pass-rate), which again means a failing baseline (Property A) so brain can lift pass-rate, not just tokens at parity.\\n\\n### Concrete recipe for a proof-ready release task\\n1. **Lower the baseline below the ceiling.** Design the MCP/Radar/history task so `no_brain` pass-rate sits well under 100% (real headroom) — directly fixes Suites 2 & 3's saturation.\\n2. **Size to n≈16/side, not 4.** The format-web-conflict ladder shows 4 and 8 are Holm-blocked `noisy`; 16 is where drop-one + Holm both pass — fixes Suite 1's noise.\\n3. **Keep prompts clean (symptom-level only).** Preserve the B1 fix — `brain_query_leak_audit` must pass so the win is not a re-introduced query-hint confound (`release-blockers.md:24-27`).\\n4. **Aim the lift at correctness/pass-rate**, since the `verdict`/proof gate keys off score-delta; an efficiency-only win at parity will still read `brain_negative` and stay non-proof-ready (`history-delivery-fix/README.md:104-107,134-143`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Uncertainty / caveats\\n- The three release `summary.json` files report `mean_turns_*: null` and `mean_cost_usd_*: null` for the Codex arms, and the `search_calls`/`used_brain` counters are unreliable for Claude/opus runs (parses Codex protocol-json, not Claude stream-json) — see `history-delivery-fix/README.md:94-99`. So \\\"efficiency\\\" claims rest on `total_tokens` + pass-rate only; I did not treat turn/cost/search counts as load-bearing.\\n- I read the `summary.json`/`report.json` structured fields and the README/audit prose; I did not re-derive the p-values from `records.ndjson`. The numbers quoted are the committed values in those files (line refs given). The narrative magnitudes in the READMEs (e.g. \\\"988,429 → 540,512\\\") are token totals across the 16 reps, whereas the `summary.json` figures I cite are per-run means — both are internally consistent but stated on different bases, so I labeled each.\"","numLines":20,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"005fded5-9a97-4790-b490-9e69abe2c307","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain","sessionId":"af861ed9-9e1d-41a7-8400-b53c2995aed8","version":"2.1.177","gitBranch":"claude/proof-gap-replay-distill","slug":"zippy-floating-lovelace"}
