{"parentUuid":"3ae9b60c-b44f-4e09-867b-522d91d7b23b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4a5cf49f-f389-4f1d-aee9-3dd08e56da64","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018ya5bGzeHYQxnQXU4MLSsj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"summary\": \"Author 4 clean, harder replay-lab tasks (correctness-axis gap) from distinct archetypes, each verified against real entire-brain code + history\",\n3\t  \"agentCount\": 4,\n4\t  \"logs\": [],\n5\t  \"result\": [\n6\t    {\n7\t      \"task_id\": \"entire-brain-clean-value-hook-post-failure-min-score\",\n8\t      \"task_json\": \"{\\\"id\\\":\\\"entire-brain-clean-value-hook-post-failure-min-score\\\",\\\"repo\\\":\\\"entire-brain\\\",\\\"repo_path\\\":\\\"entire-brain\\\",\\\"base_commit\\\":\\\"fc65f656615f4623a2370ec7943f8d575714cfe8\\\",\\\"conditions\\\":[\\\"no_brain\\\",\\\"full_brain\\\"],\\\"copy_checkpoint_ref_from_source\\\":true,\\\"prepare_semantic\\\":false,\\\"prompt\\\":\\\"Users report that the agent-harness post-failure hook regressed on precision: after a command fails, it now surfaces weakly-related gotcha/closed-negative facts that share only a single generic term with the failure text, instead of staying quiet unless the match is strong. The relevance bar that gates which facts qualify as a real dead end was loosened. Restore the intended relevance bar from project history so the post-failure hook is high-precision again. Do not change the scoring weights or the matching logic, only the gating bar. Run the focused hook tests before finishing.\\\",\\\"setup_replacements\\\":[{\\\"path\\\":\\\"internal/cli/hook_cmd.go\\\",\\\"old\\\":\\\"const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40\\\",\\\"new\\\":\\\"const hookPostFailureMinScore = 10\\\"}],\\\"setup_commands\\\":[\\\"perl -0pi -e 's{// hookPostFailureMinScore is the relevance bar for matching a failure against\\\\\\\\n// gotcha/closed-negative facts: 40 requires several strong term hits or a\\\\\\\\n// locus/substring-grade signal \\\\\\\\(term hit = 10, locus overlap = 60, whole-query\\\\\\\\n// substring = 200\\\\\\\\), keeping the post-failure hook high-precision\\\\\\\\.}{// hookPostFailureMinScore is the relevance bar for matching a failure against gotcha/closed-negative facts.}' internal/cli/hook_cmd.go\\\"],\\\"validation\\\":[\\\"test $(rg -n 'const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40' internal/cli/hook_cmd.go | wc -l | tr -d ' ') -eq 1\\\",\\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestHookMatchFailureKindsAndThreshold|TestHookEmitSilentAndBudget'\\\"],\\\"expected_files\\\":[\\\"internal/cli/hook_cmd.go\\\"],\\\"forbidden_files\\\":[\\\"go.mod\\\",\\\"go.sum\\\"],\\\"hide_expected_from_agent\\\":true,\\\"hide_validation_from_agent\\\":true,\\\"leak_markers\\\":[\\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestHookMatchFailureKindsAndThreshold|TestHookEmitSilentAndBudget'\\\",\\\"const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40\\\"],\\\"agent_hidden_paths\\\":[\\\"benchmarks/agent-brain\\\"],\\\"brain_queries\\\":[\\\"post-failure hook surfacing weak unrelated facts\\\",\\\"failure matcher admitting matches that are too weak\\\",\\\"failure moment precision dropped noisy facts\\\",\\\"scoring threshold for gotcha matches on failures\\\"]}\",\n9\t      \"anchor\": \"internal/cli/hook_cmd.go line 40: const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40 — the relevance-bar/min-score that gates whether a gotcha/closed-negative fact qualifies as a real \\\"dead end\\\" match for a failure in the post-failure agent hook. Decided value 40 (vs the scoring grades term-hit=10, locus-overlap=60, whole-query-substring=200). Regressed to 10.\",\n10\t      \"regression_summary\": \"setup_replacements changes `const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40` -> `= 10` at base_commit fc65f656. setup_commands runs a perl scrub that replaces the 4-line rationale comment (which itself names \\\"40\\\", the scoring grades, and the high-precision rationale) with a single neutral one-line comment, so the agent-readable file has zero answer leakage. 40 vs 10 is a plausible-but-wrong alternative (10 = the single-term-hit floor in the documented scoring model), no code-smell tell. Verified in a clean worktree: at regressed value 10 the file compiles and the focused hook tests still pass (they assert behavior via the f:neg fact at score 90 and silence on unrelated failures, both insensitive to a 10-vs-40 bar), so the test only guards the matching plumbing; the decisive gate is the rg literal `const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40` which is count 0 at the regressed state and count 1 once correctly restored. Symptom is genuine: probe showed a single-term failure makes fact f:gotcha score 20, which surfaces at bar 10 but is correctly suppressed at bar 40 — the exact precision regression described in the prompt.\",\n11\t      \"why_no_brain_fails\": \"The decisive value 40 is arbitrary within the scoring model (10/60/200 grades make 10, 20, 30, 60 all plausible bars) and a no_brain agent has no in-repo signal pinning it: the rationale comment is scrubbed, the focused go tests pass at 10/20/30/60 alike (f:neg scores 90, well above any of them; unrelated query scores 0), README/docs/seed contain no mention of the value or \\\"post-failure hook\\\" rationale (grep confirmed only generic uses of \\\"high-precision\\\" and pattern-scoring weights like 0.40), and the prompt only describes the symptom. So a no_brain agent restores SOME plausible precision bar but not exactly 40, and the hidden rg validation `... = 40` returns count 0 -> validation fails (< 100% pass).\",\n12\t      \"why_brain_passes\": \"The decision lives only in session history. The session 20260611T055732Z (where hook_cmd.go was authored) contains, verbatim in a Write tool call, the full comment \\\"...relevance bar for matching a failure against gotcha/closed-negative facts: 40 requires several strong term hits or a locus/substring-grade signal (term hit = 10, locus overlap = 60, whole-query substring = 200), keeping the post-failure hook high-precision.\\\" and `const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40`. All four symptom-level brain_queries retrieve this exact session via the built brain binary, so a full_brain agent reads the intended value 40, restores it, and both the rg literal check and the focused go test pass.\",\n13\t      \"history_evidence\": \"Brain store session sessions/main/20260611T055732Z_claude-code_fc0c6aa0...jsonl contains verbatim: \\\"relevance bar for matching a failure against\\\\n// gotcha/closed-negative facts: 40 requires several strong term hits or a\\\\n// locus/substring-grade signal (term hit = 10, locus overlap = 60, whole-query\\\\n// substring = 200), keeping the post-failure hook high-precision.\\\\nconst hookPostFailureMinScore = 40\\\". The built binary's `search \\\"failure matcher admitting matches that are too weak\\\"` and `search \\\"post-failure hook surfacing weak unrelated facts\\\"` both return [history] hits in this exact session (history:5d146712..., history:3257a174...).\",\n14\t      \"old_string_verified_at_head\": true,\n15\t      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n16\t    },\n17\t    {\n18\t      \"task_id\": \"entire-brain-clean-order-distill-cache-after-success\",\n19\t      \"task_json\": \"{\\n  \\\"id\\\": \\\"entire-brain-clean-order-distill-cache-after-success\\\",\\n  \\\"repo\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"repo_path\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"base_commit\\\": \\\"fc65f656615f4623a2370ec7943f8d575714cfe8\\\",\\n  \\\"conditions\\\": [\\\"no_brain\\\", \\\"full_brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"copy_checkpoint_ref_from_source\\\": true,\\n  \\\"prepare_semantic\\\": false,\\n  \\\"prompt\\\": \\\"Incremental distill is silently dropping work: when a session's agent chunk calls error out, that session still gets written into the on-disk distill cache as if it were finished, so the very next run treats it as up-to-date and never re-attempts it. A failed session must instead remain uncached so a later run retries it. Restore the intended ordering/behavior documented in the project history. Run the focused distill test before finishing.\\\",\\n  \\\"setup_replacements\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/distill_cmd.go\\\",\\n      \\\"old\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\tsessionFailed := false\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tfor chunkIdx, chunk := range ps.chunks {\\\",\\n      \\\"new\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\tfor chunkIdx, chunk := range ps.chunks {\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/distill_cmd.go\\\",\\n      \\\"old\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\twarnings = append(warnings, fmt.Sprintf(\\\\\\\"agent failed on %s:%d: %v\\\\\\\", session.SessionID, chunk.StartLine, runErr))\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tsessionFailed = true\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tfailedChunks++\\\",\\n      \\\"new\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\twarnings = append(warnings, fmt.Sprintf(\\\\\\\"agent failed on %s:%d: %v\\\\\\\", session.SessionID, chunk.StartLine, runErr))\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tfailedChunks++\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/distill_cmd.go\\\",\\n      \\\"old\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\t// Only cache a session as distilled when every chunk succeeded, so a\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t// session whose agent calls failed is retried on the next run rather\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t// than being silently treated as done.\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tif !sessionFailed {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tnewCache.Sessions[distillSessionCacheKey(branch, session.SessionID)] = ps.fingerprint\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\t\\\\treportProgress()\\\",\\n      \\\"new\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\t// Record the session's fingerprint so the next run skips re-distilling\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t// transcript content that has not changed.\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tnewCache.Sessions[distillSessionCacheKey(branch, session.SessionID)] = ps.fingerprint\\\\n\\\\t\\\\treportProgress()\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"setup_commands\\\": [\\n    \\\"perl -0pi -e 's{\\\\\\\\t// Second run \\\\\\\\(no --force\\\\\\\\): failed sessions must be retried, not skipped as\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\t// cached\\\\\\\\. This time the agent succeeds\\\\\\\\.}{\\\\\\\\t// Second run (no --force). This time the agent succeeds.}' internal/cli/distill_cmd_test.go\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"validation\\\": [\\n    \\\"test $(rg -n 'if !sessionFailed \\\\\\\\{' internal/cli/distill_cmd.go | wc -l | tr -d ' ') -eq 1\\\",\\n    \\\"test $(rg -n 'sessionFailed := false' internal/cli/distill_cmd.go | wc -l | tr -d ' ') -eq 1\\\",\\n    \\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestRunDistillForBrainRetriesFailedSessions'\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"expected_files\\\": [\\\"internal/cli/distill_cmd.go\\\"],\\n  \\\"forbidden_files\\\": [\\\"go.mod\\\", \\\"go.sum\\\"],\\n  \\\"leak_markers\\\": [\\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestRunDistillForBrainRetriesFailedSessions'\\\"],\\n  \\\"agent_hidden_paths\\\": [\\\"benchmarks/agent-brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"hide_expected_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"hide_validation_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"brain_queries\\\": [\\n    \\\"cache poisoned by failed agent run\\\",\\n    \\\"distill caches failed sessions\\\",\\n    \\\"session marked done after chunk failure\\\",\\n    \\\"rerun does not reprocess broken session\\\",\\n    \\\"next run should retry incomplete distill\\\"\\n  ]\\n}\",\n20\t      \"anchor\": \"internal/cli/distill_cmd.go lines 937-942 (at HEAD fc65f656): the guarded cache write `if !sessionFailed { newCache.Sessions[distillSessionCacheKey(branch, session.SessionID)] = ps.fingerprint }` preceded by the rationale comment \\\"Only cache a session as distilled when every chunk succeeded...\\\". The regression removes the per-session failure tracking (`sessionFailed := false` at line 870, `sessionFailed = true` at line 886) and makes the fingerprint cache-write unconditional, reproducing the historical \\\"third bug\\\" where a failed session is marked done.\",\n21\t      \"regression_summary\": \"Plausible-but-wrong reordering: the three edits drop the `sessionFailed` per-session failure flag entirely and move the fingerprint cache-write out of its `if !sessionFailed` guard, so every session is cached as distilled regardless of chunk-agent errors. It compiles cleanly with NO visible tell (no `if false`, no commented-out code, no contradictory warning) — it reads like a benign refactor that caches \\\"to skip unchanged content next run\\\". The bug: a session whose agent chunks all failed is written to the on-disk distill cache, so the next run treats it as up-to-date and never retries it. Verified end-to-end as the harness would apply it: all 3 replacements are unique-match at HEAD; after applying them + the setup_command scrub, `go build ./internal/cli` succeeds, both validation greps return 0, and `TestRunDistillForBrainRetriesFailedSessions` FAILS with \\\"failed sessions were cached and skipped instead of retried\\\". After `git checkout` (corrected state) both greps return 1 and the focused test passes.\",\n22\t      \"why_no_brain_fails\": \"The decisive answer is not derivable from the agent's checkout. (1) The rationale comment that explains \\\"cache only when every chunk succeeded / retried on the next run\\\" lives ONLY in internal/cli/distill_cmd.go and is OVERWRITTEN by the regression with a neutral comment, so the agent never sees it. (2) The history session containing the decision (sessions/main/...20260606..., \\\"I write each session's fingerprint into the cache before the agent runs, so all 56 sessions got cached as done even though every chunk failed\\\") is NOT a tracked repo file at base_commit — `git ls-tree -r fc65f656` returns ZERO entries under seed/ and sessions/; that data exists only in the brain's ingested store reachable via the brain channel. (3) No other repo-visible doc reveals the fix (grep across the repo excluding seed/sessions/benchmarks finds only the in-code comment). (4) Validation requires the EXACT original token `if !sessionFailed {` and `sessionFailed := false`; a no_brain agent that reverse-engineers a behavioral fix from the test name is likely to reintroduce a differently-named variable or structure (e.g. `chunkFailed`, `if anySuccess`) that may pass the test but FAILS the exact-string greps. The contract-revealing comment in the test file (\\\"must be retried, not skipped as cached\\\") is scrubbed by setup_commands, leaving only the function name as a weak hint.\",\n23\t      \"why_brain_passes\": \"The full_brain (history channel) returns the exact decision with rationale. Verified live against the built binary: `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT=... /tmp/entire-brain-proofgap search \\\"cache poisoned by failed agent run\\\"` returns as its TOP hit history:8f0e99bd1d680a2c62c87368 — \\\"Tests pass — but the failed run exposed a third bug: I write each session's fingerprint into the cache *before* the agent runs, so all 56 sessions got cached as 'done' even though every chunk failed\\\". This tells the agent the intended ordering (cache only AFTER all chunks succeed) and that a failed session must stay uncached so a later run retries it — leading to restoration of the `if !sessionFailed` guard and per-session failure flag, which satisfies both exact-string greps and the focused test. A second query \\\"distill caches failed sessions\\\" surfaces a Codex session patching distill_cmd.go around newCache, pinpointing the file/region.\",\n24\t      \"history_evidence\": \"history:8f0e99bd1d680a2c62c87368 (sessions/main/20260606T195648Z_claude-code_d770cc65-b914-4759-91e4-31397a99ba57_a83bcb1f958b.jsonl:862), top hit for query \\\"cache poisoned by failed agent run\\\": \\\"Tests pass — but the failed run exposed a **third bug**: I write each session's fingerprint into the cache *before* the agent runs, so all 56 sessions got cached as \\\\\\\"done\\\\\\\" even though every chunk [failed]...\\\" — this is the exact archetype decision (cache fingerprint only AFTER all chunks succeed; caching before poisoned 56 sessions).\",\n25\t      \"old_string_verified_at_head\": true,\n26\t      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n27\t    },\n28\t    {\n29\t      \"task_id\": \"entire-brain-clean-default-fact-merge-confidence\",\n30\t      \"task_json\": \"{\\n  \\\"id\\\": \\\"entire-brain-clean-default-fact-merge-confidence\\\",\\n  \\\"repo\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"repo_path\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"conditions\\\": [\\\"no_brain\\\", \\\"full_brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"copy_checkpoint_ref_from_source\\\": true,\\n  \\\"base_commit\\\": \\\"fc65f656615f4623a2370ec7943f8d575714cfe8\\\",\\n  \\\"prepare_semantic\\\": false,\\n  \\\"prompt\\\": \\\"The durable-facts reconcile engine has the wrong default confidence gate for auto-applying a merge/supersede. As shipped, the gate sits at a plausible-looking round value, but it is not the value the project deliberately settled on, so uncertain machine judgments are being treated as auto-apply when they should fall just short. Restore the intended default confidence gate from project history. Do not state a guessed value — recover the one that was actually decided.\\\",\\n  \\\"setup_replacements\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/facts_merge.go\\\",\\n      \\\"old\\\": \\\"\\\\tdefaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0.75\\\",\\n      \\\"new\\\": \\\"\\\\tdefaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0.7\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"setup_commands\\\": [\\n    \\\"perl -0pi -e 's/falls back to the default \\\\\\\\(0\\\\\\\\.75\\\\\\\\)/falls back to the default/g' docs/durable_facts_plan.md\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"validation\\\": [\\n    \\\"test $(rg -n 'defaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0\\\\\\\\.75' internal/cli/facts_merge.go | wc -l | tr -d ' ') -eq 1\\\",\\n    \\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestApplyFactActionsHighConfidenceMerge|TestApplyFactActionsLowConfidenceQueuesProposal|TestApplyFactActionsHighConfidenceSupersede'\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"expected_files\\\": [\\\"internal/cli/facts_merge.go\\\"],\\n  \\\"forbidden_files\\\": [\\\"go.mod\\\", \\\"go.sum\\\"],\\n  \\\"hide_expected_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"hide_validation_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"leak_markers\\\": [\\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestApplyFactActionsHighConfidenceMerge|TestApplyFactActionsLowConfidenceQueuesProposal|TestApplyFactActionsHighConfidenceSupersede'\\\"],\\n  \\\"agent_hidden_paths\\\": [\\\"benchmarks/agent-brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"brain_queries\\\": [\\\"uncertain fact merge applied automatically without review\\\", \\\"confidence needed before auto applying a merge\\\", \\\"gate that decides which fact updates apply automatically\\\", \\\"low confidence merge should not silently overwrite a fact\\\"]\\n}\",\n31\t      \"anchor\": \"internal/cli/facts_merge.go:17 — `defaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0.75`, the confidence gate at/above which a distilled merge/supersede auto-applies; below it both facts stay active and the action is queued for `facts review`.\",\n32\t      \"regression_summary\": \"Regress the decided confidence gate from 0.75 to 0.7 (a plausible round-number alternative with no visible tell). The literal 0.75 lives only at facts_merge.go:17; every test and call site references the named constant defaultFactConfidenceThreshold, so the regressed value compiles and passes the entire suite. Verified end-to-end: with 0.7 the guard tests (TestApplyFactActionsHighConfidenceMerge / LowConfidenceQueuesProposal / HighConfidenceSupersede) still pass (action confidences 0.9/0.95/0.4 stay on the correct side of 0.7), but the rg literal check yields 0; with 0.75 restored it yields 1. The only agent-readable value leak (docs/durable_facts_plan.md line 712 'falls back to the default (0.75)') is stripped by a setup_command; the remaining 0.75 hits in the repo are unrelated ($0.75 Codex pricing in agent_benchmark_plan.md). The rationale comment in facts_merge.go is retained because it explains the semantics but never reveals the value, which is exactly the arbitrary-value gap.\",\n33\t      \"why_no_brain_fails\": \"The decisive value (0.75) is not derivable from the repo the agent sees: the constant is shown regressed to 0.7, the docs value-leak is scrubbed, and the surrounding comment/flag-help describe only the semantics ('minimum agent confidence to auto-apply a merge/supersede'), not the number. A no-brain agent must guess and will reach for a common gate value (0.7, 0.8, 0.5, 0.9); 0.75 is the specific decided value it cannot reconstruct. Whatever it picks compiles and passes go test, so it gets no feedback signal — yet the hidden rg exact-value validator (== 0.75 exactly once) fails for any value other than 0.75. Pass-rate < 100%.\",\n34\t      \"why_brain_passes\": \"The full_brain history channel surfaces the verbatim decision. Symptom query 'uncertain fact merge applied automatically without review' returns history:e0da38f9de4c9b62143f13de (sessions/main/20260605T190220Z_claude-code...) at rank 3, whose Write payload contains the literal block `// defaultFactConfidenceThreshold gates automatic merge/supersede. At or above it, the action applies; below it both facts stay active and the action is queued as a proposal for `facts review`, so a low-confidence machine judgment never silently rewrites memory.  defaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0.75`. The agent reads 0.75 with its rationale directly from history, restores it, and the rg validator passes (count 1) while the guard tests confirm the fix did not break merge/supersede behavior. Pass-rate 100%.\",\n35\t      \"history_evidence\": \"history:e0da38f9de4c9b62143f13de (sessions/main/20260605T190220Z_claude-code_d770cc65-b914-4759-91e4-31397a99ba57_146bc9764dec.jsonl) — Write content includes verbatim: 'defaultFactConfidenceThreshold gates automatic merge/supersede. At or\\\\n\\\\t// above it, the action applies; below it both facts stay active and the\\\\n\\\\t// action is queued as a proposal for `facts review`, so a low-confidence\\\\n\\\\t// machine judgment never silently rewrites memory.\\\\n\\\\tdefaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0.75'. This exact string appears 48 times in the on-disk history store and is retrieved by the symptom-level search at rank 3.\",\n36\t      \"old_string_verified_at_head\": true,\n37\t      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n38\t    },\n39\t    {\n40\t      \"task_id\": \"entire-brain-clean-contract-checkpoint-branch-reachability\",\n41\t      \"task_json\": \"{\\n  \\\"id\\\": \\\"entire-brain-clean-contract-checkpoint-branch-reachability\\\",\\n  \\\"repo\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"repo_path\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"base_commit\\\": \\\"fc65f656615f4623a2370ec7943f8d575714cfe8\\\",\\n  \\\"conditions\\\": [\\\"no_brain\\\", \\\"full_brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"copy_checkpoint_ref_from_source\\\": true,\\n  \\\"prepare_semantic\\\": false,\\n  \\\"prompt\\\": \\\"Session export regression: a transcript whose checkpoint was originally recorded on a now-merged feature branch is being filed under that old feature-branch folder, even though that work is now part of the default branch line. It should be grouped with the default-branch transcripts instead, while still remembering where it originally came from. Restore the intended grouping behavior from project history. The grouping logic lives in the export selection path. Do not edit go.mod or go.sum.\\\",\\n  \\\"setup_replacements\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/export.go\\\",\\n      \\\"old\\\": \\\"\\\\tif d.DefaultBranch != \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" && len(d.DefaultCheckpointIDs) > 0 {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tif _, ok := d.DefaultCheckpointIDs[checkpointID]; ok {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\treturn []string{d.DefaultBranch}\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\tbranch := strings.TrimSpace(metadataBranch)\\\\n\\\\tif branch == \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\treturn []string{\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"}\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\treturn []string{branch}\\\",\\n      \\\"new\\\": \\\"\\\\tbranch := strings.TrimSpace(metadataBranch)\\\\n\\\\tif branch != \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\treturn []string{branch}\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\tif d.DefaultBranch != \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" && len(d.DefaultCheckpointIDs) > 0 {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tif _, ok := d.DefaultCheckpointIDs[checkpointID]; ok {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\treturn []string{d.DefaultBranch}\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\treturn []string{\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"}\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"setup_commands\\\": [\\n    \\\"perl -0pi -e 's/\\\\\\\\nfunc TestSelectedSessionOnDefaultBranchOverridesSourceBranch\\\\\\\\(t \\\\\\\\*testing\\\\\\\\.T\\\\\\\\) \\\\\\\\{.*?\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\n//s' internal/cli/export_test.go\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"validation\\\": [\\n    \\\"test $(rg -U --count-matches 'len\\\\\\\\(d\\\\\\\\.DefaultCheckpointIDs\\\\\\\\) > 0 \\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tif _, ok := d\\\\\\\\.DefaultCheckpointIDs\\\\\\\\[checkpointID\\\\\\\\]; ok \\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\treturn \\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\]string\\\\\\\\{d\\\\\\\\.DefaultBranch\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\tbranch := strings\\\\\\\\.TrimSpace\\\\\\\\(metadataBranch\\\\\\\\)' internal/cli/export.go) -eq 1\\\",\\n    \\\"test $(rg -c 'if branch != \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"' internal/cli/export.go || echo 0) -eq 0\\\",\\n    \\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestReachableCheckpointRoutesToDefaultBranchFolder'\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"validation_files\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/zz_contract_validation_test.go\\\",\\n      \\\"content\\\": \\\"package cli\\\\n\\\\nimport (\\\\n\\\\t\\\\\\\"testing\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\t\\\\\\\"time\\\\\\\"\\\\n)\\\\n\\\\nfunc TestReachableCheckpointRoutesToDefaultBranchFolder(t *testing.T) {\\\\n\\\\tselected := map[string]selectedSession{}\\\\n\\\\tdestinations := checkpointBranchDestinations{\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tDefaultBranch: \\\\\\\"main\\\\\\\",\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tDefaultCheckpointIDs: map[string]struct{}{\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\"aaa111aaa111\\\\\\\": {},\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t},\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\n\\\\taddSelectedSession(selected, selectedSession{\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tCheckpointID: \\\\\\\"aaa111aaa111\\\\\\\",\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tSessionID:    \\\\\\\"session-one\\\\\\\",\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tBranch:       \\\\\\\"feature/already-merged\\\\\\\",\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tCreatedAt:    time.Date(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC),\\\\n\\\\t}, destinations)\\\\n\\\\n\\\\tsession, ok := selected[selectedSessionKey(\\\\\\\"main\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"session-one\\\\\\\")]\\\\n\\\\tif !ok {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tt.Fatalf(\\\\\\\"reachable checkpoint was not routed to the default branch folder: %+v\\\\\\\", selected)\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\tif session.Branch != \\\\\\\"main\\\\\\\" {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tt.Fatalf(\\\\\\\"destination branch = %q, want main\\\\\\\", session.Branch)\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\tif session.SourceBranch != \\\\\\\"feature/already-merged\\\\\\\" {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tt.Fatalf(\\\\\\\"source branch = %q, want the original feature branch preserved\\\\\\\", session.SourceBranch)\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\tif _, leaked := selected[selectedSessionKey(\\\\\\\"feature/already-merged\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"session-one\\\\\\\")]; leaked {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tt.Fatalf(\\\\\\\"reachable checkpoint must not also land in its recorded feature-branch folder: %+v\\\\\\\", selected)\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n}\\\\n\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"expected_files\\\": [\\\"internal/cli/export.go\\\"],\\n  \\\"forbidden_files\\\": [\\\"go.mod\\\", \\\"go.sum\\\"],\\n  \\\"hide_expected_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"hide_validation_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"leak_markers\\\": [\\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestReachableCheckpointRoutesToDefaultBranchFolder'\\\"],\\n  \\\"agent_hidden_paths\\\": [\\\"benchmarks/agent-brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"brain_queries\\\": [\\n    \\\"exported session from a merged feature branch lands in the wrong folder\\\",\\n    \\\"which folder should hold a checkpoint that is also on the main line\\\",\\n    \\\"should the recorded branch or git reachability decide the export folder\\\",\\n    \\\"session grouping after a feature branch was merged\\\",\\n    \\\"where do merged checkpoints belong in the export\\\"\\n  ]\\n}\",\n42\t      \"anchor\": \"internal/cli/export.go — the (checkpointBranchDestinations).BranchesFor method (HEAD lines 1478-1489). It encodes the structural ownership/grouping contract: when a checkpoint ID is reachable from the default branch (present in DefaultCheckpointIDs), the exported session is filed under the default-branch folder (sessions/main) and overrides the recorded metadata branch; the recorded feature branch survives only as SourceBranch. The regression reorders the method so the recorded metadata branch wins first and reachability is only a fallback.\",\n43\t      \"regression_summary\": \"setup_replacements reorders BranchesFor in internal/cli/export.go so a non-empty recorded metadata branch returns immediately (return []string{branch}) BEFORE the DefaultCheckpointIDs reachability check, with reachability demoted to a fallback only reached when the recorded branch is empty. This is a plausible-but-wrong 'use the branch the session recorded' alternative: it compiles cleanly, has no code-smell tell, and a broad export test run still passes. Its only behavioral effect is that a checkpoint recorded on a merged feature branch but reachable from the default branch is filed under the stale feature-branch folder instead of the default-branch folder — exactly the symptom the prompt describes. setup_commands removes the upstream test TestSelectedSessionOnDefaultBranchOverridesSourceBranch from export_test.go so the agent cannot read the contract; a fresh standalone test (TestReachableCheckpointRoutesToDefaultBranchFolder) is injected only at validation time via validation_files. Verified end-to-end through the real run.py harness: on the regressed worktree the build succeeds (rc 0) and all three validation commands fail (rc 2/1/1, ok=False); after restoring the canonical upstream block all three pass (rc 0/0/0, ok=True).\",\n44\t      \"why_no_brain_fails\": \"The decisive contract — that reachability-from-default WINS over the recorded branch (and the recorded branch is kept only as SourceBranch) — is not derivable from anything the no_brain agent can read. The regressed BranchesFor is internally consistent and plausible ('prefer the recorded branch, fall back to reachability'), with no comment, dead code, or smell indicating which ordering is correct. The only upstream test that pinned the override direction is stripped from export_test.go in setup; after removal no readable test/doc/comment references DefaultCheckpointIDs, BranchesFor, the override, or 'feature/already-merged'. README/docs contain zero per-branch-grouping or routing-priority text (grep-confirmed empty). The runtime warning strings in export.go ('branch folders may be less precise') hint reachability matters but never state that it overrides the recorded branch when both apply — the genuine ambiguity. A broad `go test ./internal/cli` re-run does not flag the regression (only the one scrubbed/injected contract test does), so the agent gets no test signal either. Without the history channel the agent has a 50/50 guess on ordering and cannot reliably reconstruct the exact upstream block the rg validation requires.\",\n45\t      \"why_brain_passes\": \"All five symptom-level brain_queries retrieve the recorded decision from the history channel. The strongest hit (history:9b6a00d25315815535feda06, sessions/main/20260525...1516a9d8f651.jsonl:2172) states verbatim: 'Fixed the semantics. Now a session log lands in `sessions/main/` when its checkpoint ID appears in commits reachable from the default branch. If it originally came from a feature branch, the manifest [preserves the source].' A second hit (history:00347492f3cd...:2133) adds 'merged/default-reachable checkpoint IDs now go to `sessions/main` instead of their [feature branch folders].' These tell the brain-equipped agent the exact priority (reachability beats recorded branch; recorded branch kept as source), which maps directly to restoring the upstream ordering in BranchesFor — satisfying the rg-count guard, the absence-of-regressed-marker guard, and the injected behavioral test.\",\n46\t      \"history_evidence\": \"history:9b6a00d25315815535feda06 (sessions/main/20260525T230531Z_codex_019e5dd1-d451-7ca1-b1e4-df15cf69fcc0_1516a9d8f651.jsonl:2172): \\\"Fixed the semantics. Now a session log lands in `sessions/main/` when its checkpoint ID appears in commits reachable from the default branch. If it originally came from a feature branch, the manifest...\\\" — retrieved by the symptom query \\\"exported session from a merged feature branch lands in the wrong folder\\\". Corroborated by history:00347492f3cd8d2098ca2e5a:2133 \\\"...merged/default-reachable checkpoint IDs now go to `sessions/main` instead of their [feature branch folders].\\\" Retrieval ran against the live brain binary: ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT=/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain /tmp/entire-brain-proofgap search \\\"...\\\".\",\n47\t      \"old_string_verified_at_head\": true,\n48\t      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n49\t    }\n50\t  ]\n51\t}"}]},"uuid":"bd9d6a7e-e7c8-4c04-b276-2289d0753f72","timestamp":"2026-06-18T18:13:44.624Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-Documents-Coding-entire-brain/4e09703c-666f-441f-9b82-52efaa7da599/tasks/wmo82kh36.output","content":"{\n  \"summary\": \"Author 4 clean, harder replay-lab tasks (correctness-axis gap) from distinct archetypes, each verified against real entire-brain code + history\",\n  \"agentCount\": 4,\n  \"logs\": [],\n  \"result\": [\n    {\n      \"task_id\": \"entire-brain-clean-value-hook-post-failure-min-score\",\n      \"task_json\": \"{\\\"id\\\":\\\"entire-brain-clean-value-hook-post-failure-min-score\\\",\\\"repo\\\":\\\"entire-brain\\\",\\\"repo_path\\\":\\\"entire-brain\\\",\\\"base_commit\\\":\\\"fc65f656615f4623a2370ec7943f8d575714cfe8\\\",\\\"conditions\\\":[\\\"no_brain\\\",\\\"full_brain\\\"],\\\"copy_checkpoint_ref_from_source\\\":true,\\\"prepare_semantic\\\":false,\\\"prompt\\\":\\\"Users report that the agent-harness post-failure hook regressed on precision: after a command fails, it now surfaces weakly-related gotcha/closed-negative facts that share only a single generic term with the failure text, instead of staying quiet unless the match is strong. The relevance bar that gates which facts qualify as a real dead end was loosened. Restore the intended relevance bar from project history so the post-failure hook is high-precision again. Do not change the scoring weights or the matching logic, only the gating bar. Run the focused hook tests before finishing.\\\",\\\"setup_replacements\\\":[{\\\"path\\\":\\\"internal/cli/hook_cmd.go\\\",\\\"old\\\":\\\"const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40\\\",\\\"new\\\":\\\"const hookPostFailureMinScore = 10\\\"}],\\\"setup_commands\\\":[\\\"perl -0pi -e 's{// hookPostFailureMinScore is the relevance bar for matching a failure against\\\\\\\\n// gotcha/closed-negative facts: 40 requires several strong term hits or a\\\\\\\\n// locus/substring-grade signal \\\\\\\\(term hit = 10, locus overlap = 60, whole-query\\\\\\\\n// substring = 200\\\\\\\\), keeping the post-failure hook high-precision\\\\\\\\.}{// hookPostFailureMinScore is the relevance bar for matching a failure against gotcha/closed-negative facts.}' internal/cli/hook_cmd.go\\\"],\\\"validation\\\":[\\\"test $(rg -n 'const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40' internal/cli/hook_cmd.go | wc -l | tr -d ' ') -eq 1\\\",\\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestHookMatchFailureKindsAndThreshold|TestHookEmitSilentAndBudget'\\\"],\\\"expected_files\\\":[\\\"internal/cli/hook_cmd.go\\\"],\\\"forbidden_files\\\":[\\\"go.mod\\\",\\\"go.sum\\\"],\\\"hide_expected_from_agent\\\":true,\\\"hide_validation_from_agent\\\":true,\\\"leak_markers\\\":[\\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestHookMatchFailureKindsAndThreshold|TestHookEmitSilentAndBudget'\\\",\\\"const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40\\\"],\\\"agent_hidden_paths\\\":[\\\"benchmarks/agent-brain\\\"],\\\"brain_queries\\\":[\\\"post-failure hook surfacing weak unrelated facts\\\",\\\"failure matcher admitting matches that are too weak\\\",\\\"failure moment precision dropped noisy facts\\\",\\\"scoring threshold for gotcha matches on failures\\\"]}\",\n      \"anchor\": \"internal/cli/hook_cmd.go line 40: const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40 — the relevance-bar/min-score that gates whether a gotcha/closed-negative fact qualifies as a real \\\"dead end\\\" match for a failure in the post-failure agent hook. Decided value 40 (vs the scoring grades term-hit=10, locus-overlap=60, whole-query-substring=200). Regressed to 10.\",\n      \"regression_summary\": \"setup_replacements changes `const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40` -> `= 10` at base_commit fc65f656. setup_commands runs a perl scrub that replaces the 4-line rationale comment (which itself names \\\"40\\\", the scoring grades, and the high-precision rationale) with a single neutral one-line comment, so the agent-readable file has zero answer leakage. 40 vs 10 is a plausible-but-wrong alternative (10 = the single-term-hit floor in the documented scoring model), no code-smell tell. Verified in a clean worktree: at regressed value 10 the file compiles and the focused hook tests still pass (they assert behavior via the f:neg fact at score 90 and silence on unrelated failures, both insensitive to a 10-vs-40 bar), so the test only guards the matching plumbing; the decisive gate is the rg literal `const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40` which is count 0 at the regressed state and count 1 once correctly restored. Symptom is genuine: probe showed a single-term failure makes fact f:gotcha score 20, which surfaces at bar 10 but is correctly suppressed at bar 40 — the exact precision regression described in the prompt.\",\n      \"why_no_brain_fails\": \"The decisive value 40 is arbitrary within the scoring model (10/60/200 grades make 10, 20, 30, 60 all plausible bars) and a no_brain agent has no in-repo signal pinning it: the rationale comment is scrubbed, the focused go tests pass at 10/20/30/60 alike (f:neg scores 90, well above any of them; unrelated query scores 0), README/docs/seed contain no mention of the value or \\\"post-failure hook\\\" rationale (grep confirmed only generic uses of \\\"high-precision\\\" and pattern-scoring weights like 0.40), and the prompt only describes the symptom. So a no_brain agent restores SOME plausible precision bar but not exactly 40, and the hidden rg validation `... = 40` returns count 0 -> validation fails (< 100% pass).\",\n      \"why_brain_passes\": \"The decision lives only in session history. The session 20260611T055732Z (where hook_cmd.go was authored) contains, verbatim in a Write tool call, the full comment \\\"...relevance bar for matching a failure against gotcha/closed-negative facts: 40 requires several strong term hits or a locus/substring-grade signal (term hit = 10, locus overlap = 60, whole-query substring = 200), keeping the post-failure hook high-precision.\\\" and `const hookPostFailureMinScore = 40`. All four symptom-level brain_queries retrieve this exact session via the built brain binary, so a full_brain agent reads the intended value 40, restores it, and both the rg literal check and the focused go test pass.\",\n      \"history_evidence\": \"Brain store session sessions/main/20260611T055732Z_claude-code_fc0c6aa0...jsonl contains verbatim: \\\"relevance bar for matching a failure against\\\\n// gotcha/closed-negative facts: 40 requires several strong term hits or a\\\\n// locus/substring-grade signal (term hit = 10, locus overlap = 60, whole-query\\\\n// substring = 200), keeping the post-failure hook high-precision.\\\\nconst hookPostFailureMinScore = 40\\\". The built binary's `search \\\"failure matcher admitting matches that are too weak\\\"` and `search \\\"post-failure hook surfacing weak unrelated facts\\\"` both return [history] hits in this exact session (history:5d146712..., history:3257a174...).\",\n      \"old_string_verified_at_head\": true,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"task_id\": \"entire-brain-clean-order-distill-cache-after-success\",\n      \"task_json\": \"{\\n  \\\"id\\\": \\\"entire-brain-clean-order-distill-cache-after-success\\\",\\n  \\\"repo\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"repo_path\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"base_commit\\\": \\\"fc65f656615f4623a2370ec7943f8d575714cfe8\\\",\\n  \\\"conditions\\\": [\\\"no_brain\\\", \\\"full_brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"copy_checkpoint_ref_from_source\\\": true,\\n  \\\"prepare_semantic\\\": false,\\n  \\\"prompt\\\": \\\"Incremental distill is silently dropping work: when a session's agent chunk calls error out, that session still gets written into the on-disk distill cache as if it were finished, so the very next run treats it as up-to-date and never re-attempts it. A failed session must instead remain uncached so a later run retries it. Restore the intended ordering/behavior documented in the project history. Run the focused distill test before finishing.\\\",\\n  \\\"setup_replacements\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/distill_cmd.go\\\",\\n      \\\"old\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\tsessionFailed := false\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tfor chunkIdx, chunk := range ps.chunks {\\\",\\n      \\\"new\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\tfor chunkIdx, chunk := range ps.chunks {\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/distill_cmd.go\\\",\\n      \\\"old\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\twarnings = append(warnings, fmt.Sprintf(\\\\\\\"agent failed on %s:%d: %v\\\\\\\", session.SessionID, chunk.StartLine, runErr))\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tsessionFailed = true\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tfailedChunks++\\\",\\n      \\\"new\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\twarnings = append(warnings, fmt.Sprintf(\\\\\\\"agent failed on %s:%d: %v\\\\\\\", session.SessionID, chunk.StartLine, runErr))\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tfailedChunks++\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/distill_cmd.go\\\",\\n      \\\"old\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\t// Only cache a session as distilled when every chunk succeeded, so a\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t// session whose agent calls failed is retried on the next run rather\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t// than being silently treated as done.\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tif !sessionFailed {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tnewCache.Sessions[distillSessionCacheKey(branch, session.SessionID)] = ps.fingerprint\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\t\\\\treportProgress()\\\",\\n      \\\"new\\\": \\\"\\\\t\\\\t// Record the session's fingerprint so the next run skips re-distilling\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t// transcript content that has not changed.\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tnewCache.Sessions[distillSessionCacheKey(branch, session.SessionID)] = ps.fingerprint\\\\n\\\\t\\\\treportProgress()\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"setup_commands\\\": [\\n    \\\"perl -0pi -e 's{\\\\\\\\t// Second run \\\\\\\\(no --force\\\\\\\\): failed sessions must be retried, not skipped as\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\t// cached\\\\\\\\. This time the agent succeeds\\\\\\\\.}{\\\\\\\\t// Second run (no --force). This time the agent succeeds.}' internal/cli/distill_cmd_test.go\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"validation\\\": [\\n    \\\"test $(rg -n 'if !sessionFailed \\\\\\\\{' internal/cli/distill_cmd.go | wc -l | tr -d ' ') -eq 1\\\",\\n    \\\"test $(rg -n 'sessionFailed := false' internal/cli/distill_cmd.go | wc -l | tr -d ' ') -eq 1\\\",\\n    \\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestRunDistillForBrainRetriesFailedSessions'\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"expected_files\\\": [\\\"internal/cli/distill_cmd.go\\\"],\\n  \\\"forbidden_files\\\": [\\\"go.mod\\\", \\\"go.sum\\\"],\\n  \\\"leak_markers\\\": [\\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestRunDistillForBrainRetriesFailedSessions'\\\"],\\n  \\\"agent_hidden_paths\\\": [\\\"benchmarks/agent-brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"hide_expected_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"hide_validation_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"brain_queries\\\": [\\n    \\\"cache poisoned by failed agent run\\\",\\n    \\\"distill caches failed sessions\\\",\\n    \\\"session marked done after chunk failure\\\",\\n    \\\"rerun does not reprocess broken session\\\",\\n    \\\"next run should retry incomplete distill\\\"\\n  ]\\n}\",\n      \"anchor\": \"internal/cli/distill_cmd.go lines 937-942 (at HEAD fc65f656): the guarded cache write `if !sessionFailed { newCache.Sessions[distillSessionCacheKey(branch, session.SessionID)] = ps.fingerprint }` preceded by the rationale comment \\\"Only cache a session as distilled when every chunk succeeded...\\\". The regression removes the per-session failure tracking (`sessionFailed := false` at line 870, `sessionFailed = true` at line 886) and makes the fingerprint cache-write unconditional, reproducing the historical \\\"third bug\\\" where a failed session is marked done.\",\n      \"regression_summary\": \"Plausible-but-wrong reordering: the three edits drop the `sessionFailed` per-session failure flag entirely and move the fingerprint cache-write out of its `if !sessionFailed` guard, so every session is cached as distilled regardless of chunk-agent errors. It compiles cleanly with NO visible tell (no `if false`, no commented-out code, no contradictory warning) — it reads like a benign refactor that caches \\\"to skip unchanged content next run\\\". The bug: a session whose agent chunks all failed is written to the on-disk distill cache, so the next run treats it as up-to-date and never retries it. Verified end-to-end as the harness would apply it: all 3 replacements are unique-match at HEAD; after applying them + the setup_command scrub, `go build ./internal/cli` succeeds, both validation greps return 0, and `TestRunDistillForBrainRetriesFailedSessions` FAILS with \\\"failed sessions were cached and skipped instead of retried\\\". After `git checkout` (corrected state) both greps return 1 and the focused test passes.\",\n      \"why_no_brain_fails\": \"The decisive answer is not derivable from the agent's checkout. (1) The rationale comment that explains \\\"cache only when every chunk succeeded / retried on the next run\\\" lives ONLY in internal/cli/distill_cmd.go and is OVERWRITTEN by the regression with a neutral comment, so the agent never sees it. (2) The history session containing the decision (sessions/main/...20260606..., \\\"I write each session's fingerprint into the cache before the agent runs, so all 56 sessions got cached as done even though every chunk failed\\\") is NOT a tracked repo file at base_commit — `git ls-tree -r fc65f656` returns ZERO entries under seed/ and sessions/; that data exists only in the brain's ingested store reachable via the brain channel. (3) No other repo-visible doc reveals the fix (grep across the repo excluding seed/sessions/benchmarks finds only the in-code comment). (4) Validation requires the EXACT original token `if !sessionFailed {` and `sessionFailed := false`; a no_brain agent that reverse-engineers a behavioral fix from the test name is likely to reintroduce a differently-named variable or structure (e.g. `chunkFailed`, `if anySuccess`) that may pass the test but FAILS the exact-string greps. The contract-revealing comment in the test file (\\\"must be retried, not skipped as cached\\\") is scrubbed by setup_commands, leaving only the function name as a weak hint.\",\n      \"why_brain_passes\": \"The full_brain (history channel) returns the exact decision with rationale. Verified live against the built binary: `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT=... /tmp/entire-brain-proofgap search \\\"cache poisoned by failed agent run\\\"` returns as its TOP hit history:8f0e99bd1d680a2c62c87368 — \\\"Tests pass — but the failed run exposed a third bug: I write each session's fingerprint into the cache *before* the agent runs, so all 56 sessions got cached as 'done' even though every chunk failed\\\". This tells the agent the intended ordering (cache only AFTER all chunks succeed) and that a failed session must stay uncached so a later run retries it — leading to restoration of the `if !sessionFailed` guard and per-session failure flag, which satisfies both exact-string greps and the focused test. A second query \\\"distill caches failed sessions\\\" surfaces a Codex session patching distill_cmd.go around newCache, pinpointing the file/region.\",\n      \"history_evidence\": \"history:8f0e99bd1d680a2c62c87368 (sessions/main/20260606T195648Z_claude-code_d770cc65-b914-4759-91e4-31397a99ba57_a83bcb1f958b.jsonl:862), top hit for query \\\"cache poisoned by failed agent run\\\": \\\"Tests pass — but the failed run exposed a **third bug**: I write each session's fingerprint into the cache *before* the agent runs, so all 56 sessions got cached as \\\\\\\"done\\\\\\\" even though every chunk [failed]...\\\" — this is the exact archetype decision (cache fingerprint only AFTER all chunks succeed; caching before poisoned 56 sessions).\",\n      \"old_string_verified_at_head\": true,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"task_id\": \"entire-brain-clean-default-fact-merge-confidence\",\n      \"task_json\": \"{\\n  \\\"id\\\": \\\"entire-brain-clean-default-fact-merge-confidence\\\",\\n  \\\"repo\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"repo_path\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"conditions\\\": [\\\"no_brain\\\", \\\"full_brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"copy_checkpoint_ref_from_source\\\": true,\\n  \\\"base_commit\\\": \\\"fc65f656615f4623a2370ec7943f8d575714cfe8\\\",\\n  \\\"prepare_semantic\\\": false,\\n  \\\"prompt\\\": \\\"The durable-facts reconcile engine has the wrong default confidence gate for auto-applying a merge/supersede. As shipped, the gate sits at a plausible-looking round value, but it is not the value the project deliberately settled on, so uncertain machine judgments are being treated as auto-apply when they should fall just short. Restore the intended default confidence gate from project history. Do not state a guessed value — recover the one that was actually decided.\\\",\\n  \\\"setup_replacements\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/facts_merge.go\\\",\\n      \\\"old\\\": \\\"\\\\tdefaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0.75\\\",\\n      \\\"new\\\": \\\"\\\\tdefaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0.7\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"setup_commands\\\": [\\n    \\\"perl -0pi -e 's/falls back to the default \\\\\\\\(0\\\\\\\\.75\\\\\\\\)/falls back to the default/g' docs/durable_facts_plan.md\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"validation\\\": [\\n    \\\"test $(rg -n 'defaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0\\\\\\\\.75' internal/cli/facts_merge.go | wc -l | tr -d ' ') -eq 1\\\",\\n    \\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestApplyFactActionsHighConfidenceMerge|TestApplyFactActionsLowConfidenceQueuesProposal|TestApplyFactActionsHighConfidenceSupersede'\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"expected_files\\\": [\\\"internal/cli/facts_merge.go\\\"],\\n  \\\"forbidden_files\\\": [\\\"go.mod\\\", \\\"go.sum\\\"],\\n  \\\"hide_expected_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"hide_validation_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"leak_markers\\\": [\\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestApplyFactActionsHighConfidenceMerge|TestApplyFactActionsLowConfidenceQueuesProposal|TestApplyFactActionsHighConfidenceSupersede'\\\"],\\n  \\\"agent_hidden_paths\\\": [\\\"benchmarks/agent-brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"brain_queries\\\": [\\\"uncertain fact merge applied automatically without review\\\", \\\"confidence needed before auto applying a merge\\\", \\\"gate that decides which fact updates apply automatically\\\", \\\"low confidence merge should not silently overwrite a fact\\\"]\\n}\",\n      \"anchor\": \"internal/cli/facts_merge.go:17 — `defaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0.75`, the confidence gate at/above which a distilled merge/supersede auto-applies; below it both facts stay active and the action is queued for `facts review`.\",\n      \"regression_summary\": \"Regress the decided confidence gate from 0.75 to 0.7 (a plausible round-number alternative with no visible tell). The literal 0.75 lives only at facts_merge.go:17; every test and call site references the named constant defaultFactConfidenceThreshold, so the regressed value compiles and passes the entire suite. Verified end-to-end: with 0.7 the guard tests (TestApplyFactActionsHighConfidenceMerge / LowConfidenceQueuesProposal / HighConfidenceSupersede) still pass (action confidences 0.9/0.95/0.4 stay on the correct side of 0.7), but the rg literal check yields 0; with 0.75 restored it yields 1. The only agent-readable value leak (docs/durable_facts_plan.md line 712 'falls back to the default (0.75)') is stripped by a setup_command; the remaining 0.75 hits in the repo are unrelated ($0.75 Codex pricing in agent_benchmark_plan.md). The rationale comment in facts_merge.go is retained because it explains the semantics but never reveals the value, which is exactly the arbitrary-value gap.\",\n      \"why_no_brain_fails\": \"The decisive value (0.75) is not derivable from the repo the agent sees: the constant is shown regressed to 0.7, the docs value-leak is scrubbed, and the surrounding comment/flag-help describe only the semantics ('minimum agent confidence to auto-apply a merge/supersede'), not the number. A no-brain agent must guess and will reach for a common gate value (0.7, 0.8, 0.5, 0.9); 0.75 is the specific decided value it cannot reconstruct. Whatever it picks compiles and passes go test, so it gets no feedback signal — yet the hidden rg exact-value validator (== 0.75 exactly once) fails for any value other than 0.75. Pass-rate < 100%.\",\n      \"why_brain_passes\": \"The full_brain history channel surfaces the verbatim decision. Symptom query 'uncertain fact merge applied automatically without review' returns history:e0da38f9de4c9b62143f13de (sessions/main/20260605T190220Z_claude-code...) at rank 3, whose Write payload contains the literal block `// defaultFactConfidenceThreshold gates automatic merge/supersede. At or above it, the action applies; below it both facts stay active and the action is queued as a proposal for `facts review`, so a low-confidence machine judgment never silently rewrites memory.  defaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0.75`. The agent reads 0.75 with its rationale directly from history, restores it, and the rg validator passes (count 1) while the guard tests confirm the fix did not break merge/supersede behavior. Pass-rate 100%.\",\n      \"history_evidence\": \"history:e0da38f9de4c9b62143f13de (sessions/main/20260605T190220Z_claude-code_d770cc65-b914-4759-91e4-31397a99ba57_146bc9764dec.jsonl) — Write content includes verbatim: 'defaultFactConfidenceThreshold gates automatic merge/supersede. At or\\\\n\\\\t// above it, the action applies; below it both facts stay active and the\\\\n\\\\t// action is queued as a proposal for `facts review`, so a low-confidence\\\\n\\\\t// machine judgment never silently rewrites memory.\\\\n\\\\tdefaultFactConfidenceThreshold = 0.75'. This exact string appears 48 times in the on-disk history store and is retrieved by the symptom-level search at rank 3.\",\n      \"old_string_verified_at_head\": true,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"task_id\": \"entire-brain-clean-contract-checkpoint-branch-reachability\",\n      \"task_json\": \"{\\n  \\\"id\\\": \\\"entire-brain-clean-contract-checkpoint-branch-reachability\\\",\\n  \\\"repo\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"repo_path\\\": \\\"entire-brain\\\",\\n  \\\"base_commit\\\": \\\"fc65f656615f4623a2370ec7943f8d575714cfe8\\\",\\n  \\\"conditions\\\": [\\\"no_brain\\\", \\\"full_brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"copy_checkpoint_ref_from_source\\\": true,\\n  \\\"prepare_semantic\\\": false,\\n  \\\"prompt\\\": \\\"Session export regression: a transcript whose checkpoint was originally recorded on a now-merged feature branch is being filed under that old feature-branch folder, even though that work is now part of the default branch line. It should be grouped with the default-branch transcripts instead, while still remembering where it originally came from. Restore the intended grouping behavior from project history. The grouping logic lives in the export selection path. Do not edit go.mod or go.sum.\\\",\\n  \\\"setup_replacements\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/export.go\\\",\\n      \\\"old\\\": \\\"\\\\tif d.DefaultBranch != \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" && len(d.DefaultCheckpointIDs) > 0 {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tif _, ok := d.DefaultCheckpointIDs[checkpointID]; ok {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\treturn []string{d.DefaultBranch}\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\tbranch := strings.TrimSpace(metadataBranch)\\\\n\\\\tif branch == \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\treturn []string{\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"}\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\treturn []string{branch}\\\",\\n      \\\"new\\\": \\\"\\\\tbranch := strings.TrimSpace(metadataBranch)\\\\n\\\\tif branch != \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\treturn []string{branch}\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\tif d.DefaultBranch != \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" && len(d.DefaultCheckpointIDs) > 0 {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tif _, ok := d.DefaultCheckpointIDs[checkpointID]; ok {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\treturn []string{d.DefaultBranch}\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\treturn []string{\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"}\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"setup_commands\\\": [\\n    \\\"perl -0pi -e 's/\\\\\\\\nfunc TestSelectedSessionOnDefaultBranchOverridesSourceBranch\\\\\\\\(t \\\\\\\\*testing\\\\\\\\.T\\\\\\\\) \\\\\\\\{.*?\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\n//s' internal/cli/export_test.go\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"validation\\\": [\\n    \\\"test $(rg -U --count-matches 'len\\\\\\\\(d\\\\\\\\.DefaultCheckpointIDs\\\\\\\\) > 0 \\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\tif _, ok := d\\\\\\\\.DefaultCheckpointIDs\\\\\\\\[checkpointID\\\\\\\\]; ok \\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\treturn \\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\]string\\\\\\\\{d\\\\\\\\.DefaultBranch\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\t\\\\\\\\}\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\tbranch := strings\\\\\\\\.TrimSpace\\\\\\\\(metadataBranch\\\\\\\\)' internal/cli/export.go) -eq 1\\\",\\n    \\\"test $(rg -c 'if branch != \\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"' internal/cli/export.go || echo 0) -eq 0\\\",\\n    \\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestReachableCheckpointRoutesToDefaultBranchFolder'\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"validation_files\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"path\\\": \\\"internal/cli/zz_contract_validation_test.go\\\",\\n      \\\"content\\\": \\\"package cli\\\\n\\\\nimport (\\\\n\\\\t\\\\\\\"testing\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\t\\\\\\\"time\\\\\\\"\\\\n)\\\\n\\\\nfunc TestReachableCheckpointRoutesToDefaultBranchFolder(t *testing.T) {\\\\n\\\\tselected := map[string]selectedSession{}\\\\n\\\\tdestinations := checkpointBranchDestinations{\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tDefaultBranch: \\\\\\\"main\\\\\\\",\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tDefaultCheckpointIDs: map[string]struct{}{\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\"aaa111aaa111\\\\\\\": {},\\\\n\\\\t\\\\t},\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\n\\\\taddSelectedSession(selected, selectedSession{\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tCheckpointID: \\\\\\\"aaa111aaa111\\\\\\\",\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tSessionID:    \\\\\\\"session-one\\\\\\\",\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tBranch:       \\\\\\\"feature/already-merged\\\\\\\",\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tCreatedAt:    time.Date(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC),\\\\n\\\\t}, destinations)\\\\n\\\\n\\\\tsession, ok := selected[selectedSessionKey(\\\\\\\"main\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"session-one\\\\\\\")]\\\\n\\\\tif !ok {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tt.Fatalf(\\\\\\\"reachable checkpoint was not routed to the default branch folder: %+v\\\\\\\", selected)\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\tif session.Branch != \\\\\\\"main\\\\\\\" {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tt.Fatalf(\\\\\\\"destination branch = %q, want main\\\\\\\", session.Branch)\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\tif session.SourceBranch != \\\\\\\"feature/already-merged\\\\\\\" {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tt.Fatalf(\\\\\\\"source branch = %q, want the original feature branch preserved\\\\\\\", session.SourceBranch)\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n\\\\tif _, leaked := selected[selectedSessionKey(\\\\\\\"feature/already-merged\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"session-one\\\\\\\")]; leaked {\\\\n\\\\t\\\\tt.Fatalf(\\\\\\\"reachable checkpoint must not also land in its recorded feature-branch folder: %+v\\\\\\\", selected)\\\\n\\\\t}\\\\n}\\\\n\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"expected_files\\\": [\\\"internal/cli/export.go\\\"],\\n  \\\"forbidden_files\\\": [\\\"go.mod\\\", \\\"go.sum\\\"],\\n  \\\"hide_expected_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"hide_validation_from_agent\\\": true,\\n  \\\"leak_markers\\\": [\\\"go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestReachableCheckpointRoutesToDefaultBranchFolder'\\\"],\\n  \\\"agent_hidden_paths\\\": [\\\"benchmarks/agent-brain\\\"],\\n  \\\"brain_queries\\\": [\\n    \\\"exported session from a merged feature branch lands in the wrong folder\\\",\\n    \\\"which folder should hold a checkpoint that is also on the main line\\\",\\n    \\\"should the recorded branch or git reachability decide the export folder\\\",\\n    \\\"session grouping after a feature branch was merged\\\",\\n    \\\"where do merged checkpoints belong in the export\\\"\\n  ]\\n}\",\n      \"anchor\": \"internal/cli/export.go — the (checkpointBranchDestinations).BranchesFor method (HEAD lines 1478-1489). It encodes the structural ownership/grouping contract: when a checkpoint ID is reachable from the default branch (present in DefaultCheckpointIDs), the exported session is filed under the default-branch folder (sessions/main) and overrides the recorded metadata branch; the recorded feature branch survives only as SourceBranch. The regression reorders the method so the recorded metadata branch wins first and reachability is only a fallback.\",\n      \"regression_summary\": \"setup_replacements reorders BranchesFor in internal/cli/export.go so a non-empty recorded metadata branch returns immediately (return []string{branch}) BEFORE the DefaultCheckpointIDs reachability check, with reachability demoted to a fallback only reached when the recorded branch is empty. This is a plausible-but-wrong 'use the branch the session recorded' alternative: it compiles cleanly, has no code-smell tell, and a broad export test run still passes. Its only behavioral effect is that a checkpoint recorded on a merged feature branch but reachable from the default branch is filed under the stale feature-branch folder instead of the default-branch folder — exactly the symptom the prompt describes. setup_commands removes the upstream test TestSelectedSessionOnDefaultBranchOverridesSourceBranch from export_test.go so the agent cannot read the contract; a fresh standalone test (TestReachableCheckpointRoutesToDefaultBranchFolder) is injected only at validation time via validation_files. Verified end-to-end through the real run.py harness: on the regressed worktree the build succeeds (rc 0) and all three validation commands fail (rc 2/1/1, ok=False); after restoring the canonical upstream block all three pass (rc 0/0/0, ok=True).\",\n      \"why_no_brain_fails\": \"The decisive contract — that reachability-from-default WINS over the recorded branch (and the recorded branch is kept only as SourceBranch) — is not derivable from anything the no_brain agent can read. The regressed BranchesFor is internally consistent and plausible ('prefer the recorded branch, fall back to reachability'), with no comment, dead code, or smell indicating which ordering is correct. The only upstream test that pinned the override direction is stripped from export_test.go in setup; after removal no readable test/doc/comment references DefaultCheckpointIDs, BranchesFor, the override, or 'feature/already-merged'. README/docs contain zero per-branch-grouping or routing-priority text (grep-confirmed empty). The runtime warning strings in export.go ('branch folders may be less precise') hint reachability matters but never state that it overrides the recorded branch when both apply — the genuine ambiguity. A broad `go test ./internal/cli` re-run does not flag the regression (only the one scrubbed/injected contract test does), so the agent gets no test signal either. Without the history channel the agent has a 50/50 guess on ordering and cannot reliably reconstruct the exact upstream block the rg validation requires.\",\n      \"why_brain_passes\": \"All five symptom-level brain_queries retrieve the recorded decision from the history channel. The strongest hit (history:9b6a00d25315815535feda06, sessions/main/20260525...1516a9d8f651.jsonl:2172) states verbatim: 'Fixed the semantics. Now a session log lands in `sessions/main/` when its checkpoint ID appears in commits reachable from the default branch. If it originally came from a feature branch, the manifest [preserves the source].' A second hit (history:00347492f3cd...:2133) adds 'merged/default-reachable checkpoint IDs now go to `sessions/main` instead of their [feature branch folders].' These tell the brain-equipped agent the exact priority (reachability beats recorded branch; recorded branch kept as source), which maps directly to restoring the upstream ordering in BranchesFor — satisfying the rg-count guard, the absence-of-regressed-marker guard, and the injected behavioral test.\",\n      \"history_evidence\": \"history:9b6a00d25315815535feda06 (sessions/main/20260525T230531Z_codex_019e5dd1-d451-7ca1-b1e4-df15cf69fcc0_1516a9d8f651.jsonl:2172): \\\"Fixed the semantics. Now a session log lands in `sessions/main/` when its checkpoint ID appears in commits reachable from the default branch. If it originally came from a feature branch, the manifest...\\\" — retrieved by the symptom query \\\"exported session from a merged feature branch lands in the wrong folder\\\". Corroborated by history:00347492f3cd8d2098ca2e5a:2133 \\\"...merged/default-reachable checkpoint IDs now go to `sessions/main` instead of their [feature branch folders].\\\" Retrieval ran against the live brain binary: ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT=/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain /tmp/entire-brain-proofgap search \\\"...\\\".\",\n      \"old_string_verified_at_head\": true,\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","numLines":51,"startLine":1,"totalLines":51}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3ae9b60c-b44f-4e09-867b-522d91d7b23b","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/Documents/Coding/entire-brain","sessionId":"af861ed9-9e1d-41a7-8400-b53c2995aed8","version":"2.1.177","gitBranch":"claude/proof-gap-replay-distill","slug":"zippy-floating-lovelace"}
