{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-06-18T21:47:25.039Z","sessionId":"af861ed9-9e1d-41a7-8400-b53c2995aed8","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>wl34w6020</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>REDACTED</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-Documents-Coding-entire-brain/4e09703c-666f-441f-9b82-52efaa7da599/tasks/wl34w6020.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Dynamic workflow \"Adversarial review of the registered proof lane + matrix track + docs honesty; verify no overclaim, no leak, no broken wiring\" completed</summary>\n<result>{\"confirmed\":[{\"title\":\"README 'verdict' column shows 'saturated' for the contract-checkpoint row, but the machine verdict field is actually 'brain_negative' (saturated is the stability tag)\",\"severity\":\"nit\",\"location\":\"benchmarks/agent-brain/evidence/replay-lab-clean/README.md:16\",\"detail\":\"The README table column is headed 'verdict' and lists 'saturated' for entire-brain-clean-contract-checkpoint-branch-reachability. In the source data the comparison's verdict field is 'brain_negative' (delta -3.25: full_brain scored 82-83 vs no_brain 85-86); 'saturated' is the stability.tag (panel summary.json stability.tag='saturated', saturated=true). This is not an overclaim — proof_ready=false and it is honestly retained as a non-proof-ready datapoint, and 'saturated (both arms 4/4)' is an accurate human-readable description since both arms pass on pass-rate. But putting 'saturated' under a column literally labeled 'verdict' is a slight imprecision: the literal verdict is brain_negative. Harmless to the release claim (this row is explicitly not claimed) but a reviewer comparing the table to the JSON will see a mismatch.\",\"suggested_fix\":\"Either rename the README table column header from 'verdict' to 'stability' / 'outcome', or change the cell to 'brain_negative (saturated)' to match both the machine verdict and the stability tag. Purely cosmetic; no claim is affected.\",\"verdict\":{\"title\":\"README \\\"verdict\\\" column shows \\\"saturated\\\" (the stability tag) for the contract-checkpoint row whose machine verdict is actually \\\"brain_negative\\\"\",\"is_real\":true,\"severity\":\"nit\",\"reason\":\"Verified against source. In benchmarks/agent-brain/evidence/replay-lab-clean/README.md the table column is headed `verdict` (line 13). Row 2, line 16, for `entire-brain-clean-contract-checkpoint-branch-reachability`, places `saturated` in that `verdict` column. But in the source summary.json the comparison's `verdict` field is `\\\"brain_negative\\\"` (panel-p01-clean-proof-claude-20260618T195058Z/summary.json:90), with `verdict_reasons` = both_conditions_passed + score_drop_or_overhead_exceeds_gate (lines 91-94), `delta`: -3.25 (full_brain mean 82.5 vs no_brain 85.75, lines 19/26/27). `saturated` is the stability tag, not the verdict: `stability.tag: \\\"saturated\\\"`, `saturated: true` (lines 80-81). The README is internally inconsistent: row 1 (line 15) correctly puts the machine verdict in this column (`brain_positive (stable)` = verdict `brain_positive` + tag `brain_positive_stable`), while row 2 substitutes the stability tag and omits the actual `brain_negative` verdict. A reviewer cross-checking the table against the JSON `verdict` field will see a mismatch on row 2 only. This is a REAL defect but minor: it is a human-authored doc table (the README is NOT machine-generated — `clean-proof:evidence:update` in mise.toml:90-107 regenerates only codex-audit-report.{json,md}), and no release gate parses the README (audit_release_matrix.py and audit_codex.py have no README/verdict/saturated references; the gate reads summary.json/manifest/records). The release claim is unaffected: the proof row (fact-merge 0/4 vs 4/4, brain_positive, proof_ready=true) is accurate, and that is the only thing claimed; this row is explicitly proof_ready=false / \\\"retained datapoint\\\" and the prose (lines 47-48) honestly describes it as saturated. Through the docs-honesty lens it is a slight inaccuracy worth a one-line cosmetic fix; through the release/proof lens it is harmless. FIX (per the original reviewer, correct): either rename the README column header from `verdict` to `stability`/`outcome`, or change the row-2 cell to `brain_negative (saturated)` so it matches both the machine verdict and the stability tag. Severity nit: cosmetic, no gate or proof affected.\"}},{\"title\":\"Eval-ledger header says 'one row per retrieval-quality measurement' but the new row is an end-to-end agent pass-rate measurement\",\"severity\":\"nit\",\"location\":\"docs/eval_ledger.md:3-4 (header) vs line 25 (new row)\",\"detail\":\"The ledger preamble (lines 3-8) frames the table as 'One row per retrieval-quality measurement that gated a decision.' The new 2026-06-18 row is honestly labeled in its own Comparison column as 'end-to-end agent validation pass-rate' (0/4 -&gt; 4/4), which is an agent-outcome measurement rather than a retrieval-quality (useful/1k, precision) measurement like every prior row. The row content itself is honest and the within-baseline discipline is respected; this is only a mild category drift between the ledger's stated charter and the kind of evidence in the new row. It does not cause an overclaim — the row clearly states what it measures — but the table preamble no longer perfectly describes its contents.\",\"suggested_fix\":\"Optionally broaden the ledger preamble (e.g. 'one row per retrieval-quality or agent-outcome measurement that gated a decision'), or add a one-clause note on the new row that it is an agent-lift/pass-rate datapoint rather than a retrieval-quality datapoint. Cosmetic alignment only.\",\"verdict\":{\"title\":\"Eval-ledger preamble says \\\"retrieval-quality measurement\\\" but new row is an agent pass-rate datapoint\",\"is_real\":true,\"severity\":\"nit\",\"reason\":\"Verified against the files. docs/eval_ledger.md:3 header reads \\\"One row per retrieval-quality measurement that gated a decision,\\\" and every prior row (lines 11-24) reports retrieval-quality metrics (useful/1k, precision, recall). The new 2026-06-18 row at docs/eval_ledger.md:25 is instead an \\\"end-to-end agent validation pass-rate\\\" (no_brain 0/4 -&gt; full_brain 4/4), an agent-outcome metric, not a retrieval-quality one. So the reviewer's factual premise is accurate: the preamble's \\\"retrieval-quality\\\" umbrella no longer perfectly covers all rows.\\n\\nHowever this is NOT a defect that must be fixed; it is cosmetic only, so I classify it as a nit:\\n- No overclaim. The row is self-honest: its own Comparison column explicitly says \\\"end-to-end agent validation pass-rate\\\" and the Headline shows 0/4 -&gt; 4/4. A reader cannot mistake it for a useful/1k retrieval number.\\n- The load-bearing honesty guardrail in the preamble (\\\"numbers are comparable only within a row's own baseline, never across rows\\\") still fully applies to the new row, and the row respects it (names its co-located saturated comparison, lists which scopes stay no-claim). \\n- \\\"retrieval-quality\\\" is a charter/scope label, not a correctness claim; the within-row labeling is unambiguous. The reviewer themselves calls it \\\"cosmetic alignment only\\\" and \\\"mild category drift ... does not cause an overclaim.\\\"\\n\\nFix (optional polish, not required to merge): broaden docs/eval_ledger.md:3 to e.g. \\\"One row per retrieval-quality or agent-outcome measurement that gated a decision,\\\" and optionally mirror the same broadening in the \\\"Adding a row\\\" note at docs/eval_ledger.md:54-56 (which currently says \\\"When a measurement gates a ship/park decision\\\" — already generic enough, so the only true mismatch is the line-3 word \\\"retrieval-quality\\\").\"}}],\"all\":[{\"title\":\"Proof is real, fair, and auditor-clean — reproduced 0 hard flags / proof_ready=1 independently\",\"severity\":\"nit\",\"location\":\"benchmarks/agent-brain/evidence/replay-lab-clean/m